ns.ZoneLore = {
[-3065]={articles={"Hall of Thanes (instance)","The Restless Dead (Forever)","Important Heirlooms"},text="Deep beneath the High Seat of Ironforge, below the long-sealed chambers of Old Ironforge, lies the Hall of Thanes. Here the dwarves lay their honoured dead in tombs watched over by the hall's keepers, and its lower vaults hold heirlooms and relics from the long history of the Bronzebeard clan.",chapters={{after=96393,articles={"Old Ironforge Incursion","Underground Map","Durgen Dirgehammer"},text="The Dark Iron map that Earthseer Farsen read in Dun Morogh was no idle plan. The Dark Irons had broken through into the Hall of Thanes, coming up out of the depths in mole machines, and their commander, Durgen Dirgehammer, led the push into the lower vaults. His head is in King Magni Bronzebeard's hands now; the guards are clearing out the rest, and the breaches are being sealed so that no such incursion can happen again."},{after=96395,articles={"An Ancient Grudge","The Restless Dead (Forever)"},text="The Dark Irons' coming woke the dead of the hall, and the keepers would no longer go down among angry ghosts. A faithful attendant's spirit, barely able to speak, begged for help for its thane, Faldrim Anvilmar, who could find no rest, consumed by an ancient grudge. Faldrim has been laid to rest at last, and his attendant with him."},{after=98423,articles={"The Treaty of Understanding"},text="Among the vault's treasures was a tablet recording the peace that ended the War of the Three Hammers, when the Bronzebeards won the mountain. King Magni recognised it as the work of his ancestor Madoran, the first Bronzebeard king of Ironforge after that war. The Bronzebeards and the Wildhammers signed it; the Dark Irons never did."}}},
[-3039]={articles={"Krol'dok Stronghold","Riverglades"},text="Krol'dok Stronghold is an ogre fortress in the mountains of the Riverglades, north of Sunnyglade, hidden in a network of caves. It was here that the Horde's broken army fled after the Siege of Farholde, and today it is a stronghold of the Twilight's Hammer, its ogres led by Overlord Mugg.",chapters={{after=94982,articles={"Eye of Mugg (Alliance)"},text="Brotherhood scouts found Twilight's Hammer cultists moving in and out of hidden caves along the northwestern mountains, often by dark, and most who went in to learn more never came back. Commander Brant judged that only a deathly blow to their leader would let the Brotherhood keep driving them from its lands. Overlord Mugg is dead, his eye proof of it."},{after=94968,articles={"Eye of Mugg (Horde)"},text="The Horde's scouts found the Twilight's Hammer foothold at Krol'dok far larger than anyone had feared, and the cultists were making sacrifices of the Horde's allies. Krom'rosh of the Horde would not stand by: he sent fighters to slaughter the stronghold's forces and bring back proof that its leader was dead. Overlord Mugg's eye is that proof, and a message to the Hammer."},{after=94997,articles={"Lingering Essence"},text="Sergeant Riftan has battled the Twilight's Hammer for years, and the mementos his men took from the cultists unsettled him enough to send them to Farholde Keep at once. Ashe Amberhall, the keep's mage, worried that fatigue was wearing on the old soldier's mind, but on looking at the satchel she admitted his fears might be founded."}}},
[-2999]={articles={"Ruins of Lordaeron (instance)","Light's Justice (quest)"},text="Above the Undercity lie the ruins of Lordaeron's capital, razed when Prince Arthas came home and gave the kingdom to the Scourge. The Forsaken rule the city below, but on the streets above, through King's Alley to the old graveyard by the Lordamere Overlook, the Scourge still roam in great numbers, and the Forsaken lose soldiers every day trying to take the ruins back.",chapters={{after=92422,articles={"The Wrath of Rath'mael","Rath'mael"},text="An unholy fog has crept over the streets and graveyard of the ruins, letting the Scourge move unseen. The Deathguard of Brill traced it to a new power among them, a necromancer called Rath'mael, conjuring it from the Lordaeron Graveyard. Rath'mael is destroyed, and though Deathguard Kristof is sure another will take his place, the Scourge's ranks are thinner for now."},{after=92401,articles={"A Frightened Request"},text="A Forsaken couple lost their son when Arthas razed the city, and his body was never found. Edward Heartweaver could not let go of his guilt and grief, and every week he went searching the overrun graveyard for the boy, until one week he did not come back. A stranger went into the ruins to learn his fate; his wife thanked them, and hoped they might meet again without such sorrow."},{after=95161,articles={"Remember That I Love You (2)","Remember That I Love You"},text="In the graveyard lay a torn, bloodstained letter about a missing child of the Heartweaver family. The orphanage in Stormwind remembered him: little Jeremy Heartweaver, brought in after Lordaeron fell, who had lost both his parents and was recently adopted by Avette Fellwood of Duskwood. Avette took the letter, but will not give it to him yet: Jeremy has not spoken since the attack, and she will show him his past when he is older."},{after=97292,articles={"Unending Torment (5)","Unending Torment","Unending Torment (2)","Unending Torment (4)"},text="The abomination called the Baron, which stalked the ruins, had lost its head and had it stitched back on many times. Master Apothecary Faranell knew the head: it had once belonged to Othmar Garithos, a Grand Marshal of the Alliance whom the Dark Lady killed herself. Joined to a fresh body in the Apothecarium, Garithos woke reliving his last moments and refused to serve, so Faranell fed him a hissing serum of poisons to bend him to Sylvanas's will. The apothecary has many plans for him."}}},
[-2998]={articles={"Excavation Site: Wetlands","Songblade Search","Lost in the Thicket Things"},text="In the highlands of the Wetlands, Prospector Whelgar's dwarves have dug into a mountain gully where no dwarf had set foot before, opening ruins of some bygone age. The highland behind them is a world of its own, of towering grasses, thicket raptors and strange creatures, and the dig drew in diggers, herbalists and the curious from across the Alliance.",chapters={{after=95646,articles={"Horrors in the Highland"},text="Rethiel the Greenwarden felt a corruption seeping through the gully into the Wetlands, and the highland's creatures, without the hardening salt of the lowland waters, have little defence against it. The root core of a Highland Horror confirmed her fears: an ancient energy is stirring in the ruins, and she warns the excavators to stop digging for their own safety."},{after=95795,articles={"Fallen in the Fen","Songblade Search"},text="Daewyn Songblade joined a digging crew from Loch Modan bound for the highlands, and when his letters stopped, his brother Dorin, a smith in Lakeshire, asked travellers to look for him. Daewyn was found dead inside the excavation, his gun still in his hands. Dorin wept for not going with him, and gave away the armour he had been forging as a surprise for his brother."},{after=95682,articles={"Open the Maw","Dragonmaw Rumors"},text="Rumours reached the Forsaken that the Dragonmaw, the clan that once bound dragons to its will, was preparing some display of power in the Wetlands. A Deathstalker agent sent Horde fighters to clear the Dragonmaw out of the excavation, and took their dispatches before anyone else could read them. The clan's remnants hope that what they found will win back Rend Blackhand's favour; the agent thinks it a wasted effort, and the news is for the Dark Lady alone."},{after=98824,articles={"Prehistoric Prism","Lost Relic Carry","Relic Guardian"},text="At the heart of the ruins stood the Relic Guardian, a stone automaton, and from its broken body came a shard of some unknown composite that scatters light like a living prism. Prospector Whelgar saw titan-like inscriptions on it and sent it to Ironforge, where High Explorer Magellas declared it marked by the keepers beyond doubt, a find to push forward the world's wisdom."},{after=98823,articles={"Earthen Echo","Elder Knowledge","Relic Guardian"},text="The Horde, too, carried a shard of the Relic Guardian out of the ruins, and the elders of Thunder Bluff sent it to Muln Earthfury of the Earthen Ring. He heard the voice of the world in it: the light inside is a memory of the past, held by a titan machine that had waited lifetimes to be understood. The Earthen Ring will keep it safe and study it until it can share its truths."}}},
[-2994]={articles={"Alcaz Prison","Alcaz Island"},text="Alcaz Prison stands on Alcaz Island, a tropical island off the northeast coast of Dustwallow Marsh that the Strashaz naga once controlled. Its keepers include a torturer known only as the Questioner, and both Theramore and Orgrimmar have reasons to want to know what goes on behind its walls.",chapters={{after=96337,articles={"More Valuable Than Gold","The Man in the Iron Chains"},text="Deep in the prison, after its dragon fell, a prisoner named Mason Connery sat in a ball and chain, and the key was on the Questioner's belt. Freed, Connery had no gold to give, only a note leading to something worth more, hidden beneath a loose floorboard in the inn at Moonbrook in Westfall. He asked one thing in return: if anyone asks, say he died, vaporised by dragonfire and blown out to sea."}}},
[-2970]={articles={"Drowned City"},text="The Drowned City lies beneath the waves off the western coast of Stranglethorn Vale, south of the Haunted Isle and due west of Grom'gol Base Camp, and the only way in is underwater. Its sunken ruins, with a ship beached among them, draw adventurers from Booty Bay, Grom'gol and the Rebel Camp alike."},
[-2959]={articles={"City of Dalaran","Power Overwhelming (Forever)","Key to the City (Forever)"},text="Dalaran, the city of the Kirin Tor beside Lordamere Lake, was flattened by the archdemon Archimonde in the Third War. Its surviving mages have been rebuilding it behind magical wards, reclusive and distrustful of outsiders, while the Forsaken watch from Tarren Mill, uneasy at a city of Alliance mages rising on the Dark Lady's doorstep.",chapters={{after=92459,articles={"Friend of the Kirin Tor","Heart of Disruption (Alliance)","An Alarming Request","Shade of the Archmage"},text="Something disrupted Dalaran's ley lines with a thunderous crash in the parlours atop the Violet Citadel, and the city's constructs and enchantments turned wild. The high council ordered the matter kept quiet, but Archmage Modera quietly sent for outside help and slipped it into the city through the sewers. With the arcane mote at the source recovered from the Shade of the Archmage, the city is calming, the wards can be lowered, and the council will have to accept the results, however it feels about the subterfuge."},{after=96986,articles={"The Grave Knight"},text="Beneath Dalaran, in the city's underbelly, a necromancer of disturbing talent was raising an army of the dead right under the Kirin Tor's noses. To the Forsaken he was a traitor to their kind, Atrexis the Grave Knight. Melisara of Tarren Mill cared nothing for the mages' fate, but she wanted him to learn that no one is beyond the Dark Lady's reach, and now he has."},{after=97287,articles={"Shrewd Negotiations","Blood in the Streets","Heart of Disruption (Horde)"},text="When Dalaran's barrier fell, Magus Wordeen Voidglare of Tarren Mill sent agents in through a sewer key found at the mages' lakeside camp. Archmage Modera, desperate, let them help: if Dalaran fell, it would take the Undercity with it. But Wordeen kept the arcane mote taken from the citadel, meaning to trade it back to the Kirin Tor for the one thing he wants, a way into their vast store of arcane knowledge."}}},
[-362]={articles={"Scarlet Monastery","Mythology of the Titans (quest)"},text="The library of the Scarlet Monastery holds the old collections of Lordaeron's church, gathered when the monastery was a seminary for paladins. The Crusade guards them now, and uses them to train its zealots.",chapters={{after=1050,articles={"Mythology of the Titans (quest)"},text="Librarian Mae Paledust of the Explorers' League believed that by studying the past, people can be ready for the future, and that the monastery's corrupt halls are no place for a historical treasure. She wanted a book rumoured to be kept in its library, the Mythology of the Titans. The book is safe in Ironforge now, to be studied and kept for the dwarves."},{after=1049,articles={"Compendium of the Fallen (Classic)"},text="A tauren sage in Thunder Bluff doubted the Forsaken, whose history is full of deceit, and feared the tauren made their pact with them too hastily. He suspected, too, that the voices whispering in the Elder Council's ears had their own motives. The Crusade's Compendium of the Fallen, a book he half doubted existed, is now in his hands, one piece of a puzzle he means to solve before advising Cairne."},{after=1160,articles={"Test of Lore (5)"},text="The Forsaken scholar Parqual Fintallas tests those who seek his knowledge, and sent them for Beginnings of the Undead Threat, a book the monastery's paladins used to learn how to fight his kind. The Crusade were followers of the Light once, he says, but now they would cleanse anything not of their faith, the Forsaken first of all. With the book taken from them, Parqual has his question ready."}}},
[-361]={articles={"Scarlet Monastery"},text="The graveyard of the Scarlet Monastery is where the Crusade buries its dead and keeps its prisoners. Interrogators work here, and the restless dead it has failed to keep in the ground stir among the crypts.",chapters={{after=1051,articles={"Vorrel's Revenge"},text="The Forsaken Vorrel Sengutz was found dying in the graveyard, his will his own but his life force broken after weeks of torture by Interrogator Vishas, who took his wedding ring and gave it to his own wife, Nancy, in her house by Lake Lordamere. Vorrel's last wish was revenge. Nancy Vishas is dead, and the ring has gone home to Vorrel's widow, Monika, in Tarren Mill, where she mourns him."}}},
[-318]={articles={"Bodley","Anthion Harmon"},text="Deliana in Ironforge and Mokvar in Orgrimmar seek out seasoned adventurers who have proven themselves in Azeroth's deadliest places, offering better arms for help with a matter they do not explain at first. The work leads far: to a goblin tinkerer in Gadgetzan, the haunted gates of Stratholme, the halls of Dire Maul, and the heights of Blackrock Spire.",chapters={{after=8928,articles={"A Shifty Merchant","The Ectoplasmic Distiller","Hunting for Ectoplasm","A Portable Power Source"},text="Mux Manascrambler of Gadgetzan had a device in mind but no money to build it: an extra-dimensional ghost revealer, to reach those whose souls have not quite left the world. It took ectoplasm distilled from the restless dead of Silithus, Winterspring and the Eastern Plaguelands, the magma core of Magma Lord Bokk, and a fel elemental rod bought from an imp in Darkwhisper Gorge. The finished revealer can show the living what no one else can see."},{after=8946,articles={"Proof of Life","Dead Man's Plea","Ysida Harmon"},text="Before the gates of Stratholme waits the ghost of Anthion Harmon. His wife Ysida joined the Argent Dawn's crusade against his wishes, and was taken prisoner by Baron Rivendare; Anthion died trying to save her. Adventurers finished what he started and freed her from the Baron, and she sent her locket back with them as proof she lived. At peace at last, Anthion agreed to answer their questions while his time in the world lasts."},{after=9015,articles={"The Challenge (quest)","Anthion's Strange Request","Anthion's Old Friend","Falrin's Vendetta","Theldren"},text="Anthion once led a mercenary company that took a medallion from a powerful foe and split it with the rest of the spoils, not knowing what it was. The first piece went to the dwarf Theldren, who beat Anthion within an inch of his life when he tried to take it back, and became a gladiator for the Dark Irons. Falrin Treeshaper of Dire Maul, after a bloody price paid in ogre warbeads, enchanted a banner in Theldren's family colours that he could not ignore. Planted in the Ring of the Law, it drew him into the fight, and the first piece of the amulet is recovered."},{after=8996,articles={"Return to Bodley","Mea Culpa, Lord Valthalak","Bodley","Lord Valthalak","Three Kings of Flame"},text="The ghost of Bodley in Blackrock Mountain told the rest. His company, the Veiled Blade, was hired by a Booty Bay smuggler to steal the spellbook of Lord Valthalak, the drakonid warlock who was general of Blackrock Spire before Drakkisath. They killed him, but the company fought over his amulet until it broke in three, and his spirit's spectral assassins have hunted them one by one ever since. With the pieces gathered, Valthalak was summoned in his old chamber and slain again, and his amulet returned. Scornful but satisfied, he has called off his assassins, for now."}}},
[213]={articles={"Ragefire Chasm (Classic)"},text="Ragefire Chasm is a network of volcanic caverns beneath Orgrimmar, the orcs' new capital, entered from the Cleft of Shadow. Troggs have lately begun climbing up out of its lava-filled tunnels, and the tauren elder Magatha Grimtotem's attempt to make peace with them was met with violence.\n\nRumour also holds that a cult loyal to the demonic Shadow Council has hidden itself in the chasm's fiery depths, right under the Warchief's city. Many believe Thrall knows, and lets it be in the hope it will lead him to the Council itself.",chapters={{after=5724,articles={"Returning the Lost Satchel","Searching for the Lost Satchel","Testing an Enemy's Strength"},text="The troggs were not all that drew Magatha Grimtotem's eye to the chasm. One of her servants, Maur Grimtotem, found something strange while trying to speak with them, but never came out to show her. His body lay deep in the caverns, beaten by trogg clubs, with a satchel still clutched in his arms as if he had died protecting it; it has gone back to Thunder Bluff, and what it held is Magatha's business."},{after=5761,articles={"Slaying the Beast (quest)"},text="The cult in the chasm is the Searing Blade, and its master was Taragaman the Hungerer, a huge demon who stood on an island in a lake of lava. Neeru Fireblade, the warlock of the Cleft of Shadow, claimed he wanted the cult rooted out for Thrall's sake, and sent adventurers to bring back Taragaman's heart. The demon is dead, and Neeru promised to tell the Warchief himself."},{after=5730,articles={"Hidden Enemies (5)","Hidden Enemies (3)","Hidden Enemies (2)","Hidden Enemies"},text="Thrall knew all along that Neeru Fireblade secretly leads the Burning Blade, and that the Burning Blade is the Shadow Council itself. Carrying a Burning Blade insignia, a spy won Neeru's trust, then went into the chasm and slew the Searing Blade's other leaders, the satyr Bazzalan and the warlock Jergosh the Invoker. Neeru, raging at Thrall's \"do-gooders\" and never suspecting the spy, let slip that the Council will turn to new schemes in the Barrens and Ashenvale, and Thrall's agents are already on their trail."}}},
[219]={articles={"Zul'Farrak"},text="Zul'Farrak is the sun-blasted city of the Sandfury trolls in northwestern Tanaris. Tanaris was once jungle, part of the Gurubashi Empire, until the Great Sundering turned it to desert; the Farraki trolls stranded there adapted to the sands and became sand trolls.\n\nThe Sandfury are known for their ruthlessness and dark mysticism, and are said to be raising an army of undead trolls. Rumour tells of a band of mercenaries from Gadgetzan trapped inside the walls, and of an ancient creature asleep in a sacred pool at the city's heart, which none should be foolish enough to wake.",chapters={{after=2991,articles={"Nekrum's Medallion (quest)"},text="Thadius Grimshade, a mystic at Nethergarde Keep, agreed to divine where Sharpbeak, a lost Wildhammer gryphon, had been taken, but only for a price: the medallion of Nekrum Gutchewer, one of Zul'Farrak's chieftains. The medallion is said to have given the troll an unnaturally long life, at a cost. Grimshade has it now and means to take its power without its unpleasant side effects; in return, he has kept his side of the bargain."},{after=2936,articles={"The Spider God"},text="Master Gadrin of Sen'jin Village needed the Witherbark trolls' most potent venom, which comes from their spider god, and she can be summoned only by her true name. That name was carved on a tablet in the tomb of Theka the Martyr in Zul'Farrak. It is Elortha no Shadra, and Gadrin warns that it should be spoken only in whispers, for it carries great weight in the realm of spirits."},{after=2770,articles={"Gahz'rilla (quest)"},text="At the Mirage Raceway in the Shimmering Flats, the gnome Wizzle Brassbolts dreamed of powering his rocket car with the scales of Gahz'rilla, a huge beast that the Sandfury summon from their sacred pool, so charged with energy that its scales crackle. The summoning is a closely kept troll secret, but it was wrested from them, and the creature sleeping at the city's heart has been faced. Wizzle has his electrified scale, and only wants to know whether Gahz'rilla was as big as they say."},{after=4787,articles={"The Ancient Egg","The Prophecy of Mosh'aru"},text="Yeh'kinya, an old troll at Steamwheedle Port, sought the prophecy of Mosh'aru, which tells of a way to contain the essence of Hakkar the Soulflayer. Its two tablets lay in Zul'Farrak, one with Theka the Martyr, whose persecutors were cursed into scarabs, and one with the hydromancer Velratha by Gahz'rilla's pool. Fused together, they spoke of an ancient egg from the days of the great troll empires, hidden in Jintha'Alor in the Hinterlands, that could hold Hakkar's essence. The egg is in Yeh'kinya's hands now, and he senses time growing short."}}},
[220]={articles={"Temple of Atal'Hakkar"},text="The Temple of Atal'Hakkar was raised in the Swamp of Sorrows by the Atal'ai, troll priests exiled from the Gurubashi Empire after their attempt to summon their blood god, Hakkar the Soulflayer, tore the empire apart. The Aspect Ysera learned of their plans and smashed the temple beneath the swamp, where it lies drowned under the Pool of Tears, guarded by her green dragons.\n\nSome of the Atal'ai survived Ysera's wrath, and under their spiritual leader, Jammal'an the Prophet, they have returned to the temple to take up their rituals again. Explorers, the Horde and the dragons themselves all have their reasons to venture inside.",chapters={{after=1445,articles={"The Temple of Atal'Hakkar (quest)"},text="Fel'Zerul at Stonard heard from an Atal'ai exile of Jammal'an's prophecy: that once Hakkar returns from the Nether, the god will grant the Atal'ai immortality. He thought it foolish trickery that could bring an early doomsday, but feared the enchanted fetishes the exile spoke of were the key to the ritual. The fetishes of Hakkar have been seized from the temple, and the Horde means to understand their power."},{after=1446,articles={"Jammal'an the Prophet (Classic)"},text="An Atal'ai exile living among the Witherbark trolls of the Hinterlands was cast out for urging caution when Jammal'an proclaimed his prophecy; what if it was nothing more than manipulation? He wanted the prophet's head, hoping his people might be freed. Jammal'an is dead, but the exile takes no joy in it: the prophet's reckless trust in false visions has already doomed the Atal'ai, and he has no home left to return to."},{after=3512,articles={"In Eranikus' Own Words","The Essence of Eranikus (2)"},text="In the temple's depths lingers the shade of Eranikus, a green dragon twisted by nightmares, and from it came a gem holding what free will is left of his mind. Itharius, of Ysera's dragonflight, believes his soul worthy of redemption, and took the gem to reach him. Eranikus lies between sleep and death, so Itharius sent for Umbranse the Spiritspeaker, a human medium in Winterspring who speaks for the dead. Umbranse is willing, but his most potent augury device has been stolen, and until it is back, Eranikus cannot speak in his own words."},{after=3528,articles={"The God Hakkar"},text="Yeh'kinya sent the ancient egg into the Sanctum of the Fallen God. Invoked there, it stirred Hakkar's minions; their blood put out the eternal flames that held his spirit, and the avatar of Hakkar entered the world, to be defeated and its essence drawn into the egg. Yeh'kinya felt the egg stir with life, and vowed to keep it from evil hands, though he wondered aloud what Hakkar might do if he were ever born again."}}},
[221]={articles={"Blackfathom Deeps (Classic)"},text="Blackfathom Deeps is a half-flooded temple on the Zoram Strand of Ashenvale, reached by swimming down a sunken stair. It was once a glorious temple to the night elves' moon goddess, Elune, until the Sundering cast it beneath the waves of the Veiled Sea.\n\nNaga and satyrs, drawn by its ancient power, have come to plumb its secrets, and legend says that Aku'mai, a favoured pet of the Old Gods, now dwells in its ruins. The cult of the Twilight's Hammer has come to bask in its presence, and the shamans of the Earthen Ring and the Argent Dawn have both taken notice.",chapters={{after=971,articles={"Knowledge in the Deeps (Classic)"},text="Gerrig Bonegrip, a strange collector of lore who dwells in Ironforge's Forlorn Cavern, wanted the Lorgalis Manuscript from the bottom of the Deeps: old elven lore, he said, of the kind that opens doorways and awakens \"Those Who Sleep\". The manuscript is his now, and he was overjoyed. Copies will be made, he promised, and its knowledge shared at last with his brothers and sisters."},{after=6565,articles={"Allegiance to the Old Gods (2)","Allegiance to the Old Gods","The Essence of Aku'Mai (Classic)"},text="Je'neu Sancrea of the Earthen Ring, at Zoram'gar Outpost, saw the naga harvesting sapphires charged with the spirits of water from the Deeps' walls. A damp note found in the temple explained why: the crystals were to fuel the cult's water-summonings and appease Aku'mai, until there was power enough to bring back the age of the Old Gods. The man behind the plan, the Twilight's Hammer cultist Lorgus Jett, is dead; the cult is not finished, Je'neu says, but this plot at least is undone."},{after=1200,articles={"Blackfathom Villainy (Alliance)","In Search of Thaelrid"},text="The Argent Dawn's scout, Argent Guard Thaelrid, was found weak and wounded in the Deeps. He told how the corruption of an Old God seeped up through the temple's sacred moonwell, and how Aku'mai rose from its waters; the Twilight's Hammer, allied with the naga, sacrifices innocents to the beast for power. Their leader, Twilight Lord Kelris, a man the Argent Dawn had thought dead or missing, is slain, and his head was brought to Dawnwatcher Selgorm in Darnassus."},{after=6561,articles={"Blackfathom Villainy (Horde)"},text="The Argent Dawn's scout, Argent Guard Thaelrid, was found weak and wounded in the Deeps. He told how the corruption of an Old God seeped up through the temple's sacred moonwell, and how Aku'mai rose from its waters; the Twilight's Hammer, allied with the naga, sacrifices innocents to the beast for power. Their leader, Twilight Lord Kelris, is slain, and Bashana Runetotem in Thunder Bluff gave thanks to the Earthmother that his sacrifices are ended."}}},
[225]={articles={"Stormwind Stockade (Classic)"},text="The Stockade is Stormwind's high-security prison, hidden beneath the city's canal district and kept by Warden Thelwater. It holds petty crooks, political insurgents, murderers and some of the most dangerous criminals in the land, many of them Defias.\n\nNot long ago the prisoners rose up and drove out the guards, and now the convicts roam the cell blocks freely. The warden has escaped the holding area and is looking for brave souls to go in and put an end to the riot.",chapters={{after=388,articles={"The Color of Blood"},text="With the army sent away to the front, Nikova Raskol's grandson Mac took up arms to defend the city, and was posted as a guard in the Stockade at fourteen. When he scolded the Defias prisoners for tearing up their wool blankets to make red scarves, the badge of their gang, they stabbed him in the back. Ten of their blood-red bandanas have been brought to his grieving grandmother, who wonders that her boy died for such filthy rags."},{after=386,articles={"What Comes Around..."},text="Targorr the Dread was the Blackrock orcs' executioner under Gath'Ilzogg, and tortured the Redridge's defenders at his whim. He was sentenced to die in the Stockade, but some noble put a hold on his execution, and Lakeshire's guards suspected foul play. Guard Berton saw no reason to wait for the courts: Targorr is dead, and justice was found outside the courtroom."},{after=377,articles={"Crime and Punishment (Classic)"},text="Dextren Ward was caught selling bodies from Duskwood's cemetery to the necromancer Morbent Fel, and Lord Ebonlocke sentenced him to death; instead the House of Nobles claimed him as Stormwind's prisoner. Councilman Millstipe of Darkshire took that as an insult to his council. Ward is dead and his hand is in Darkshire, and Millstipe warns that if Stormwind will not see to Duskwood's needs, Duskwood may break away."},{after=391,articles={"The Stockade Riots","Bazil Thredd (quest)"},text="Bazil Thredd, Edwin VanCleef's lieutenant from his Stonemason days, was locked in the Stockade after the masons' riot, and the uprising was his doing. Warden Thelwater suspected anyone who asked for Thredd of being one of his cronies, but let them go down to him on the condition that they come back with his head. Thredd is dead, the riot leaderless, and the warden admits he misjudged the stranger who did it; he knows a thing or two about Thredd's dealings himself."}}},
[226]={articles={"Gnomeregan"},text="Gnomeregan was the gnomes' underground capital in Dun Morogh, a marvel of gnomish engineering. During the Third War troggs, awakened by dwarven digging, burrowed up into its lowest levels, and after years of fighting the gnomes flooded their own city with toxic radiation to destroy them.\n\nThe troggs survived, and many grew stronger. Most of the gnomes died within days, others were turned into mindless leper gnomes, and the survivors fled to Ironforge. Troggs and lepers hold the irradiated city now, while the exiles in Tinker Town plan how to take it back.",chapters={{after=2922,articles={"Save Techbot's Brain!","Tinkmaster Overspark (quest)"},text="When Gnomeregan fell, a stray spark jolted Tinkmaster Overspark's creation Techbot, once a kind and helpful machine full of gadgets and knowledge. Its brain's polarity flipped, its behaviour turned to the opposite, and it roamed the city in a frenzy. Techbot's memory core is back in Overspark's hands in Ironforge, and he is already at work trying to fix his poor machine."},{after=2904,articles={"A Fine Mess"},text="Deep in the city, the goblin Kernobee was found beaten and thrown in with the troggs, captured by Dark Iron dwarves. He was led out alive and went to Ratchet to warn the goblin engineers that the Dark Irons have a hand in building Gnomeregan's new super rig. Scooty in Booty Bay rewarded his rescuers, and had a few words for the backstabbing Mekgineer Thermaplugg."},{after=2841,articles={"Rig Wars"},text="Gnomeregan's engineers have built a new rig better than every goblin shredder put together, and Nogg of Orgrimmar wanted the blueprints. They were locked in a safe beside Mekgineer Thermaplugg himself, who alone knew the combination. The blueprints are in Nogg's workshop now, and the gnomes' innovations will soon be goblin innovations."},{after=2929,articles={"The Grand Betrayal"},text="High Tinker Mekkatorque trusted Mekgineer Thermaplugg, and Thermaplugg betrayed him and all the gnomish people: the gnomes were driven from their home, and the madman rules what is left of it, a king of nothing. Mekkatorque asked one task of those who would join the fight to retake the city: kill the betrayer. Thermaplugg is dead, and the High Tinker says his plans to retake Gnomeregan are one step closer."}}},
[230]={articles={"Uldaman","Discs of Norgannon"},text="Uldaman is an ancient titan vault buried deep beneath the mountains of the Badlands: a ruined city of carved stone halls, watched over by great guardians of living rock. Before the Third War the dwarves of Ironforge dug into it in search of artifacts and knowledge, and woke the troggs sleeping in its depths, who slaughtered most of the diggers and drove the rest out.\n\nTroggs now swarm the old excavation, and Dark Iron dwarves have pushed into its halls as well. The Explorers' League has never given up on the place: legend holds that its deepest chamber keeps the titans' own records of their works, set down on enormous platinum discs.",chapters={{after=2240,articles={"The Hidden Chamber","The Lost Dwarves (quest)"},text="When the troggs overran the dig, a small band of dwarves led by Baelog was cut off deep inside, and Prospector Stormpike feared them lost. They are alive, holed up and trading bets on how long they will wait, but unable to leave. Baelog's journal told how to open the sealed door of the Chamber of Khaz'mul, a secret vault he was sure existed, and it has now been found. Stormpike hopes the discovery will stir Ironforge's leaders to retake Uldaman, so that the chamber's secrets can be theirs."},{after=2278,articles={"The Platinum Discs (neutral)","Lore Keeper of Norgannon","Discs of Norgannon"},text="At the bottom of Uldaman stand the Discs of Norgannon, four huge platinum discs covered in unknown sigils. At a touch they call up a stone watcher, a keeper of knowledge, which told how the Creators made the earthen: a race shaped from the stone of Azeroth itself, to work the world's deep places. Under strain the earthen's stone could break down, and the records name what they became: the brutish troggs, and the dwarves. The discs then made a miniature copy of themselves to carry out of the vault."},{after=2964,articles={"A Future Task (Alliance)","Return to Ironforge","The Stone Watcher","Seeing What Happens (Alliance)","Portents of Uldum (Alliance)","The Platinum Discs (Alliance)"},text="To the Explorers' League the miniature discs were the find of the century, proof at last of the dwarves' enchanted creation, and Historian Karnik tied them to the Valley of the Watchers in Tanaris. Carried to the sealed gates of Uldum, they woke another stone watcher, which refused entry without the Plates of Uldum, discs that the Creators keep for themselves. The League has no idea where the plates might be, so the search is on hold; Uldum will have to wait."},{after=2968,articles={"A Future Task (Horde)","Return to Thunder Bluff (2)","The Stone Watcher","Seeing What Happens (Horde)","The Platinum Discs"},text="The miniature discs went to the sages of Thunder Bluff, who traced them to the Valley of the Watchers in Tanaris. Carried to the sealed gates of Uldum, they woke another stone watcher, which refused entry without the Plates of Uldum, discs that the Creators keep for themselves. Nara Wildmane does not know whether to be excited or terrified: some secrets the earth keeps might destroy everyone, and perhaps should stay buried. For now no one knows where the plates lie, and the search must wait."}}},
[234]={articles={"Dire Maul"},text="Dire Maul, in the north of Feralas, was once Eldre'Thalas, a city built twelve thousand years ago by Highborne sorcerers to protect Queen Azshara's most prized arcane secrets. The Great Sundering ravaged it, but much of it still stands, its stones bound with arcane power.\n\nIts three quarters are overrun now: satyrs and corrupted guardians of nature in the Warpwood Quarter, spectral Highborne in the Capital Gardens, and the Gordunni ogres of King Gordok in the Gordok Commons. Of the city's elves, only a secretive sect called the Shen'dralar remains, withdrawn into its heart.",chapters={{after=5526,articles={"Shards of the Felvine (Classic)","A Reliquary of Purity"},text="Eldre'Thalas once flourished around the Fruit of Fertility, a vine of protection and growth, until the satyr Alzzin the Wildshaper corrupted it into the Felvine. Rabine Saturna of the Cenarion Circle needed a Reliquary of Purity to hold anything so vile, and the last one known was recovered from the ruins of Southwind in Silithus. Alzzin is dead and a shard of the Felvine sealed inside it, so the Circle can study the taint and seek new ways to fight corruption."},{after=7482,articles={"Elven Legends (Alliance)"},text="A high elf master fled the ruin of Quel'Thalas carrying ancient elven artifacts, and the scholars at Feathermoon Stronghold tracked him across the sea to Feralas, where he sought sanctuary in Eldre'Thalas. His bones lie in the Shen'dralar's library, beside Lorekeeper Lydros, who admits that the Prince took exception to a high elf in his domain. Lydros wants no more attention drawn to his people, so word went back that nothing was found; in return, he will share the dead master's secrets."},{after=7481,articles={"Elven Legends (Horde)"},text="A high elf master fled the ruin of Quel'Thalas carrying ancient elven artifacts, and the scholars at Camp Mojache tracked him across the sea to Feralas, where he sought sanctuary in Eldre'Thalas. His bones lie in the Shen'dralar's library, beside Lorekeeper Lydros, who admits that the Prince took exception to a high elf in his domain. Lydros wants no more attention drawn to his people, so word went back that nothing was found; in return, he will share the dead master's secrets."},{after=7461,articles={"The Madness Within (Classic)","Dire Maul"},text="Cut off from the Well of Eternity, the Highborne of Eldre'Thalas imprisoned a demon, Immol'thar, and lived on its power for thousands of years. When binding it began to cost more than it gave, Prince Tortheldrin slaughtered many of his own people to keep more of its power for himself and the rest. The pylons of its prison are down, Immol'thar is destroyed and the Prince slain, and the spirits of the Highborne have gathered in the courtyard to give thanks."},{after=7703,articles={"Unfinished Gordok Business (Classic)"},text="The Gordok have a new king, and their captain told the new ruler an old story: Tortheldrin, the \"creepy elf\" who calls himself Prince, stole the Gauntlet of Gordok Might from an ogre king long ago, and every king since had failed to get it back. The gauntlet has been found, drained of its power, but it bears a mark older than the Gordok, and the ogres know how to bring it back to life. The gauntlet has its strength again, and the true king of the Gordok wears it."}}},
[242]={articles={"Blackrock Depths"},text="Blackrock Depths is the deepest part of Blackrock Mountain, the volcanic capital of the Dark Iron dwarves: Shadowforge City, its prison and arena, its forges and its tavern, the Grim Guzzler, much of it built by the master architect Franclorn Forgewright. Emperor Dagran Thaurissan rules here, the latest in a line of sorcerer-thanes.\n\nThe Dark Irons labour for Ragnaros the Firelord, whom their own ancestors brought into the world centuries ago, and his fire elementals drive them towards ever more destructive ends. From the Burning Steppes outside, the Alliance at Morgan's Vigil and the Horde's expeditionary force at Kargath both fight the mountain's masters.",chapters={{after=3802,articles={"Dark Iron Legacy (2)","Dark Iron Legacy"},text="The ghost of Franclorn Forgewright haunts his tomb in the lava pit before the Depths. Under Ragnaros's control, King Thaurissan tore out the heart of the mountain, and Franclorn built Blackrock Spire for him, mightier than any fortress before it; but he watched the Firelord's corruption drive his people to madness and evil, and when the Spire was done, his will to live among them gave out. His hammer, Ironfel, has been taken back from Fineous Darkvire and placed in the hands of his statue at the Shrine of Thaurissan, where it will stay forever, and the compartment beneath it gave up his master key to the city."},{after=3907,articles={"Disharmony of Fire"},text="Thunderheart, a shaman at Kargath, tasted a foulness on the winds of the Badlands, and the wind and earth cried a name: Lord Incendius, an emissary of something hidden deeper in Blackrock. The Tablet of Kurniya, taken from Incendius, named his master: Ragnaros, a general of the Old Gods, in this world. Thunderheart warns that Kargath must choose between standing to fight and fleeing a new sundering, for a greater evil than anything in the world lies in those fiery depths."},{after=3701,articles={"The Smoldering Ruins of Thaurissan (2)","The Smoldering Ruins of Thaurissan"},text="Royal Historian Archesonus of Ironforge sent a stranger to the smouldering ruins of Thaurissan in the Burning Steppes, guarded by Dark Iron assassins. The relics there burned away into memories: eight Dark Iron dwarves performing a ritual, a lone dwarf kneeling among seven corpses before a monolith of flame, a creature made entirely of fire. The city was destroyed by a being not of this world, and something evil still lurks in the heart of the mountain."},{after=4322,articles={"Jail Break!","A Shred of Hope","Abandoned Hope","Marshal Windsor (quest)","The True Masters"},text="In the Burning Steppes, the Alliance learned that the black dragonflight drives the Blackrock orcs, and the trail led to Marshal Reginald Windsor, a prisoner in the Depths. Windsor had been gathering proof for Highlord Bolvar, whom he calls blind to a reptile standing two feet away from him, but his evidence was lost, until a crumpled note showed that General Angerforge and Golem Lord Argelmach held it. With his tablets back, Windsor broke out with his gear and his fellow prisoners and stormed past Morgan's Vigil, bound for Stormwind to confront Bolvar and Lady Prestor."},{after=6403,articles={"The Great Masquerade","Stormwind Rendezvous"},text="Windsor's evidence proved it: Lady Prestor is Onyxia, brood of Deathwing and twin of Nefarian, and for years she had held Stormwind, and Highlord Bolvar himself, under her spell. In the halls of Stormwind Keep she was unmasked and fled, but Windsor gave his life to break her hold. Bolvar, free at last, holds the shattered medallion that bound him; it once let him glimpse her mind, and he believes the dragon's eye within it can be attuned again to reach her."},{after=4132,articles={"Operation: Death to Angerforge","Precarious Predicament"},text="General Angerforge, the Butcher of Blackrock, coordinated the Dark Iron attacks that slaughtered the 109th division of the Kargath Expeditionary Force. After the capture of the traitor Grark Lorkrub, Warlord Goretooth sent his best on a last mission into the Depths. Angerforge has been brought to justice, and the K.E.F. honours its avenger with a medallion."},{after=4363,articles={"The Princess's Surprise","Kharan's Tale","The Fate of the Kingdom"},text="Kharan Mighthammer, a prisoner in the Depths, told how Princess Moira had insisted on going to help Lakeshire against the Blackrock, and how Dark Irons poured out of the very rocks to ambush her escort in the Burning Steppes. King Magni learned that his old adversary Thaurissan held his daughter under a spell, and asked for the emperor's death, without harming her. Thaurissan is dead, but Moira wept over him: she carries his child, and swears that the heir to Ironforge will be a Dark Iron dwarf. Magni slumped into his throne, saying that even in death, Thaurissan has won."},{after=4004,articles={"The Princess Saved?","The Royal Rescue","What Is Going On?","Commander Gor'shak (quest)"},text="Commander Gor'shak let the Dark Irons capture him so that he could spy for Thrall, and learned that Thaurissan had kidnapped Princess Moira Bronzebeard of Ironforge and held her in the city under his control. Thrall wanted her freed by the emperor's death. Thaurissan is dead, but Moira named him her husband and swore that the child she carries will rule Ironforge, and that the Horde will be hunted for this. Thrall sees a new threat: as Magni's years dwindle, a Dark Iron could take Ironforge's throne, and he must take counsel with Sylvanas and Cairne."}}},
[248]={articles={"Onyxia's Lair"},text="Below the Dragonmurk, in the Wyrmbog of Dustwallow Marsh, a fiery cave carved to look like a dragon's maw leads to the lair of Onyxia, brood mother of the black dragonflight. She is a daughter of the Aspect Deathwing and twin of Nefarian, lord of Blackrock Spire.\n\nWhere her father favours brute force and her brother breeds abominations, Onyxia prefers subtle manipulation, and delights in meddling in the affairs of mortal kingdoms. Her kin guard her lair, and her wards keep out anyone without the key.",chapters={{after=7496,articles={"Celebrating Good Times","Victory for the Alliance"},text="Onyxia is dead, and her head was brought to Highlord Bolvar Fordragon, who was held so long under her captivating gaze. To Bolvar her death is justice at last and the honour of Stormwind restored. In the Valley of Heroes, Major Mattingly proclaimed the deed to the city, and the dread lady who tried to usurp the kingdom now hangs from its arches."},{after=7491,articles={"For All To See","Victory for the Horde"},text="Onyxia is dead, and when her enormous head was brought before Thrall, the Warchief's stoic face cracked for a moment and his hand went to the Doomhammer. Calling it a great victory for the Horde, Thrall chose to honour the hero who had so often honoured him, and Overlord Runthak led the celebration in Orgrimmar's Valley of Strength."}}},
[250]={articles={"Blackrock Spire"},text="Blackrock Spire is the great fortress in the upper reaches of Blackrock Mountain, designed by the master mason Franclorn Forgewright as a symbol of Dark Iron power. In the First War the Shadow Council hid there, and when the old Horde stormed the mountain it took the Spire as its headquarters.\n\nNow Nefarian, son of Deathwing, and his black dragonflight hold the upper spire and make war on the Dark Irons below, while Nefarian works on his sinister experiments. The lower city belongs to the Dark Horde, orcs, ogres and trolls who serve the dragons, and to the dragonspawn armies of his generals.",chapters={{after=8181,articles={"Confront Yeh'kinya"},text="The last Mosh'aru tablets, recovered from Blackrock Spire, told Prospector Ironboot the grim truth: the egg into which Hakkar's essence was captured would not contain the god, but recreate him. Confronted, Yeh'kinya laughed. Hakkar has been reborn, and grows in power in the ancient troll kingdom of Zul'Gurub, and Yeh'kinya thanked the hero who helped hasten the Soulflayer's return."},{after=4743,articles={"Seal of Ascension (2)","Seal of Ascension (quest)"},text="The image of Vaelan, of the red dragonflight, guides those who would break into the upper spire, sealed to all but bearers of the Seal of Ascension. The seal needed gemstones from three of the lower citadel's generals and forging in the flames of a black dragon, which Overlord Wyrmthalak would never give, so the will of the ancient drake Emberstrife was broken in the Wyrmbog of Dustwallow Marsh. Vaelan has warded the finished seal with the might of the red flight, and warns that Nefarian works feverishly to unleash his evil on the world."},{after=4974,articles={"For The Horde! (quest)","Eitrigg's Wisdom","Warlord's Command"},text="Among the documents taken from the lower spire's leaders was news that shook Warlord Goretooth: Rend Blackhand, son of Blackhand, thought slain decades ago, lives and calls himself Warchief. Eitrigg, who once followed Rend and lost both his sons in the Blackrock's wars with the Dark Irons, knew him for a stubborn, power-hungry fool, and Thrall ordered him destroyed. The false Warchief is dead; his head hangs beside Thrall's throne, and Thrall blessed all of Orgrimmar with his power in honour of the deed."},{after=5102,articles={"General Drakkisath's Demise","General Drakkisath's Command (quest)"},text="A letter found on Overlord Wyrmthalak, written by his superior General Drakkisath, made its way to Marshal Maxwell at Morgan's Vigil. Maxwell fights the black dragonflight on two fronts, from the Burning Steppes and inside Stormwind itself, and wanted Drakkisath, ruler of Nefarian's dragonspawn armies, destroyed. The general is dead, and Maxwell is already looking to strike at the dragon lords next."},{after=7761,articles={"Blackhand's Command (quest)"},text="A letter from Rend Blackhand, found on the Scarshield Quartermaster, gave away how to reach Nefarian's lair, Blackwing: an orb in the Spire carries visitors there, but only those branded with the Mark of Drakkisath may use it, and the general guards the brand himself. That brand has now been pressed into a stranger's hand, and the way to Blackwing Lair is open."},{after=6502,articles={"Drakefire Amulet (quest)","The Dragon's Eye (quest)"},text="Bolvar's fragment of the shattered medallion needed someone of dragon blood to reforge it, and the help came from Haleh of the blue dragonflight, in the mountains above Mazthoril in Winterspring. She found the piece of Onyxia revolting, but explained that whole, it had been a key to her lair, and that repairing it needed the blood of a black dragon champion: General Drakkisath. With his blood she made the Drakefire Amulet, and while its bearer holds it, Onyxia's wards cannot keep them out of her lair in Dustwallow Marsh."},{after=6602,articles={"Blood of the Black Dragon Champion (quest)","Ascension...","Mistress of Deception"},text="To reach Onyxia, the Horde turned to illusion. Rexxar sent a champion to Myranda the Hag, a master illusionist of old Lordaeron who owed Thrall a favour, and in disguise the champion passed the black dragonflight's trials and was given a medallion shaped from enemy skulls, to be attuned by General Drakkisath as the badge of a guardian to the brood mother. Rexxar chose a cruder way: Drakkisath is dead, and with his blood Rexxar activated the Drakefire Amulet, the key to Onyxia's lair."}}},
[279]={articles={"Wailing Caverns"},text="The Wailing Caverns are a network of caves beneath the Barrens, south of the Crossroads, named for the mournful cry of the steam that bursts from their fissures. Underground rivers run through them for miles and well up as the oases of the Barrens.\n\nThe night elf druid Naralex led his disciples into the caverns, meaning to use the springs and the Emerald Dream to make the Barrens green again. But his dream turned into a nightmare he cannot wake from: the caves have filled with twisted, deviate creatures, and many of his disciples have gone mad and become the Druids of the Fang.",chapters={{after=1487,articles={"Deviate Eradication"},text="A few of Naralex's disciples kept their minds, and still watch over the caverns from a ledge above the entrance. While some of them seek a way to wake their master, the disciple Ebru turned her attention to the deviate beasts his nightmare has spawned, and adventurers have cut their numbers down. It is only a first step, she says, in the long work of seeing the Barrens restored."},{after=914,articles={"Leaders of the Fang","Nara Wildmane (quest)","Hamuul Runetotem (quest)"},text="The oases of the Barrens are tainted, and the land seems to dream without waking. The elder druid Hamuul Runetotem entered the Dream and saw a cult of druids in the caverns, once noble and now servants of the taint, and young Nara Wildmane stayed long enough to see their scaled faces. The four leaders of the Druids of the Fang, Cobrahn, Anacondra, Pythas and Serpentis, are dead, and their dream gems are in Thunder Bluff; Nara's dreams are free of them at last."},{after=3369,articles={"In Nightmares (Horde)","The Glowing Shard"},text="Deep in the caverns, adventurers found a glowing green shard with smoke swirling inside it. Falla Sagewind, a druid camped above the caves, saw in it the pure essence of the Emerald Dream, and a nightmare rather than a dream, and sent it straight to Hamuul Runetotem. Hamuul looked into it and would say only that things in the Emerald Dream are not as the druids thought, and that it is unwise to speak of it."},{after=3370,articles={"In Nightmares (Alliance)","The Glowing Shard"},text="Deep in the caverns, adventurers found a glowing green shard with smoke swirling inside it. Falla Sagewind, a druid camped above the caves, saw in it the pure essence of the Emerald Dream, and a nightmare rather than a dream, and sent it straight to Mathrengyl Bearwalker of the Cenarion Circle in Darnassus. Mathrengyl looked into it and would say only that things in the Emerald Dream are not as the druids thought, and that it is unwise to speak of it."}}},
[280]={articles={"Maraudon"},text="Maraudon is a vast cavern beneath the Valley of Spears in Desolace, the holiest ground of the centaur and a primal temple of elemental earth. Legend says the earth elemental Princess Theradras and Zaetar, son of Cenarius, begot the first centaur khans, who murdered their father; Theradras entombed his body here, and her corruption has seeped through the caves ever since.\n\nThe Maraudine centaur guard it with their lives, and only spirits and the most devout priests may enter. Inside, the ghosts of the first khans walk among Theradras's elemental minions, and the satyr Lord Vyletongue, an agent of the Burning Legion, has come to spread his own corruption through the caves.",chapters={{after=7067,articles={"The Pariah's Instructions (quest)","The Pariah's Instructions"},text="A centaur outcast, exiled as a heretic, dreamed of forging the warring tribes into one great tribe, and needed an outsider for what no centaur could do. In Maraudon, the Nameless Prophet carried the Amulet of Spirits, incomplete without five gems held by the spirits of the first khans: Gelk, Kolk, Magra, Maraudos and Veng. The prophet has been slain on holy ground and the khans' spirits robbed, sacrilege twice over to any centaur. With the Amulet of Union made whole, the pariah means to begin uniting the tribes."},{after=7046,articles={"The Scepter of Celebras (quest)","Legends of Maraudon"},text="Celebras, Zaetar's nephew, came to Maraudon hoping to bring peace to his uncle's spirit, but the caves' corruption blinded him, and Vyletongue and his creation Noxxion stole the two parts of his scepter. Freed from his cursed form at last, Celebras's spirit joined rod and diamond into the Scepter of Celebras again, which opens a way into Earth Song Falls in the depths. He has passed his task on: to go deeper still and find his uncle's remains."},{after=7066,articles={"Seed of Life (quest)","Corruption of Earth and Seed (Alliance)","Corruption of Earth and Seed (Horde)"},text="The druids of Stonetalon wanted Theradras slain so that Zaetar's remains could be recovered, and her hold over the depths is broken. But Zaetar's spirit will not leave: his misbegotten centaur children still need their father, and the green haven his remains nourished is theirs. Instead he sent the first seed to fall from it to his brother, Keeper Remulos in Moonglade, as a sign that he lives on. Remulos, who had thought his brother lost to arrogance and anger, sees him reborn, and senses the seed will matter to this war-torn land one day."}}},
[291]={articles={"Deadmines (Classic)"},text="The Deadmines are winding tunnels dug deep beneath Westfall, once the greatest gold mines in the human lands. They were abandoned when the Horde razed Stormwind in the First War, and their way in lies through a barn in the quiet village of Moonbrook.\n\nNow the Defias Brotherhood has made the dark tunnels its private sanctum. It is rumoured that the thieves have pressed goblins into helping them build something terrible at the bottom of the mines, but no one yet knows what.",chapters={{after=167,articles={"Oh Brother. . .","Collecting Memories"},text="Wilder Thistlenettle of the Explorers' League was surveying the mine under Moonbrook when a tunnel collapsed on his crew; he got out, but the others were never heard from. His brother is dead: the foreman now walks the flooded tunnels as one of the undead, his Explorers' League badge still on him. Wilder has his answer, grim as it is, and the lost miners' union cards are going back to their families."},{after=92819,articles={"Destruction in Deadmines (2)","Destruction in Deadmines","Detonation at a Distance (2)","Moonbrook Espionage","The State of the Mines"},text="The poison in Westfall's wells and soil did not come from the mines the Defias work above ground. Alba Fairmoon, an elf studying the blight, traced it to the Deadmines, where a great forge was pouring toxic metals into the land. With explosives built by a gnome engineer in Stormwind, and a remote detonator quietly supplied by SI:7, adventurers planted a bomb by the forge and set it off from the hills behind Moonbrook. Alba hopes that with the forge gone, the land can begin to heal."},{after=166,articles={"The Defias Brotherhood (7)","The Defias Brotherhood (6)","Red Silk Bandanas"},text="A Defias traitor led adventurers through Moonbrook to the Brotherhood's hideout, and at the bottom of the Deadmines they found a hidden cove where goblins were building a great ship for the Defias. Their leader, Edwin VanCleef, is dead, and Gryan Stoutmantle of the People's Militia has his head. Stoutmantle calls it the beginning of the end for the Brotherhood, and hopes peace will come back to Westfall soon."},{after=373,articles={"The Unsent Letter","Bazil Thredd (quest)"},text="On VanCleef's body was a sealed letter, addressed to Baros Alexston, the City Architect of Stormwind. The two were once Stonemasons together: when the masons finished rebuilding Stormwind after the war, the nobles refused to pay them, and VanCleef led a riot and left the city swearing revenge. Baros remembers a peerless fighter, an idealist and a romantic, consumed by vengeance, and he admits he cannot entirely blame him."}}},
[300]={articles={"Razorfen Downs (Classic)"},text="Razorfen Downs is the quilboar's ancestral burial ground and traditional capital, a labyrinth of the same mighty thorns as Razorfen Kraul, on the eastern side of the southern Barrens. It is home to an army of quilboar and their high priests, the Death's Head tribe.\n\nLately a shadow has fallen over the downs: the dead walk among the brambles, and travellers whisper that the undead Scourge has reached Kalimdor.",chapters={{after=6521,articles={"An Unholy Alliance (Classic 2)","A Host of Evil"},text="Ambassador Malcin, the Scourge's envoy, waited in a hut outside Razorfen Downs, granting undeath to quilboar who came begging him for power. Varimathras wanted him dead, his mission a failure in the Lich King's eyes; he took some comfort that the crone of the Kraul had never agreed to the deal. Malcin is slain, and the Scourge's advance has been checked, though how deep its hold on the downs runs, the Forsaken have yet to learn."},{after=3525,articles={"Extinguishing the Idol (Classic)","Scourge of the Downs (Classic)"},text="In a cage deep in the downs sat Belnistrasz, a stranger who cared nothing for Horde or Alliance, only for stopping a threat to all the races. The Scourge had made a deal with the quilboar, he said, and near the entrance stood an idol where the quilboar cooked and ate the people they carried off from the Barrens. His rescuers held off the quilboar while he shut the idol down for good; then he vanished, leaving a brazier in which a great red dragon appeared, and spoke with his voice to thank the heroes he had hoped they would be."},{after=3341,articles={"Bring the End"},text="The Royal Apothecary Society learned that a lich, Amnennar the Coldbringer, rules the quilboar of the downs, bending their every move to his will, and that he speaks directly to Ner'zhul, the Lich King. Andrew Brownell wanted that bond severed. Amnennar is slain and his skull is in the Undercity: let the liches know, Brownell says, that such loyalty brings only destruction."},{after=3636,articles={"Bring the Light"},text="Archbishop Benedictus of Stormwind learned that the quilboar of Razorfen Downs had made a pact with the Scourge, breeding creatures far worse than before, all ruled by the lich Amnennar the Coldbringer. He called on the Light's faithful to end the unholy union. Amnennar is slain, and the Archbishop believes the Scourge will think twice before trying to swell its ranks again."}}},
[301]={articles={"Razorfen Kraul (Classic)"},text="Ten thousand years ago the demigod Agamaggan, a colossal boar, fell fighting the Burning Legion, and where his blood was spilled huge, thorny vines grew from the earth. The quilboar, believed to be his mortal children, hold these thorns sacred, and the heart of them is the Razorfen, at the southern edge of the Barrens.\n\nRazorfen Kraul, the quilboar's ancestral home, has been taken by the old crone Charlga Razorflank. Under her rule its shamanistic quilboar raid rival tribes and Horde villages alike.",chapters={{after=1142,articles={"Mortality Wanes","Lonebrow's Journal (quest)"},text="The night elf Heralath was studying the corruption of Agamaggan's resting place when the quilboar fell upon his party, and he was found dying inside the Kraul. He had sent a brave dwarf, Henrig Lonebrow, to warn Falfindel Waywarder of what they had found, but Lonebrow died on the way, his journal still in his hand. Heralath's last wish was for his stolen pendant of bonding to go home to his wife, Treshala, in Darnassus; she has it now, and mourns him alone."},{after=1101,articles={"The Crone of the Kraul"},text="Falfindel Waywarder of Thalanaar would not let Heralath and Lonebrow die for nothing, and with no time to send word to Darnassus, asked adventurers to slay the crone who was mustering an army in the Kraul. Charlga Razorflank is dead, and her medallion is at Thalanaar. The elves can go on with their studies of what corrupted Agamaggan's resting place, though Falfindel fears the answer lies in treachery as well."},{after=1102,articles={"A Vengeful Fate"},text="The tauren lived in the southern Barrens for generations, until the quilboar and other foes drove them from their holy land. Cairne has united his people on Thunder Bluff, but the old tauren Auld Stonespire could not forgive as easily. Charlga Razorflank's heart was brought to him, and Auld says he can rest in peace now that the Razorfen know his people's pain."},{after=6522,articles={"An Unholy Alliance (Classic)"},text="On Charlga Razorflank's body was a small scroll, a letter from Ambassador Malcin offering her the Barrens and the lands beyond in return for a free hand in the area. Varimathras recognised the seal: Malcin was a human diplomat of Lordaeron before the plague, and now serves the Scourge. The Scourge is not content with one continent, and the Forsaken mean to act."}}},
[310]={articles={"Shadowfang Keep (Classic)"},text="Shadowfang Keep looms over Pyrewood Village in southern Silverpine Forest. It was once Baron Silverlaine's keep, a bastion of the Alliance, until the Third War.\n\nThe wizards of Dalaran who fell to the Scourge rose again to fight for it, and against the advice of his peers the archmage Arugal summoned the feral worgen to bolster the Kirin Tor's ranks. The wolf-men slaughtered the Scourge, then turned on the wizards and overran the keep. Driven mad with guilt, Arugal is said to have adopted the worgen as his children and retreated into the keep, which is haunted by the baron's vengeful ghost.",chapters={{after=1098,articles={"Deathstalkers in Shadowfang"},text="The Forsaken are preparing to assault Shadowfang Keep, and sent two deathstalkers, Adamant and Vincent, to scout it. Adamant was found alive and is sending his report to High Executor Hadrec. Vincent lies dead in the courtyard, and whatever he learned died with him."},{after=1013,articles={"The Book of Ur (quest)"},text="Keeper Bel'dugur of the Royal Apothecary Society wanted the Book of Ur, the work of a Dalaran mage who studied other worlds before the Scourge came; it had come into Arugal's hands, and was shelved in the keep. The book is in the Apothecarium now. Ur's knowledge was great, Bel'dugur says, but his conscience held him back, a weakness the Forsaken cannot afford if they mean to survive, or to conquer."},{after=1014,articles={"Arugal Must Die"},text="The Forsaken could never hold Silverpine for the Dark Lady while Arugal lived, and Dalar Dawnweaver of the Sepulcher swore to unravel his magic once he was dead. Arugal is slain, and his head was brought to the Sepulcher. Silverpine Forest is free of the mad archmage at last."}}},
[317]={articles={"Stratholme"},text="Stratholme was the second city of the kingdom of Lordaeron, where the Order of the Silver Hand was founded. When the plague reached it in tainted grain, Prince Arthas purged its people, and it became the Scourge's capital in Lordaeron; it is burning still.\n\nThe ruined city is divided now. The Scarlet Crusade holds the Scarlet Bastion and its quarter under Grand Crusader Dathrohan, while the death knight Baron Rivendare rules the rest for the Scourge from its ziggurats and slaughterhouse. The Argent Dawn and others send adventurers in after both.",chapters={{after=5944,articles={"In Dreams","Scarlet Subterfuge"},text="Highlord Taelan Fordring of the Scarlet Crusade, son of the exiled paladin Tirion Fordring, had lived for decades as a puppet of the Grand Crusader. Shown proof of his past by a stranger disguised by Myranda the Hag's illusion, he broke with the Crusade and cut his way out of Hearthglen to be taken to his father. Grand Inquisitor Isillien caught them on the road and struck Taelan down, and Tirion arrived too late to save his son. Grieving, Tirion has ended his long idleness: he has taken his place as Highlord of a new Order of the Silver Hand."},{after=5282,articles={"The Restless Souls (2)"},text="Egan, a man who lives on his hatred for the Scourge but has no use for the Crusade's zealotry, built a blaster to free the tortured souls still wandering Stratholme's ransacked streets. The ghosts of its citizens have been released, and Egan wiped away a tear as he asked whether his own mother was among them."},{after=5213,articles={"The Active Agent","The Flesh Does Not Lie"},text="Betina Bigglezink of the Argent Dawn had believed the plague's potency was dwindling, but flesh from Stratholme's most recently infected showed the opposite: it is growing stronger, and its active agents have changed. Scourge data recovered from the city's ziggurats, where the Scourge draws great power, will take the Dawn months to dissect."},{after=5262,articles={"The Truth Comes Crashing Down","The Archivist"},text="Duke Nicholas Zverenhoff of the Argent Dawn sent a stranger to kill Archivist Galford and burn the Scarlet Crusade's archives. Beyond them, the Grand Crusader himself fell, and showed his true face: a dreadlord, Balnazzar, brother of Varimathras of the Undercity. Shown the demon's severed head, Zverenhoff wonders whether the whole Crusade has been an unwitting pawn of a greater evil all along."},{after=6163,articles={"Ramstein (quest)"},text="Nathanos Blightcaller once held his ground at Marris Stead in defence of Lordaeron, until a great wave of Scourge overwhelmed the farm and he woke as one of them, his mind joined to the Lich King's, until Sylvanas came for him. Freed, he hunted the abomination that had killed him across the Plaguelands for months, until it fled into Stratholme. Ramstein the Gorger is dead, and Nathanos means to hang its head on his wall."},{after=5125,articles={"Aurius' Reckoning","The Medallion of Faith"},text="Aurius, a paladin corrupted like so many of his brothers and sisters, sheltered in Alonsus Chapel, whose power alone kept him from becoming a death knight. With the Medallion of Faith, taken from Malor the Zealous in the Scarlet Bastion, he could leave the chapel to face the beast who helped corrupt him, Baron Rivendare. Aurius is dying, but his soul is saved."}}},
[435]={articles={"Scarlet Monastery"},text="The Scarlet Monastery stands in the northeast of Tirisfal Glades. It was once a proud seat of Lordaeron's priesthood, a place of learning with great libraries and a seminary for paladins, until the Scourge rose in the Third War.\n\nNow it is the stronghold of the Scarlet Crusade, fanatics who trust no one but their own: every outsider, they believe, may carry the plague, and must be destroyed. Scarlet Commander Mograine leads its devoted garrison, but its true master is said to be High Inquisitor Whitemane, a priestess who can raise fallen warriors to fight again.",chapters={{after=1053,articles={"In the Name of the Light"},text="Raleigh the Devout once served the Scarlet Crusade with pride, until he saw at the monastery that its grip on reality was slipping, and innocent men and women were tortured as \"plagued\" for not wearing its tabard. From Southshore he called for the deranged regime to be crushed. High Inquisitor Whitemane, Scarlet Commander Mograine, Herod the Scarlet Champion and Houndmaster Loksey are dead; Raleigh takes no joy in it, but many innocent lives have been spared."},{after=1048,articles={"Into The Scarlet Monastery (Classic)"},text="While the Dark Lady deals with the Lich King to the north, Varimathras sees to matters closer to home, and the Scarlet Crusade crawling over Tirisfal is the sharpest thorn in the Forsaken's side. He ordered High Inquisitor Whitemane and her top lieutenants destroyed. Whitemane, Scarlet Commander Mograine, Herod the Scarlet Champion and Houndmaster Loksey are dead, and the Forsaken will remember who did it."}}},
[476]={articles={"Scholomance"},text="Scholomance, the school of necromancy, lies in the crypts beneath Caer Darrow, the island keep of the noble Barov family in the Western Plaguelands. To keep their fortune and their lands past their own lifespans, the Barovs gave the keep to Kel'Thuzad and his Cult of the Damned, who turned on them and made it a school where acolytes learn the Scourge's craft. A disease born in its halls wiped out Caer Darrow, and its people still wander as ghosts.\n\nThe lich Ras Frostwhisper rules the site in the Scourge's name, and the necromancer Darkmaster Gandling serves as its headmaster.",chapters={{after=5344,articles={"The Last Barov (Alliance)"},text="Weldon Barov, one of the last of the house, shelters with the Alliance at Chillwind Camp. He wanted the family's deeds recovered from Scholomance, and then his brother Alexi, sheltering with the Horde at the Bulwark, dead, so that no one could ever contest his fortune. Alexi's head has been brought to him, and Weldon, delighted, counts the house's pockmark gone and his fortune secure."},{after=5342,articles={"The Last Barov (Horde)"},text="Alexi Barov, one of the last of the house, shelters with the Horde at the Bulwark. He wanted the family's deeds recovered from Scholomance, and then his brother Weldon, sheltering with the Alliance at Chillwind Camp, dead, so that no one could contest his claim to the fortune. Weldon's head has been brought to him, and Alexi, yawning, counts the power and fortune of house Barov his alone."},{after=5384,articles={"Kirtonos the Herald (Classic)"},text="Eva Sarkhoff, one of the ghosts of Caer Darrow, obtained the blood of innocents that Doctor Theolen Krastinov, the Butcher, used to appease his master. Placed on the brazier at Scholomance's porch, it brought Kirtonos the Herald, who cannot resist it, and Kirtonos is destroyed. One chapter of the school's horrible tale has closed, and Eva gave a piece of her own essence so that the lost souls of Caer Darrow can speak with their champion."},{after=5466,articles={"The Lich, Ras Frostwhisper (Classic)","The Human, Ras Frostwhisper"},text="The undead often destroy every remnant of their living years, but in the ruins of Stromgarde, his old home, a keepsake from Ras Frostwhisper's mortal life was found and bound into an artifact that could turn him mortal again. Confronted in his lair, the lich was made a man once more, struck down, and his human head taken. Ten thousand souls cry out together: the Scourge has been dealt a mortal blow."},{after=4771,articles={"Dawn's Gambit (quest)"},text="Betina Bigglezink of the Argent Dawn hid her latest device, Dawn's Gambit, in the container that once held plagued dragon eggs, to be placed in Scholomance's viewing room, where the undead mage Vectus leads a team of Scourge scholars. Vectus is defeated, though the device itself did not work quite as well as Betina had hoped."}}},
[499]={articles={"Deeprun Tram"},text="The Deeprun Tram is a gnomish railway running deep underground, and in part beneath a subterranean lake, between Ironforge and Stormwind City. King Magni Bronzebeard wanted a faster way to send aid and soldiers to the humans after the Second War, and High Tinker Gelbin Mekkatorque designed it; gnomes tend it every day, and travellers ride free.",chapters={{after=6662,articles={"Me Brother, Nipsy","Deeprun Rat Roundup","Monty"},text="Rats have plagued the tram's tunnels since it was built, and at the Ironforge station a gnome named Monty keeps them in check, luring them with a piper's flute and talking like a pirate. He sends the live rats to his brother Nipsy at the Stormwind depot, who turns them into kabobs. Nipsy sighs that Monty, who has never seen an ocean, has not been the same since Gnomeregan fell."}}},
[1411]={articles={"Durotar"},text="Durotar, named after Thrall's father Durotan, lies on the eastern coast of Kalimdor, between the Barrens and Azshara. Its land is rocky, red and cracked, hot and dry, much like the orcs' lost homeland of Draenor, and dangerous creatures shelter in its crags and canyons.\n\nYoung orcs prove themselves in the Valley of Trials before facing the world beyond. Since the orcs founded Orgrimmar, Durotar has become a home for all the Horde's races, above all the Darkspear trolls of the Echo Isles.",chapters={{after=794,articles={"Vile Familiars (Classic)","Burning Blade Medallion (Classic)"},text="The trials of the Valley were meant to be the first test of a young warrior, but a taint has crept in. The Burning Blade, a cult that worships demons, has made a coven in a cave in the valley's hills, and its vile familiars spilled out to cause havoc. The medallion of power hidden in the coven is out of the cult's hands now, but Zureetha Fargaze fears the Burning Blade has greater aims in Durotar."},{after=784,articles={"Vanquish the Betrayers"},text="Years ago, Admiral Proudmoore led the humans of Kul Tiras onto Durotar's shores, breaking the pact Thrall had made with Jaina Proudmoore to defeat Archimonde. The Horde threw them back and Tiragarde Keep fell, but the Admiral's reserves under Lieutenant Benedict retook its ruins. Benedict is dead now, and many of his sailors and marines with him."},{after=831,articles={"The Admiral's Orders","The Admiral's Orders (2)"},text="A weathered envelope sealed with Admiral Proudmoore's crest held his orders. The Admiral is dead, but he laid his plans before he fell, and Gar'Thok of Razor Hill fears that more of his men will land on Durotar's shores. Vol'jin, who long urged Thrall not to trust the humans, has taken the orders to the Warchief himself."},{after=786,articles={"Thwarting Kolkar Aggression"},text="Over the ridge west of Razor Hill, the Kolkar centaurs of Kolkar Crag were planning a three-pronged attack on the orcs and trolls of Durotar, its plans divided among three of their leaders. Those plans are destroyed and the invasion stopped before it began, though Lar Prowltusk is sure there will be blood spilled before these troubled times are through."},{after=826,articles={"Report to Sen'jin Village (Classic)","Zalazane (quest)"},text="The Darkspear trolls are the orcs' honourable friends, but they have troubles of their own. The Echo Isles off the coast were once their home, until the witch doctor Zalazane took them; from there he sent his trolls to the mainland to hex the Darkspear and drag them under his sway. Zalazane is dead, his head brought to Master Gadrin, and Sen'jin Village can sleep in peace again."},{after=99048,articles={"A Missing Hand"},text="Orgnil Soulscar had a scout of the Shattered Hand, a band of subtler fighters, watching Tiragarde Keep from a distance, until her reports stopped. Heglan Shadeeye lies dead in the wreckage of her hut north of the keep, and whatever killed her was not human."},{after=829,articles={"Dark Storms (quest)","Margoz (Classic)","Skull Rock (Classic)","Neeru Fireblade (quest)"},text="The Burning Blade has spread beyond the Valley of Trials. The goblin warlock Fizzle Darkstorm led its cultists in Thunder Ridge, and a larger band holds Skull Rock, a cave just outside Orgrimmar, where they burn their own flesh with searing collars to attune themselves to demonic power. The shaman Margoz couldn't read the collars' secrets, so one has gone to Neeru Fireblade, a warlock of Orgrimmar's Cleft of Shadow who claims he uses his arts only against demons. True or not, the Horde may need his help."},{after=924,articles={"Ak'Zeloth (Classic)","The Demon Seed (quest)"},text="The Burning Blade rallies around the Demon Seed, a tool of the Burning Legion kept in a cave atop Dreadmist Peak in the Barrens. Neeru wanted it destroyed, which his assistant Ak'Zeloth thought strange, and with one of Ak'Zeloth's power stones it is gone. But its destruction sent waves of staggering power through the ether, and Ak'Zeloth is sure that others felt them, and will wonder who broke their toy."}}},
[1412]={articles={"Mulgore","Mulgore (Classic)","Earthen Echo"},text="The green plains of Mulgore, home of the tauren, lie in the foothills of the Stonetalon Mountains, walled in by mountains on every side. Thunder Bluff towers over the northern plains, Bloodhoof Village stands at their heart, and young tauren train at Camp Narache on Red Cloud Mesa. On the Skywatcher Plateau in the northwest, Muln Earthfury and the elders of the Earthen Ring council study the relics brought to them from Kalimdor's buried ruins.\n\nFor all its peace, Mulgore has troubles: quilboar overrun Brambleblade Ravine and the sacred Red Rocks, the goblins of the Venture Company mine the eastern mountains, dwarves dig at the Bael'dun Digsite, Windfury harpies claim the far north, and Palemane gnolls camp in the south.",chapters={{after=757,articles={"Rites of the Earthmother","Rite of Strength (Classic)"},text="A young tauren earns the respect of Thunder Bluff's elders through the three Rites of the Earthmother: Strength, Vision and Wisdom. The first is a test of bravery against the tribe's enemies, the Bristleback quilboar of Brambleblade Ravine, who ambush hunting parties and steal from Camp Narache by night. With their belts brought to Chief Hawkwind, the first rite is passed."},{after=3376,articles={"Break Sharptusk!"},text="The tauren carved their home from this land at a cost, and the Bristlebacks' war on them has been long. Their chief, Sharptusk Thornmantle, led it from a makeshift village in Brambleblade Ravine. His head has gone to Brave Windfeather, a warning to all who would threaten the tauren's home."},{after=746,articles={"Mulgore (Classic)","Dwarven Digging"},text="Dwarves of the Alliance have opened the Bael'dun Digsite in the mountains of Mulgore, searching for traces of their own ancestry. Baine Bloodhoof agrees that secrets lie in the earth, but hollowing and defiling the land is no way to earn its teachings. Their tools are smashed now; the beasts of Mulgore may calm, and, spirits willing, the dwarves may learn."},{after=760,articles={"Poison Water (Classic)","Wildmane Cleansing (Classic)"},text="Goblins and their servants poisoned Mulgore's three sacred wells, Winterhoof, Thunderhorn and Wildmane. One by one they have been cleansed with totems made from the gifts of the land itself, and beasts drink from them eagerly again. Bloodhoof Village is grateful, and hopes the waters of Mulgore stay clean forever."},{after=773,articles={"Rite of Vision (2)","Rite of Wisdom (Classic)"},text="The Rite of Vision is drunk, not fought: the Water of the Seers, brewed from well stones and ambercorn, brings a vision before the tribal fire that leads the seeker onward. The Ancestral Spirits of Red Rocks, the tauren who gave their lives to found and defend Thunder Bluff, bless only those who have drunk it, and with the Rite of Wisdom they pass the duty of protecting the city to the young. With all three rites passed, a young tauren may enter Thunder Bluff with pride."},{after=770,articles={"The Demon Scarred Cloak"},text="The great wolf Ghost Howl roamed Mulgore with a terrible wound in its hide, gained in its battles against the Burning Legion. It has been slain at last, to the astonishment of the old hunter Skorn Whitecloud, who hopes its spirit has finally found peace."},{after=765,articles={"The Ravaged Caravan (Horde) (1)","The Venture Co. (Classic)","Supervisor Fizsprocket (Classic)"},text="Tauren braves burned a Venture Company caravan on the shore of Stonebull Lake. Its crates held ore from a goblin mine in the eastern foothills, and papers laying out the company's plans to drive the tauren from their lands and plunder them freely. The mine has been sent a message the goblins can't ignore, and its supervisor, Fizsprocket, is dead; his papers may yet tell what the Venture Company means to do next."}}},
[1413]={articles={"The Barrens"},text="The Barrens is a vast, dry savanna in the heart of Kalimdor, held mostly by the Horde, with low mountains for its borders. Around the Crossroads in the north lie a handful of oases, fed by the waters of the Wailing Caverns, which carry a life energy that invigorates every plant and beast that drinks them.\n\nLong ago the Barrens was forest, tended by the night elves. The Burning Legion, war and the Great Sundering turned it into the barren expanse it is now, though there is more to it than dry soil and sand.",chapters={{after=880,articles={"The Barrens Oases","Altered Beings (Classic)"},text="The druids of Thunder Bluff sensed a strange power seeping into the Barrens, and sent Tonga Runetotem to the Crossroads to find its source. The new oases that have formed in recent years each hide a fissure, and the fissures seem to be what gives them their life. The snapjaws that drink from them have been changed by it: their shells told Tonga much, but raised more questions than they answered."},{after=850,articles={"Kolkar Leaders (Classic)"},text="The Kolkar centaurs have plagued the tauren for years, but they were never more than disorganised raiders here, until new leaders rose to rally them. Barak Kodobane, who camped west of the Forgotten Pools, was only one strand of a tangled thread: other Kolkar leaders still gather their warbands across the Barrens."},{after=4021,articles={"Counterattack! (Classic)"},text="The deaths of their leaders maddened the Kolkar rather than breaking them. A host came from their homeland in Desolace to take revenge, and threw itself against the Horde's bunkers west of the Crossroads. Regthar Deathgate's line held, their warlord Krom'zar fell, and word of the victory has gone to Thrall himself."},{after=876,articles={"Harpy Lieutenants (Classic)","Serena Bloodfeather (Classic)"},text="The Witchwing harpies of the Dry Hills have been raiding the Horde's caravans, and not for plunder alone. Their leader, Serena Bloodfeather, was avenging her sister Bloodfeather, slain by Rexxar years ago. Serena is dead now too, and Darsok Swiftdagger of the Crossroads meant to send her head out on the next caravan, as a warning to the rest of her kind."},{after=898,articles={"Free From the Hold"},text="On the coast, Theramore's soldiers hold Northwatch Hold, and they treat everyone they meet as an enemy of the Alliance. They sank a moonshine transport sailing between Ratchet and Booty Bay, and threw its only survivor, a deckhand named Gilthares Firebough, into a cell. He broke out at last and made it home to Ratchet, where his friends had long given him up for dead."},{after=95508,articles={"Trouble in the Valley","Unwelcome Guests (Barrens)"},text="The hermit tanner on the ridge above the Dry Hills is Walton, once a sailor of the Kul Tiras navy. When Lord Admiral Proudmoore took Theramore and began his campaign in Durotar, Walton objected to its methods, above all the execution of civilians, and deserted. Kul Tiran marines have come to the Barrens hunting for him, and with a little help, he lived through their visit; but his past has caught up with him at last."},{after=906,articles={"Tribes at War","Betrayal from Within (1)","Betrayal from Within (2)"},text="Mangletooth, a Razormane quilboar caged at Camp Taurajo, aided his tribe even from behind bars, and they left him there to rot. So he turned on them. Blessing the Horde with the power of Agamaggan, he had them weaken the rival Bristlebacks, then hunt down the three who came out of Razorfen Kraul to lead the raids on the Crossroads: Nak, Kuz and their leader, Lok Orcbane. Thork, who had blamed another quilboar for the raids, was astonished to be handed Lok's skull."},{after=849,articles={"Gann's Reclamation","Revenge of Gann (2)"},text="The dwarves of Bael Modan have blasted and torn at the land where Gann Stonespire's tribe once lived, killing many tauren and driving the rest from their birthplace, and every attempt at talks failed. Gann, who now wanders those roads alone, struck back: Prospector Khazgorm was killed, and the dig's flying machine was blown apart with explosives made from the dwarves' own supplies. The keep at Bael Modan still stands, built to guard the dig and now ready for war."},{after=97250,articles={"Wrongly Blamed, Justly Corrected"},text="Tension is rising along the eastern coast. Alliance soldiers have crossed into the Barrens at the border with Dustwallow Marsh, toppling the landmarks the tauren pathfinders left behind and blaming the Horde for a tavern burned to the ground. Camp Taurajo drove them back, though even its grunts doubt the Horde had a hand in the fire: too many of its own people rely on that road."}}},
[1416]={articles={"Alterac Mountains (Classic)","Syndicate","Dalaran"},text="The Alterac Mountains are high, rain-soaked peaks north of the Hillsbrad Foothills, once home to the human kingdom of Alterac and the city-state of Dalaran. Alterac's lords betrayed the Alliance in the Second War, and their kingdom never recovered: Crushridge ogres have claimed the ruins of Alterac City, and the Syndicate, a band of cutthroats led by Alterac's fallen nobles, hold the ruins of Strahnbrad and the Uplands.\n\nOn the western edge of the mountains, beside Lordamere Lake, the mages of Dalaran have opened the great magical dome they raised over their city after Archimonde razed it, revealing a city under repair. The Kirin Tor guard it jealously from Alliance and Horde alike. Deeper in the mountains, the Stormpike dwarves and the Frostwolf orcs fight over Alterac Valley.",chapters={{after=508,articles={"Taretha's Gift","Blackmoore's Legacy","Gol'dir (quest)","Infiltration (Classic)"},text="The orc Gol'dir was long held captive by the Syndicate, moved from camp to camp, until papers taken from them led the Horde to him in the Alterac Mountains. Freed, he told what he had learned: the Syndicate's leader, Lord Aliden Perenolde, meant to make slaves of the orcs, as his father and his mentor, the hated Blackmoore, once had. Perenolde is dead now, and his frightened mistress Elysa gave up the pendant around her neck, a keepsake of Taretha Foxton, Thrall's friend from his childhood in Durnholde, which the Warchief wanted back."},{after=537,articles={"Dark Council","Further Mysteries","Letter to Stormpike","Encrypted Letter (Hillsbrad Foothills)"},text="An encrypted letter taken from the Syndicate's camps in the foothills could be read by no one in Southshore, but Prospector Stormpike of Ironforge cracked it. Its writer called himself Nagaz, a member of something called the Argus Wake, which had taken up with the Syndicate. Nagaz and his shadow mages have been hunted down in the Uplands, but Magistrate Maleb takes little comfort: Nagaz was only the servant of another, and the Argus Wake may run deep through the continent, or even the world."},{after=523,articles={"Baron's Demise","Assassin's Contract (quest)"},text="Among the Syndicate's papers was a contract for the murder of Southshore's own Magistrate Henry Maleb, sealed in red wax with a distinctive signet ring. It was signed by Baron Vardus, a noble of the old kingdom of Alterac known for his cold heart and brutal ways. The Baron has been found among the Syndicate's haunts in the Uplands, and he will sign no more contracts."},{after=521,articles={"The Crown of Will (5)","The Crown of Will (4)","The Crown of Will"},text="Lady Sylvanas sent the ogre Mug'thol and his Crushridge warband, bound to her service, into Alterac to retrieve a relic called the Crown of Will. Instead of bringing it back, Mug'thol put it on, and the crown let him throw off her hold over him. His lieutenants are dead, Mug'thol's head has gone to Tarren Mill crown and all, and the Crown of Will is on its way to the Dark Lady."},{after=566,articles={"WANTED: Baron Vardus"},text="High Executor Darthalia of Tarren Mill put a price on the head of Baron Vardus, one of the Syndicate's highest-ranking nobles, for his crimes against the Forsaken. He was hunted down in the Uplands north of Tarren Mill, and his head has gone to Darthalia to claim the reward."}}},
[1417]={articles={"Arathi Highlands (Classic)"},text="The Arathi Highlands are a flat, craggy, grey land in southeastern Lordaeron, the cradle of human civilisation, where the Empire of Arathor was founded at the city of Strom. Its heir, the martial kingdom of Stromgarde, fought for the Alliance in the Second War. It escaped the plague, but was overrun soon after by the Syndicate and the Boulderfist ogres, and its surviving soldiers hold out at Refuge Pointe while Prince Galen Trollbane's militia fights a three-way guerrilla war in the ruins of Stromgarde.\n\nThe Horde holds Hammerfall in the east, once an internment camp for captured orcs. The Witherbark trolls haunt the southeast, and the Circle of Binding stones hold ancient secrets. To the south, the dwarven Thandol Span crosses into the Wetlands.",chapters={{after=681,articles={"Northfold Manor (Classic)"},text="Captain Nials of Refuge Pointe admits that Stromgarde is losing its war. The ogres, the Syndicate and Stromgarde's soldiers have carved their once great city between them in a bloody stalemate, and Stromgarde's numbers dwindle while its enemies grow. With the Syndicate's base at Northfold Manor thinned, more supplies may get through from Southshore, and Nials dares to hope the cause is not yet lost."},{after=697,articles={"Malin's Request (2)","Attack on the Tower (Classic)","Trelane's Defenses (Classic)","Worth Its Weight in Gold (Classic)"},text="Archmage Malin of Stormwind sent his apprentice Kryten and the dwarf Skuerto to Arathi to gather reagents from the Witherbark trolls, and to recover the belongings of Archmage Trelane from the Tower of Arathor in Stromgarde, now surrounded by Boulderfist ogres. One of the ogres, Kor'gresh Coldrage, had even begun to master Trelane's wand. With the tower's own defences woken, Trelane's phylactery, orb and ember agate have been taken back, and Malin was overjoyed to hear it."},{after=661,articles={"Hints of a New Plague? (5)","Hints of a New Plague? (3)","Hints of a New Plague?"},text="Phin Odelic of Southshore leads a band of adventurers looking into strange happenings: his cousin, a Hillsbrad farmer, had to put down his dog after it grew to an immense size and attacked anyone near. Phin suspected the undead, and two of his companions, Quae and Kinelory, went to watch Go'Shek Farm in Arathi. There, the Forsaken Apothecary Jorell had been running deadly experiments and sending his research to Tarren Mill; Kinelory has stolen what was left of it, and Phin has proof that the Forsaken are at work."},{after=656,articles={"Summoning the Princess","Theldurin the Lost (quest)","Myzrael's Allies (Alliance)","Crystal in the Mountains"},text="From crystal shards in the mountains, a voice called to passers-by: Myzrael, a princess of the earth, who said the giants had chained her beneath the highlands. Those who listened broke the Stones of Binding and the Keystone and set her free, but her friends in Ironforge and Hammerfall were cultists of the Twilight's Hammer, and her scroll was in the hands of Theldurin the Lost, a mad deserter from their order. Summoned too soon, Myzrael has been defeated and bound to her shards again; a wail rose from far below, and the shards now pulse with menace."},{after=847,articles={"Guile of the Raptor (3)","Raising Spirits (1)","Hammerfall (Classic)"},text="Gor'mul was a prisoner at Hammerfall when Thrall and Orgrim Doomhammer freed the camp, and he saw Doomhammer struck down from behind by a knight's lance. Left behind as the others escaped, he wandered without hope for years, until the Horde returned to make Hammerfall its own. The shaman Tor'gan, with charms made from raptor eyes, has shown him the new shamanic magic of Thrall's Horde, and Gor'mul has agreed, for now, to listen."},{after=680,articles={"The Real Threat (Classic)","Call to Arms (1)","The Hammer May Fall"},text="Hammerfall is under siege by the Boulderfist ogres and the Witherbark trolls, who have joined forces to drive the Horde from Arathi. Drum Fel, who commands the outpost, never knew why: his wife Korin learned through her auguries that the ogre Or'Kalar, Mug'thol's pawn in Stromgarde, hated Drum for defeating him years ago and had laid a trap for him. She hid the signs from her husband, and Or'Kalar is dead near the Tower of Arathor."},{after=646,articles={"Trol'kalar (2)","Trol'kalar (Classic)","Sigil of Trollbane (quest)","Trollbane (quest)"},text="With Thoras Trollbane dead and Stromgarde in ruins, Vol'jin wanted the legendary sword Trol'kalar taken from the humans for his trolls' war against Zul'Gurub. Thoras's tomb was warded by sigils scattered between the Syndicate and Stromgarde's defenders, and the last was held by his son, Prince Galen, who was slain for it. The tomb is open, and Trol'kalar, greatest weapon of the humans who ravaged the trolls for generations, is on its way to the Darkspear at Grom'gol."},{after=673,articles={"Foul Magics (2)","Foul Magics"},text="Tor'gan felt demons being summoned in the highlands, and found traces of it at Northfold Manor, along with spent bloodstone amulets. The Syndicate's ties to demonic magic led to the warlock Marez Cowl, who used a bloodstone orb to call up demons in great numbers inside the walls of Stromgarde. Marez is dead and her orb is in Tor'gan's hands, to be destroyed."},{after=662,articles={"Deep Sea Salvage","Land Ho!"},text="Captain Shakes O'Breen of Booty Bay's Blackwater Raiders led three ships into Faldir's Cove, sure the treasure he sought was there. The Harbinger docked safely, but as the Maiden's Folly and the Spirit of Silverpine came in, the tide fell strangely and both ran aground on a temple hidden in the reef, sinking almost at once. Their charts and logs have been brought up from the wrecks before prying eyes could find them, though the crew suspects they will be stranded in the cove for a long while."}}},
[1418]={articles={"Badlands (Classic)","Kargath"},text="The Badlands is a cracked desert of dry earth, scattered boulders and winding canyons in southeastern Khaz Modan, between Loch Modan to the north and the Searing Gorge to the west. Ironforge prospectors comb it for titan relics, drawn by Uldaman, the immense earthen ruin in the north that held the first clues to the dwarves' origins. The Stonevault troggs who woke there defend it savagely, and the Dark Iron dwarves have built a fortress just to its south.\n\nDustbelcher ogres camp across the wastes, and black dragons roost in Lethlor Ravine in the east. In the northwest the Horde holds Kargath, its only outpost in Khaz Modan, sent by Thrall to find a way through to Blackrock Mountain and the renegade Blackrock orcs of Rend Blackhand.",chapters={{after=739,articles={"Murdaloc (quest)"},text="Agmond, a dwarf of Prospector Ironband's from Loch Modan, never came back from the Badlands. His trail of blood ended where he died, with the name \"Murdaloc\" scratched into the sandstone beside his hand. The trogg who killed him is dead now, and Ironband, grieving, comforts himself that thick-skulled Agmond surely took plenty of troggs down with him."},{after=692,articles={"The Lost Fragments","Theldurin the Lost"},text="In the Scar of the Worldbreaker lives Theldurin the Lost, a half-mad dwarf who once belonged to the Twilight's Hammer. When he fled them he stole the Scroll of Myzrael, so the cult could not use it to draw the being Myzrael up to the surface, but enraged earth elementals stole it from him in turn and tore it to pieces. The three fragments are back in his hands, and he is patching the scroll together, fretting that Myzrael may already be free and only gathering her strength."},{after=737,articles={"Forbidden Knowledge (Classic)","Solution to Doom","Theldurin the Lost"},text="Theldurin is haunted by a doom stirring beneath the earth, and by beings whose names he dares not speak in case they hear him. The Tablet of Ryun'eh, hidden in Uldaman in a chest older than any dwarven work, told him much but not enough, so he sent a stranger bearing the sigil of the cult he fled to fool its hidden members into giving up Yagyin's Digest. With the book in hand, Theldurin rejoices that the world may yet be saved, unless the things below are set free, in which case nothing will save anyone."},{after=778,articles={"This Is Going to Be Hard (3)","Lotwil Veriatus"},text="The gnome mage Lotwil Veriatus has camped in the Badlands to perfect his shackles of elemental binding, a blend of alchemy, engineering and magic meant to let mages summon far mightier elementals. With his assistant Lucien Tosselwrench and a great deal of stone, coolant and liquid rock gathered from the wastes, the shackles were finished at last. The elemental Lotwil summoned with them promptly turned on everyone, and had to be beaten and sent back to its own plane; Lotwil cannot imagine what went wrong."},{after=762,articles={"An Ambassador of Evil","A Sign of Hope"},text="A note scrawled on a map told Prospector Ryedol that his partner Hammertoe Grez was alive, a prisoner of the Shadowforge Dark Irons in Uldaman, who wanted what he knew of the titan relics there to build stronger golems. Hammertoe was found mortally wounded, and his amulet, taken back from Magregan Deepshadow, carried the warning through Historian Karnik to King Magni's advisor Belgrum. Behind the digging stood Ambassador Infernus, sent by Ragnaros himself to oversee the Shadowforge at Angor Fortress. Infernus is dead, and Ironforge is sending help to reclaim the excavation."},{after=717,articles={"Tremors of the Earth (2)","Tremors of the Earth"},text="Garek came to Lethlor Ravine for his master, Krasus of the Kirin Tor, on an errand he would not explain. Beneath the ravine the black drakes Blacklash and Hematus lay imprisoned, and with black dragons nesting there and ogres stealing the Sign of the Earth, the key to their prison, Garek judged it could no longer hold them. So the seal was opened with the Sign and both drakes were slain, and Garek has gone to tell Krasus that the Kirin Tor need not worry over Lethlor's prisoners again."},{after=793,articles={"Broken Alliances (2)","Broken Alliances (1)"},text="In the Second War the Horde fought beside Deathwing, and two of his lieutenants, the drakes Blacklash and Hematus, were later imprisoned in Lethlor Ravine. Gorn of Kargath wanted those depraved old allies silenced to restore the Horde's honour, but Boss Tho'grun's ogre mercenaries, hired to fetch the Sign of the Earth that opens their prison, kept it for themselves. With the Sign won back and both drakes dead, Kargath can turn west, to the black dragons' allies in Blackrock Spire, the renegade Blackrock orcs."},{after=3821,articles={"Dreadmaul Rock (quest)","Kargath"},text="Three weeks ago, a platoon from Kargath went into the Burning Steppes to look into strange rituals among the Firegut ogres, and nothing has been heard from it since. Warlord Goretooth told Thal'trak to move on, but Thal'trak could not: his wife, Sha'ni, was with them. At the top of Dreadmaul Rock a stranger found only her ghost, mourning the life she and Thal'trak had planned with her parents in the Hinterlands once their tours were over."}}},
[1419]={articles={"Blasted Lands (Classic)","Nethergarde Keep"},text="The Blasted Lands were once the southern Black Morass, until the magic that brought the Dark Portal into being, and its destruction after the Second War, twisted them into a desert of red clay under constant lightning. The Portal through which the orcish Horde first came to Azeroth still stands in the south, silent. A remnant of the Burning Legion under Lord Kazzak lurks in the Tainted Scar, Dreadmaul ogres who came through the Portal roam the waste, and little else but basilisks, scorpions and hyenas can live here.\n\nOn the only road in, Nethergarde Keep keeps grim watch. Built at Archmage Khadgar's urging after the Second War and manned by the mages of Dalaran, it has depended on Stormwind since Dalaran fell, and its wizards study the taint the Portal left on the land.",chapters={{after=2522,articles={"Kum'isha's Endeavors (Classic)","To Serve Kum'isha","Kum'isha the Collector"},text="Near the Rise of the Defiler, the Lost One Kum'isha the Collector sneers that every race of Azeroth reeks of the same greed, yet pays richly for flawless spheres of draenethyst, the rarest of crystals. With their power he hopes to open a rift and lead his people back to their homeland. The first sphere let him try, and his conjuration failed; now a second is pulsing in his hands, and he dares to hope again."},{after=2783,articles={"Petty Squabbles"},text="Ambassador Ardalan of Nethergarde sees the folly of fighting the Horde over scorched earth while the Legion's power in these lands keeps growing. He sent Alliance adventurers to the ghost by the swamp road, the Fallen Hero of the Horde, who knows the truth of the Blasted Lands. The hero never thought he would see the day the Alliance and the Horde fought side by side against a common enemy, but agreed that they would unite."},{after=2743,articles={"The Cover of Darkness","Kirith (quest)","Kirith the Damned"},text="The Fallen Hero, Trebor, still had one man unaccounted for: his lieutenant Kirith, tortured beside him by Archmage Allistarj until his screams fell silent. In the Serpent's Coil his twisted soul was found in the shape of a great felhound, and killing it set his spirit free. Kirith told what the demon lord's cunning had made necessary: to summon Razelikh, they would need his amulet, whose three pieces his triad of servants wore around their necks."},{after=3628,articles={"You Are Rakh'likh, Demon (Classic)","Razelikh the Defiler"},text="Razelikh the Defiler, a dreadlord who had been Lord Kazzak's most fearsome lieutenant, made his lair atop the highest mountain in the Blasted Lands. With the amulet made whole, adventurers passed the teleport runes, called him forth at his altar and slew him, casting him back into the Twisting Nether. His severed horn went to the Fallen Hero as proof that heroes still exist in a war-torn world, and the ward that summoned him was destroyed so it can never fall into the wrong hands."}}},
[1420]={articles={"Tirisfal Glades","Bandarion Keep"},text="Tirisfal Glades, \"Tyr's Fall\", is an eerie, wooded region on the northern coast of Lordaeron, named for the Titan Keeper Tyr. It is the land of the Forsaken, soaked through with the Lich King's plague: its sky is a gloomy, blighted green, and its trees cling to their last shreds of life.\n\nThe Forsaken share it with old enemies: the Scarlet Crusade, whose Scarlet Monastery stands in the glades, and the Scourge, who haunt its derelict farms and mills. In the west, on the bluffs by the Whispering Forest, a new order of Forsaken paladins holds Bandarion Keep.",chapters={{after=364,articles={"Rude Awakening","The Mindless Ones"},text="The Forsaken rose in the Lich King's plague, but their new leader, Lady Sylvanas, the Dark Lady, freed them from his control. Now they wage war on his Scourge, and on the holdouts of humanity who dog their every step. The Mindless Ones who overran the north of Deathknell were once their brothers and sisters, but they are the Lich King's slaves still and will never join the Forsaken, so they must be destroyed."},{after=6395,articles={"Marla's Last Wish"},text="Before the plague, Marla Fipps lived with her husband Samuel. When it came, Samuel rose with the Scourge, and Marla, spared undeath, died at the hands of her mindless husband. Her dying wish was to be buried with him, and now Samuel lies beside her in the first row of Deathknell's graveyard, where perhaps they have found peace together."},{after=383,articles={"The Scarlet Crusade (quest)","The Red Messenger","Vital Intelligence (Classic)"},text="The Scarlet Crusade hunts every undead, Forsaken or Scourge, and won't rest until all are destroyed. A messenger it sent from the Scarlet Monastery to Deathknell carried a map of its field commanders and agents in Tirisfal, and that map is now with Executor Zygand in Brill. With it, the Forsaken could kill the Crusade's leaders in the glades in one swift stroke."},{after=492,articles={"A New Plague","A New Plague (4)"},text="Lady Sylvanas has challenged the Royal Apothecary Society to brew a new plague, deadlier than any ailment on Azeroth, to bring Arthas's Scourge to ruin. Apothecary Johaan of Brill boiled darkhound blood, murloc scales and spider venom into his first concoction, and tested it on a captured dwarven mountaineer held in the cellar of the Gallows' End Tavern. The drink, Johaan says, holds a subtle hint of what the Dark Lady has planned for the rest of Azeroth."},{after=411,articles={"The Lich's Identity","Proving Allegiance","The Prodigal Lich Returns"},text="A free-willed lich on the island in Brightwater Lake set his minions on anyone who came near, believing every other undead a slave of the Scourge. His spellbook named him: Gunther Arcanus, a skilled necromancer in life and, before the plague, a comrade-in-arms of the Undercity mage Bethor Iceshard. The death of the Scourge agent Lillith Nefara proved to him that others had broken free of the Lich King, and through a nether gem he now speaks with his old friend: the Forsaken have won him over."},{after=372,articles={"At War With The Scarlet Crusade (Classic)","At War With The Scarlet Crusade (2)","At War With The Scarlet Crusade (3)","At War With The Scarlet Crusade (4)"},text="With the documents from Deathknell, the Deathguard knew where every Scarlet camp in Tirisfal lay, and one by one their commanders have fallen: Captain Perrine in the ruined tower southwest of Brill, Captain Vachon near the Balnir Farmstead, and the ruthless Captain Melrache at the watch post past Faol's Rest. Executor Zygand counts each death a step closer to defeating Arthas once and for all."},{after=96898,articles={"Remnants of War"},text="Not long ago, the necropolis Naxxramas bore down on the Undercity, meaning to wipe the Forsaken off the map. The Forsaken and the Argent Dawn drove it off, and both paid dearly for it. In the battle, the great necrotic crystal atop Naxxramas shattered, scattering its fragments across the glades, and the Cult of the Damned has come to gather them. Even spent, the shards are dangerous to touch."},{after=96899,articles={"Bandarion Keep (quest)","Bandarion Keep"},text="Bandarion Keep was once Dawn's Watch, where the Argent Dawn readied itself against the Scourge's last push into Tirisfal. When the Dawn withdrew to the Plaguelands it fell into disrepair, until Danitha Morr and her paladins took it over, restored it, and named it after a friend she shared with the Argent emissary Leonid Barthalomew."},{after=96896,articles={"A Righteous Cause","Bandarion Keep"},text="That friend was Garek Bandarion, perhaps the first Forsaken to wield the Light again, who fell defending the Undercity against Naxxramas. Leonid came on behalf of Lord Tyrosus to offer Danitha's order a place in the Argent Dawn, where its Light would be valued. She refused: the Forsaken are still the people her paladins swore to protect. Many Forsaken, remembering what the living did in the Light's name, mistrust paladins deeply, and that is why she means to stay."},{after=99153,articles={"As Above, So Below","The One That Got Away (Forever)"},text="Beneath Shadowvale, the ruined village southwest of Bandarion Keep, the cellars had been festering into a Scourge uprising that went unnoticed for far too long. One of the cultists gathering the Naxxramas fragments escaped there with a piece of the crystal that still had power in it, enough to raise the dead or make them stronger. The crystal has been found before things got out of hand, and the glowing bones taken from the cellars may show the paladins how the threat grew in secret."},{after=91317,articles={"The Tarnished"},text="Not every risen paladin has made peace with undeath. The Tarnished carried their old prejudices into it, and from the ruins of Shadowvale they turned their blades on their fellow Forsaken, each killing feeding the mistrust Danitha's order is trying to overcome. With their commander dead, the order's place in Tirisfal is a little surer, though Danitha expects it may take years, even decades, for the Forsaken to embrace them."},{after=408,articles={"Deaths in the Family (Classic)","Speak with Sevren (Classic)","The Family Crypt (Classic)"},text="The Agamands were once the most prosperous family in Tirisfal. When the Scourge came, they fortified their mills and persuaded their workers to stay and defend them, dooming them all to undeath. The mills became a Scourge foothold, and in the family crypt the skeletal Captain Dargol raised the Agamand ancestors to turn against the Forsaken. His skull is now in Magistrate Sevren's hands, a telling blow the Scourge won't fail to notice."},{after=99156,articles={"Rear Guard Patrol","Rear Guard Patrol (Forever)"},text="The Bulwark holds the line between Tirisfal and the Plaguelands, and it can only hold if no Scourge strike from behind or cut its supplies; the Balnir Farmstead had become a haven for those that slipped past. Then a rogue abomination, Riptear, was seen near Venomweb Vale, and abominations don't simply slip through the Bulwark unnoticed. Riptear is dead, but the Deathguard are keeping the matter quiet: something is wrong."}}},
[1421]={articles={"Silverpine Forest (Classic)"},text="Silverpine Forest is an ancient wood of tall, silver-barked pines along Lordaeron's rugged western coast, bordered by Tirisfal Glades to the north, Lordamere Lake to the east and the Greymane Wall of Gilneas to the south. The Lich King's armies burned its farms and slaughtered its people in the Third War, and the plague has left its trees pale and dying under a ghostly mist.\n\nThe Forsaken hold the north from the Sepulcher, where High Executor Hadrec commands, but their claim is fiercely disputed. Rot Hide gnolls raid from Fenris Isle, Scourge hold some of the ruined steadings, and the wizards of Dalaran send expeditions to protect the humans of Ambermill. In the southwest, the people of Pyrewood Village have fallen under the archmage Arugal's curse and turn into bloodthirsty worgen when night falls, while Arugal himself broods in the towers of Shadowfang Keep.",chapters={{after=425,articles={"Ivar the Foul (quest)","Lost Deathstalkers","Return to Quinn (Again)","Wild Eyes"},text="The Deathstalkers Rane and Quinn Yorick were sent to scout the farms of northern Silverpine and never reported back. They were found hiding at the Ivar Patch, where the ghoul Ivar the Foul had ambushed them and badly mauled Quinn. Apothecary Renferrel's first potion did little for him, and only a second brew, made from six whole hearts, set him on the mend. Ivar is dead now, and the brothers can finally carry their report to the Sepulcher."},{after=451,articles={"A Recipe For Death (3)","A Recipe For Death (2)","A Recipe For Death"},text="The Royal Apothecary Society is brewing a new plague to wipe out both the Scourge and the living, and Silverpine's beasts are among its ingredients. One of its most promising members, Apothecary Berard, was turned into one of Arugal's mindless servants at Pyrewood, but his journal survived: he had been studying an ancient plague that once killed off all the aquatic life in Lake Lordamere. With moss from the lake's creatures and a rare tumour from a Vile Fin murloc, his work will live on in the Undercity."},{after=530,articles={"A Husband's Revenge","The Engraved Ring","Raleigh and the Undercity","The Decrepit Ferry (quest)"},text="Among the Scourge's spoils at the Decrepit Ferry was a ring engraved for a woman named Deliah. Magistrate Sevren's records traced her to her husband Raleigh, now one of the Forsaken in the Undercity. Raleigh can no longer grieve, but he could still want revenge: Deliah was murdered by his old friend Valdred Moray, who fled to the Greymane Wall hoping to slip into Gilneas. Valdred is dead, and Raleigh keeps his hands as a reminder of the betrayal."},{after=442,articles={"Assault on Fenris Isle","Thule Ravenclaw (quest)","Rot Hide Origins","The Dead Fields (quest)"},text="The Rot Hide gnolls are no natural tribe. The ichor they carry was brewed with warlock and necromantic magic, and Bethor Iceshard of the Undercity knew whose: Thule Ravenclaw, a promising young mage before the war, who betrayed his brethren to join the Lich King and made the Rot Hides his servants. Thule held Fenris Isle as the Scourge's stronghold in Silverpine until the Forsaken stormed it. He is dead, and the energies released at his death were felt as far away as the Sepulcher."},{after=491,articles={"Resting in Pieces","The Hidden Niche","Wand to Bethor"},text="Thule's head servant, Alaric, was given an unlife he could not escape by a spell meant to protect him from the plague; Thule then beheaded him and threw his head to the gnolls as a toy. Reunited with his body, Alaric chose to rest in the keep where he had spent his best days, and revealed a hidden niche holding a wand. Long ago, Thule and two friends had woven it together from their apprentice wands as a symbol of friendship; Thule came to despise it but could not part with it. Now it has gone to Bethor Iceshard, one of those friends."},{after=480,articles={"The Weaver (quest)","Dalaran's Intentions","Dalar's Analysis","Border Crossings"},text="Dalaran's wizards have been shipping crates by the wagonload from Hillsbrad to Ambermill, and a pendant taken from one of them showed why. Ambermill sits on a dormant ley energy node that the Kirin Tor had marked long ago, and the wizards were reawakening it for some greater purpose. Their archmage, Ataeric, is dead and the ley energies have fallen dormant again, but Shadow Priest Allister knows the source of power lies close at hand, and that the wizards will be back."},{after=452,articles={"Pyrewood Ambush"},text="Arugal rules Pyrewood through its town council, puppets he uses to keep the cursed villagers under control while his attention is elsewhere in Shadowfang Keep. Deathstalker Faerleia lay in wait for the councillors at the village hall, and they are dead. Without them, she hopes the sons of Arugal will turn on their master, and the Forsaken can take the town and drive him out of the keep."},{after=98299,articles={"Stop the Spread (Forever)","Arugal's Folly (5)","Arugal's Folly (4)","Arugal's Folly"},text="Dalar Dawnweaver, a mage of the Forsaken, studied Arugal's spellbooks and the enchanted shackles his worgen wear, and has exposed him as a fraud: Arugal barely had the magic to bind the beasts, let alone create them, and his famous remedy is worthless. The curse spreads some other way, by tooth and claw, and it has reached the human refugees gathered by the Greymane Wall. The Forsaken cannot catch it, but the sickly refugees could become Arugal's servants, so they have been put down; for now, the Gilneans' refusal to open their wall keeps the curse out of their kingdom."},{after=95981,articles={"Watching the Roads"},text="Ever since the barrier around Dalaran came down, the Kirin Tor have kept a tight grip on the roads, and a steady stream of relics and pendants has crossed into Silverpine. The Forsaken have thinned the wizards and watchers patrolling around Ambermill to cut the supply off at its source. Why the mages are so wary, no one at the Sepulcher yet knows."},{after=95140,articles={"Old Fire-Eye (quest)","The Debt","Lumina Windsinger (quest)","A Moon-Kissed Blade","Diplomatic Incident"},text="Two dignitaries of the Earthen Ring vanished on the road through Silverpine: a tauren, Banon, and a sky-touched elf, the Skyborne Windshaper Lumina Windsinger. One of Danitha Morr's Forsaken paladins found Lumina caged by the Rot Hides in Fenris Keep and freed her. It was not the gnolls who had struck first, though: a great worgen had bitten Banon, and he turned on her before the gnolls cut him down. That beast, Old Fire-Eye, the biggest of his kind, never seemed to stay dead, until Ott, a humble orc smith of Tarren Mill, forged a moonsilver blade to bring him down. Old Fire-Eye is slain, and Banon may rest in peace."}}},
[1422]={articles={"Western Plaguelands (Classic)"},text="The Western Plaguelands were once the lush farmland east of Lordaeron's capital, where Andorhal shipped grain to the kingdom's northern provinces. Knowing the plague would spread fastest through that grain, the necromancer Kel'Thuzad and his Cult of the Damned tainted it and set four great plague cauldrons in the fields of the largest farmsteads. Now the land is grey and blighted under a smoky mist, its creatures sick and savage, and the undead hold Andorhal, Sorrow Hill and the cauldron fields.\n\nThe Scarlet Crusade strikes at the Scourge from Hearthglen in the north. The Alliance has a foothold at Chillwind Camp in the south, and the Forsaken hold the Bulwark on the border of Tirisfal, both aided by the Argent Dawn. On the island of Caer Darrow, in Darrowmere Lake, lies the Scourge's school of necromancy, the Scholomance, and the only place of peace in all the land is Uther's Tomb, a monument to the fallen Lightbringer.",chapters={{after=211,articles={"Alas, Andorhal (Alliance Classic)","All Along the Watchtowers (Alliance)","Clear the Way (Western Plaguelands)"},text="Commander Ashlam Valorfist of Chillwind Camp meant to strike Andorhal by its weakly guarded eastern road, through the undead swarming Sorrow Hill. With a path cleared, beacons were planted at the four watchtowers the Scourge used to direct its troops, and the summoning crystals inside them, which fed the city's master his power, were destroyed. That master was the lich Araj the Summoner, and Araj has fallen; a shard of his phylactery is proof for Valorfist."},{after=105,articles={"Alas, Andorhal (Horde Classic)","All Along the Watchtowers (Horde)","Scarlet Diversions"},text="High Executor Derrington of the Bulwark could not attack Andorhal with the Scarlet Crusade advancing from Hearthglen at his back, so he set his enemies against each other: the Crusade's command tent was burned and a Scourge banner planted in its place. With the Crusade's eyes turned on the Scourge, the Forsaken marked Andorhal's watchtowers and destroyed the crystals within that fed the lich Araj the Summoner, ruler of the city. Araj has fallen, and Derrington has a shard of his phylactery."},{after=5227,articles={"Gahrron's Withering Cauldron (Classic)","Felstone Field Cauldron","Dalson's Tears Cauldron","Writhing Haunt Cauldron"},text="The four plague cauldrons of Felstone Field, Dalson's Tears, the Writhing Haunt and Gahrron's Withering still spewed their toxins over the land, each guarded by a cauldron lord holding its key. One by one the lords have been slain, and through a small access panel in each cauldron's base, new reagents have been added to brew a counter-agent to the plague. The clouds they spew have changed colour, and the Scourge nearby recoil from them."},{after=5051,articles={"Two Halves Become One","Good Luck Charm (quest)","Better Late Than Never (Classic)"},text="The ghost of Janice Felstone still haunts her farmhouse, frantic to deliver a package before the plague takes her. The package, found in the barn by her horse, was meant for her sibling, who escaped the plague and now lives far away, in Stormwind or the Undercity. The sibling sent back half of a good luck charm whose other half belonged to Janice's husband John, and with the charm made whole again, perhaps Janice's spirit can rest."},{after=5060,articles={"Locked Away (2)","Locked Away","Mrs. Dalson's Diary (quest)"},text="The last entry in Mrs. Dalson's diary told how her husband Harold turned, and how she dragged him into the only place she could find that would hold him, hid the key, and meant to flee with the money in the family's cabinet, only to realise Harold kept the cabinet's key. Farmer Dalson's ghoulish remains have been put down, and his key opened the cabinet at last."},{after=5210,articles={"Brother Carlin","The Annals of Darrowshire","Auntie Marlene","Little Pamela (Classic)"},text="The spirit of Marlene Redpath wanders a hill near Andorhal, longing to know what became of her niece Pamela, whom she hid when the battle of Darrowshire began; Pamela's ghost still waits in Darrowshire for her father. Chromie, a strange gnome in Andorhal's ruined inn, read in the Annals of Darrowshire that Pamela's father, Captain Joseph Redpath, was corrupted in the battle, joined the Scourge and betrayed Darrowshire's defenders. Chromie believes that fate can be changed, and her magic has added new pages to the book, telling of Joseph's brother Carlin, who survived and is now at Light's Hope Chapel."},{after=4973,articles={"Counting Out Time (2)","Counting Out Time (1)","A Matter of Time"},text="Chromie has found that something, most likely the Scourge, has been tampering with time in Andorhal, and temporal parasites feeding on its altered flow infested the city's silos. Every watch in Andorhal, like the great clock on the ruined town hall, stopped at three o'clock, the moment the plague first choked the life out of the city. Chromie gathers the watches to keep the timeline in balance against whatever force is meddling with it."},{after=6025,articles={"Unfinished Business (3)","Unfinished Business (1)"},text="Kirsta Deepshadow was sent from Stormwind to scout the Scarlet Crusade's defences at Hearthglen, a town some in the Alliance believe has more than historical importance. Too hurt to finish the job herself, she had others thin the Crusade's camps by the pass and then climb to a high point inside Hearthglen to count its forces. With her report complete, Kirsta is heading home to Stormwind, or, as she nearly let slip, to Elling Trias."},{after=6186,articles={"The Blightcaller Cometh","The Eastern Plagues"},text="Flint Shadowmore of SI:7 at Chillwind Camp sent his agents to the Eastern Plaguelands to learn who the \"Blightcaller\" was, and none came back. Their insignias were found in the Plaguelands, and the truth left Flint pale: the Blightcaller is Nathanos Marris, a noble ranger lord respected by all, the only human ever allowed to train under the high elves, now the Dark Lady's champion. The news has gone to Highlord Bolvar Fordragon in Stormwind."}}},
[1423]={articles={"Eastern Plaguelands (Classic)","Argent Dawn"},text="The Eastern Plaguelands are the most ravaged part of fallen Lordaeron. Their ground is brown and sick, their trees twisted into giant mushrooms, and an orange mist hangs over farms and villages like Darrowshire and Corin's Crossing, wiped out in the Third War. Stratholme, once the kingdom's largest city, is now the Scourge's southern capital, and undead, necromancers and cultists prowl the land.\n\nThe Scarlet Crusade fights a fervent, hopeless war against the Scourge from Tyr's Hand and its chapel, and welcomes no outsiders. Amani forest trolls strike from the north to reclaim their ancient empire, and high elves keep a lodge at Quel'Lithien. Only the Argent Dawn, a neutral order that welcomes Alliance and Horde alike to its war on the Scourge, keeps a camp open to all, at Light's Hope Chapel, a small refuge of the Light amid the blight.",chapters={{after=5206,articles={"Marauders of Darrowshire (Classic)","Villains of Darrowshire (Classic)","Heroes of Darrowshire (Classic)"},text="Pamela Redpath's spirit still waits in her house in Darrowshire for her father. From the Annals of Darrowshire her uncle Carlin, at Light's Hope, sent strangers after the relics of the battle: the libram of the paladin Davil Lightfire, taken by the Scarlet Crusade to Hearthglen; his brother Joseph's cracked and discarded shield; the skull of the ghoul lord Horgus the Ravager; the shattered sword of the death knight Marduk the Black; and the skulls of Scourge champions who fought there. Chromie says these are the reagents of a powerful spell, and with it, Darrowshire might be saved."},{after=5721,articles={"The Battle of Darrowshire (quest)","Battle of Darrowshire","Return to Chromie"},text="Darrowshire's defenders, Lordaeron soldiers, Silver Hand paladins and a militia under Captain Joseph Redpath, held off the Scourge and even slew Horgus, until Marduk the Black twisted Redpath's spirit and the corrupted defenders slaughtered the village. With Chromie's bundle of relics laid in the town square, the spirits of Darrowshire rose and fought the battle again, and this time Davil Lightfire held on and Redpath the Corrupted was destroyed, freeing Joseph's spirit. He has whispered to Pamela that he is coming home, and she has made tea, with lots of sugar."},{after=5544,articles={"Carrion Grubbage","Demon Dogs","Blood Tinged Skies","Tirion Fordring"},text="An old man named Tirion Fordring lives alone in a cottage on the shore of the Thondroril River with his horse, Mirador. He welcomes travellers of any race, but asks them to earn their keep: thinning the plaguehounds and the plaguebats that strike from the sky, and bringing him the meat of carrion worms, the only beasts the plague has not touched, to see him through the winter. He has a warm meal and conversation for those who help, and blesses them in the name of the Light."},{after=6021,articles={"Zaeldarr the Outcast (Classic)"},text="As if the Plaguelands had not trouble enough, Light's Hope Chapel was plagued by thieves: dead troll thieves, led by the undead troll Zaeldarr the Outcast, who crept in by night to steal bodies from the pit below the chapel and carry them off to the Undercroft. Zaeldarr is dead, to the great relief of Caretaker Alen."},{after=5248,articles={"Tormented By the Past","Fragments of the Past (2)","Fragments of the Past (Classic)"},text="Aurora Skycaller, a high elf of the Quel'Lithien Lodge, recognised the shards brought from Lake Kel'Theril in Winterspring as the Crystal of Zin-Malor, an artifact that could contain and control great arcane forces, stolen long ago and shattered by its thieves' evil intent. With the sacred Highborne writings from the Temple of Zin-Malor in Azshara and water from the Dire Pool of Dire Maul, she has fused it whole again. It has gone back to the cursed Highborne spirits of Kel'Theril, though they cannot be consoled, and Aurora wonders whether the shattered elves will ever be one again."},{after=6024,articles={"Hameya's Plea"},text="A scroll left behind a crypt was written by Hameya, one of the Amani trolls bound for Zul'Mashar. He and his companions ate bad meat on the road and were infected, and he could feel the lich's voice urging him to reach Zul'Mashar and spread the plague among his people. Before his will left him he begged to be killed, and promised his treasure, buried in a chest nearby, to whoever did it. Hameya is dead, and his chest is open."},{after=6148,articles={"The Scarlet Oracle, Demetria","Nathanos' Ruse","The Ranger Lord's Behest","The Champion of the Banshee Queen"},text="Among the first of the Dark Lady's champions is Nathanos, now called the Blightcaller, who keeps the Marris Stead and kills every Scourge and Alliance soul he meets. He sent his agents to take back a registry of his mortal life from the high elves of Quel'Lithien, and at Varimathras's bidding laid a trap for the Scarlet Crusade: disguised by a rotten apple, a Forsaken agent carried a forged command into Tyr's Hand, luring out Demetria, the Oracle, chief advisor to the Grand Crusader. She has been ambushed and slain on the road to Stratholme."}}},
[1424]={articles={"Hillsbrad Foothills (Classic)"},text="The Hillsbrad Foothills are green, pastoral hills in southern Lordaeron, with fertile meadows and rich soil, the best preserved of all the old kingdom's lands. For all that, Hillsbrad is traditionally the most blood-soaked battlefield on Azeroth: the first battle between Alliance and Horde in the Second War was fought here, and in the Third War the Scourge wiped out the town of Tarren Mill.\n\nHumans loyal to the Alliance hold the coast from Southshore, under Magistrate Henry Maleb, with farms at Hillsbrad Fields, the Azurelode Mine, and a dwarven garrison at Dun Garok by Thoradin's Wall. Tarren Mill in the hills is now the southernmost stronghold of the Forsaken, who fight Southshore without pause. The Syndicate, outlaws led by Alterac's fallen nobles, hold the ruins of Durnholde Keep, where Thrall was once raised as a slave, while murlocs and naga raid the shores.",chapters={{after=542,articles={"Return to Milton","Preserving Knowledge","Southshore (quest)"},text="The old kingdom of Alterac fell after the Second War, and with it a great library. Stormwind's Royal Library sent word to Loremaster Dibbs in Southshore, the nearest town to its ruins, to save what he could. Ogres of the Crushridge had looted the city, but tomes were recovered from them, among them the Arm of Gri'lek, an ancient book of troll lore that Dibbs now studies. The rest have gone to Milton Sheaf in Stormwind, who could scarcely believe they had survived."},{after=563,articles={"Reassignment","Stormwind Ho!","Farren's Proof (3)","Down the Coast"},text="Lieutenant Farren Orinelle was transferred from the Stormwind city guard to Southshore, a posting he loathes, and has little stomach for the murlocs and naga Marshal Redpath keeps sending him after. With a stranger quietly doing his fighting on the Western and Eastern Strands, Farren's report on the naga has gone to his old mentor, Major Samuelson, now captain of the guard, in the hope that it earns him a transfer home before Redpath catches on."},{after=498,articles={"The Rescue"},text="The Warchief sent Krusk and a band of warriors to Durnholde Keep to recover something of his and to look into this so-called Syndicate. The humans took them by surprise, and only Krusk escaped. Drull and Tog'thar have been freed from their cells now, and what they saw is clear: the Syndicate meant to make slaves of the orcs again."},{after=550,articles={"Battle of Hillsbrad (7) (Classic)","Battle of Hillsbrad (6)","Battle of Hillsbrad (4)","Battle of Hillsbrad (quest)"},text="High Executor Darthalia, under the command of Varimathras, was ordered to break the humans of Hillsbrad until the apothecaries' new plague is ready. Her soldiers burned the northern farms and executed their owners, hunted down the peasants hiding in the orchards, killed the blacksmith arming the town, and slew Magistrate Burnside and his council and destroyed the Hillsbrad Proclamation. The miners of the Azurelode Mine and the dwarves of Dun Garok fell after them, and Darthalia's commendation for the campaign has gone to Varimathras himself."},{after=524,articles={"Elixir of Agony (5)","Elixir of Agony (4)","Elixir of Pain (2)","Elixir of Suffering (2)"},text="Apothecary Lydon of Tarren Mill is working towards the plague the Dark Lady has planned for humans and Scourge alike, and he tests each step. His Elixir of Suffering killed his own toad, Umpi, and his Elixir of Pain was fed to Stanley, the dog of Hillsbrad's Farmer Ray. His Elixir of Agony, finished with Master Apothecary Faranell's colloid of decay, went into a keg of stout stolen from the dwarves of Dun Garok, and the keg was left for the captured farmers held in Tarren Mill."},{after=553,articles={"Helcular's Revenge (2)","Helcular's Revenge"},text="The wizard Helcular had almost finished turning himself into a lich when a lynch mob from Southshore found his ritual lair in the hills, killed him, and buried him in the town's own cemetery. His apprentice, Novice Thaivand of Tarren Mill, swore revenge. Helcular's rod, taken from the yeti that overran his lair, has been charged at his three ceremonial flames and driven into his grave, and Helcular has risen again among the graves of Southshore."},{after=544,articles={"Prison Break In","Lordamere Internment Camp"},text="Four Forsaken deserted the Undercity a few months ago, breaking into a secure vault and stealing four bloodstone artifacts the apothecaries needed for their studies. They fled to Dalaran, whose wizards held them at the Lordamere Internment Camp, where Warden Belamoore was studying the stolen artifacts. Kegan Darkmar and the other deserters are dead, and the bloodstones are back in Forsaken hands."}}},
[1425]={articles={"Hinterlands (Classic)","Revantusk tribe"},text="The Hinterlands are a wooded, mountainous wilderness in eastern Lordaeron, once the heart of the forest trolls' Amani Empire, whose ruined temples still rise among the trees. In the west, the Wildhammer dwarves of Aerie Peak, proud and fiercely independent, raise and ride the gryphons they are famous for, and the high elves of Highvale keep watch from the Quel'Danil Lodge.\n\nThree tribes of forest trolls share the rest of the land and little else. The Witherbark hold the south around the altar of their spider god at Shadra'Alor; the Vilebranch, cannibals sworn to the blood god Hakkar, rule from the great temple city of Jintha'Alor; and the Revantusk, on the eastern coast, have pledged themselves to the Horde. In the north, green dragons guard the great tree of Seradane.",chapters={{after=4298,articles={"Becoming a Parent","Food for Baby"},text="Agnar Beastamer took in a sprite darter egg brought all the way from Feralas, and readied raw stalker flanks for the hatchling. It hatched sooner than he expected, and Agnar gave the egg to its rescuer so the little faerie dragon would bond with them as its parent."},{after=1469,articles={"Rhapsody's Tale","Rhapsody Shindigger (quest)"},text="Brohann Caskbelly of Stormwind went looking for a troll temple said to have sunk beneath the swamps, and the trail led to Rhapsody Shindigger, a brewer camped in the Hinterlands. Paid in a Kalimdor cocktail, Rhapsody admitted he had flown over the place with Brohann once, drunk a whole keg of stout, fallen off his mount into the water and found himself among the temple's trolls. He swam away fast, but kept a souvenir: an Atal'ai tablet fragment, proof that the temple lies down there indeed."},{after=2880,articles={"Troll Necklace Bounty"},text="The Wildhammer of Aerie Peak want it understood that they are not part of the Alliance. They judge strangers by deeds rather than words, and Fraggar Thundermantle accepts the tribal necklaces of the trolls that choke the hillsides as a step in the right direction for any outsider who wants their friendship."},{after=2994,articles={"Saving Sharpbeak","Return to the Hinterlands","Thadius Grimshade (quest)","Witherbark Cages"},text="Sharpbeak, the heir of Aerie Peak's mightiest gryphon Swiftwind, vanished while hunting with his parents, and the Wildhammer blamed the trolls. He was not in the Witherbark cages, nor at the Altar of Zul, so Gryphon Master Talonaxe sent his feather to Thadius Grimshade, a Wildhammer diviner in Nethergarde Keep. Thadius saw him caged atop Jintha'Alor, with the key held by the Vilebranch high priestess Hexx. Hexx is dead, and Swiftwind and Keenclaw are fussing over their child like new parents."},{after=2938,articles={"Venom to the Undercity","Summoning Shadra (Classic)","Consult Master Gadrin"},text="Apothecary Lydon of Tarren Mill wanted a spider venom stronger than any his broodguards gave. Master Gadrin of Sen'jin Village, who knows the Witherbark well, named its source: Shadra, the Witherbark's spider god, who could be called with his parchment at her altar in Shadra'Alor. Shadra has been summoned and slain, a drop of her venom killed nearly two dozen of Lydon's rabbits, and a sample has gone to Master Apothecary Faranell in the Undercity."},{after=2782,articles={"Rin'ji's Secret (quest)","Rin'ji is Trapped!"},text="Rin'ji, a Witherbark troll captured by the high elves of the Quel'Danil Lodge, begged a passing member of the Horde to help him escape, promising a secret hidden at the Overlook Cliffs. The secret turned out to be a stone tablet covered in Witherbark markings. It might be valuable, or it might be the trick of a desperate troll, so it has gone to Oran Snakewrithe, a scholar of the Witherbark in the Undercity."},{after=7843,articles={"The Final Message to the Wildhammer"},text="The Revantusk and the Wildhammer are bitter enemies. Otho Moji'ko sent warnings to Aerie Peak, and the last of them was a blood-drenched spear wrapped in gryphon feathers, driven into the ground by the well in the middle of town. Otho only wishes he could have seen the dwarves' faces."},{after=7847,articles={"Return to Primal Torntusk","Kidnapped Elder Torntusk!"},text="In a bold midnight raid, the Vilebranch carried off Elder Torntusk, mate of the Revantusk leader Primal Torntusk. Vile Priestess Hexx was preparing him atop Jintha'Alor as a sacrifice to Hakkar, the faceless blood god. He has been freed from his cage and is on his way home, and the Revantusk owe a great debt."},{after=7850,articles={"Dark Vessels (Classic)"},text="The Vilebranch fight with unnatural ferocity because of Vile Priestess Hexx's weavings: vessels of tainted blood throughout Jintha'Alor radiate Hakkar's magic, empowering the trolls and driving them mad. The vessels have been stolen from the temple, and Primal Torntusk means to cleanse them of the taint and loosen the blood god's grip on her enemies."}}},
[1426]={articles={"Dun Morogh"},text="Dun Morogh is a range of snowy, forested mountains in western Khaz Modan, the homeland of the dwarves and gnomes. Ironforge is carved into Ironforge Mountain, and Gnomeregan lies in the foothills of Chill Breeze Valley.\n\nIts peaks are the highest in the Eastern Kingdoms: their glaciers feed the rivers of Khaz Modan, and their rock gives the dwarves all the ore they could want. Mountains wall it in on every side, and dwarven troops watch the few passes in.",chapters={{after=170,articles={"A New Threat (Dun Morogh)"},text="Troggs have been bursting out of the ground all across the dwarves' lands, and Coldridge Valley is no exception: a few nights ago they overran the camp to the west. Balir Frosthammer's shorthanded people have pushed them back from the hills and the frozen lake for now. If Coldridge is any sign of what is happening elsewhere, Balir fears hard times ahead, and hopes the king and the Senate are acting."},{after=3361,articles={"A Refugee's Quandary (Classic)"},text="The gnomes drove the troggs out of Gnomeregan, but then something went horribly wrong, and their home is now irradiated. Its people fled in every direction and are scattered across Dun Morogh. Felix Whindlebolt reached Anvilmar with nothing, his belongings carried off to the Frostmane trolls' camps, until a stranger brought them back."},{after=218,articles={"The Stolen Journal","The Troll Cave"},text="With the Senate's hands full of troggs, it sent the brothers Grelin and Senir Whitebeard to judge the threat from the Frostmane trolls. In Coldridge Valley the trolls hold little more than the southern cave, and though a band of them stole Grelin's journal in the night, a few strong arms were enough to deal with them. With the journal back, Grelin judges the trolls here no great cause for worry, and readies his report for his brother."},{after=99161,articles={"Rime's Wrath (2)","Rime's Wrath","Finding Warmth"},text="Father Gavin, a priest living as a hermit in the mountains east of Kharanos, keeps his hearth open to any traveller. A few days ago a shelf of rock and ice broke from the peaks, and the slide stirred up the ice elementals, making roads that are dangerous enough already worse still. The first and largest of them, which Gavin called Avala, had been shifting the weather and setting off smaller slides; it is destroyed now. Gavin, no fighter any more, wishes the spirits could have been calmed instead, but hopes fewer lives will be lost on the road."},{after=412,articles={"Operation Recombobulation (Classic)"},text="Gnomeregan's disaster was the gnomes' own doing: their attempt to wipe out the troggs failed, and instead turned most of their people into mindless, vicious leper gnomes. In Kharanos, Razzle Sprysprocket and Ozzie are building a machine to reverse the change, the Recombobulator, and the cogs and gears taken from the lepers outside Gnomeregan have nearly finished it. Razzle is sure it will make the gnomish race as good as new."},{after=433,articles={"The Public Servant (Classic)"},text="When the dwarves dug deep into Gol'Bolar Quarry, troggs poured out of the earth, wrecking the equipment and driving the miners out. Senator Mehr Stonehallow, of the Senate and the Explorers' League, has taken this part of the infestation as his own charge. The troggs have been thinned: a start, he says, enough perhaps for mountaineers to move in and secure the quarry so the miners can go back to work."},{after=417,articles={"A Pilot's Revenge (Classic)","The Lost Pilot (Classic)"},text="Mori Hildelve, a siege engine pilot, broke his engine on a steep hill while searching for a rare ore needed for high-grade blastpowder, and went on alone on foot. His frozen, mauled body was found in the hills with his journal: the great ice claw bear Mangeclaw had savaged him, and later came back to finish him off. His last wish was revenge, and Mangeclaw is dead; the pilots of the Ironforge Siege Brigade will remember the deed."},{after=96391,articles={"Underground Map","Nip 'Em in the Bud","Farsen's Watch"},text="Earthseer Farsen, a visitor from Aerie Peak camped above Gol'Bolar Quarry, used his farsight to show how thick the Dark Iron dwarves have grown in Dun Morogh, and sent adventurers against their spies at Ironband's Compound. One carried a map of tunnels and underground highways, written in the Dark Irons' jagged runes, all meeting at a point beneath Ironforge itself. Farsen reads it as the plan for an incursion into the vaults under the city, and the spies as its scouts; if the dwarves hurry, they might beat the Dark Irons to their goal."}}},
[1427]={articles={"Searing Gorge (Classic)","Thorium Brotherhood"},text="The Searing Gorge is a scorched, mountainous corner of Khaz Modan between Loch Modan and Blackrock Mountain, once part of the Redridge range. It was blackened by the same catastrophe as the Burning Steppes: the summoning of Ragnaros, which destroyed the Dark Iron capital of Thaurissan. Looking for new land, the Dark Irons overran the dwarven garrisons here and drove them back to Loch Modan, which sealed the passage. Now they mine the great rift of the Cauldron and its Slag Pit with slave labour, among golems, fire elementals and the cultists of the Twilight's Hammer.\n\nOn the cliffs above the Cauldron stands Thorium Point, home of the Thorium Brotherhood, master craftsmen who broke from the Dark Iron clan and keep watch on their former kin. The Alliance gate from Loch Modan stays locked, while the Horde at Kargath has cut a narrow pass through the mountains from the Badlands.",chapters={{after=3201,articles={"At Last!","The Horn of the Beast","Searing Gorge"},text="The gate into the Gorge from Loch Modan is kept locked, and Mountaineer Pebblebitty would hand over the key only to someone who had killed Margol the Rager, a huge kodo that was the bane of every archaeologist in the Gorge. Pebblebitty, who once lost an axe head in that horn, called the trophy a fake, and only Curator Thorius of Ironforge's museum, who drew up a proof of deed, could change her mind. She gave up the key grudgingly, and washes her hands of whatever happens beyond the gate."},{after=3566,articles={"Rise, Obsidion!","Dwarven Justice","Release Them"},text="The archaeologist Dorius, Curator Thorius's brother, seemed to die on the road home from the Gorge, and Thorius sent rescuers to free his dig team, whom the power of the Suntara stones kept toiling even after death. Laying a Twilight's Hammer relic on the altar of Suntara sprang a trap. Dorius had found the stones, given himself to Ragnaros as Lathoric the Black, and used his brother's grief to recover the last stone and raise the golem Obsidion against the Blackrock orcs. Lathoric and Obsidion are destroyed, and Thorius fears that if his noble brother could be turned so easily, no one is safe."},{after=3378,articles={"Prayer to Elune (2)","Prayer to Elune (Classic)","Zamael Lunthistle"},text="The ghost of the night elf Zamael Lunthistle lingers in a cage in the Gorge. As a youth in Darkshore he fell in with the Twilight's Hammer and forsook Elune, until a vision from the goddess woke him, and in repentance he began a prayer on a tablet of pure gold. The cult found it, branded him a blasphemer and executed him before it was finished. His prayer has been taken from the cultists to Astarii Starseeker in Darnassus, and a lost soul has been returned to Elune."},{after=3463,articles={"Set Them Ablaze! (Classic)","Divine Retribution","Kalaran"},text="Near Thorium Point, Kalaran Windblade, with only his lumbering squire Maltrake beside him, swore vengeance on the Dark Irons of the Cauldron for killing his father, since Stormwind's magistrates ignored his pleas. With hearts of flame, golem oil, thorium daggers and a symbol of Ragnaros he made a Torch of Retribution, and the four Dark Iron sentry towers were set ablaze with a fire no force can put out. The tale was a lie: Kalaran revealed himself as a drake of the black dragonflight, laughed that his plan had worked perfectly, and flew off to Blackrock Mountain, leaving his servant to pay his helpers in trinkets."},{after=7722,articles={"What the Flux?","Thorium Brotherhood"},text="When Overseer Maltorius deserted the Thorium Brotherhood for the Dark Irons, he took with him the secret recipe for its fiery flux, most likely the price of his new rank. Master Smith Burninate's plans have been taken back from the depths of the Slag Pit, and the Brotherhood can get down to business. It rewards those who help it against its former kin with its trust and, in time, with the fabled recipes of its smiths."}}},
[1428]={articles={"Burning Steppes (Classic)","Morgan's Vigil"},text="The Burning Steppes were once part of the Redridge Mountains, until the Dark Iron sorcerer-thane Thaurissan, seeking elemental minions to fight Ironforge and the Wildhammers, summoned Ragnaros the Firelord instead. The blast melted mountains and raised Blackrock Mountain, a volcano that still erupts over charred earth and rivers of lava. The Ruins of Thaurissan, the old Dark Iron capital, stand in the midst of the steppes, and black dragonkin roam the land.\n\nAround the mountain live the Blackrock orcs, savage castoffs of the Second War who keep none of Thrall's truce, and their allies the Firegut ogres of Dreadmaul Rock. Stormwind has all but abandoned the region; the Alliance clings to the burned village of Morgan's Vigil in the southeast, and the Horde reaches it from the north, through Blackrock Mountain.",chapters={{after=3825,articles={"Ogre Head On A Stick = Party","Oralius"},text="At Morgan's Vigil the dwarf Oralius, shell-shocked and forever confiding in an imaginary pirate friend he calls Captain Winky, ordered a strike against the Firegut ogres, who guard the mineral-rich Dreadmaul Rock for the Blackrock orcs and keep their supply lines open. Their brute lord Gor'tesh is dead, and his head on a pike now stands at the top of Dreadmaul Rock, sending the ogres running for the hills. Oralius is already dreaming of the medal he will get for it."},{after=3822,articles={"Krom'Grul (quest)","Krom'Grul","Sha'ni Proudtusk"},text="The Firegut captured Sha'ni Proudtusk's whole platoon and killed every one of them but her, because the ogre mage Krom'Grul wanted her for a ritual. She died chained to his altar atop Dreadmaul Rock, and he took her wedding nose-ring for his own finger. Krom'Grul is dead and the ring has gone back to Thal'trak at Kargath, but he wants nothing to do with it: this was to have been her last mission before they moved to the Hinterlands."},{after=4224,articles={"The True Masters (6)","The True Masters","The True Masters (3)"},text="At Morgan's Vigil, Helendis Riverhorn found that the Blackrock orcs are not savages acting alone: the black dragonflight controls them and feeds their rage. Magistrate Solomon knew Lakeshire could never stand against both, but Highlord Bolvar Fordragon, advised by the learned Lady Prestor, would send no troops on speculation, and named a stranger an acting deputy instead. Back at the Vigil, Marshal Maxwell only laughed: others had come before, and all the Vigil knew of the Blackrock was lost with Marshal Windsor, ambushed inside Blackrock Mountain. Ragged John, who escaped that ambush, swears that Windsor was dragged off alive to a prison."},{after=4241,articles={"Marshal Windsor (quest)","Reginald Windsor"},text="Maxwell sent his new deputy into Blackrock Depths to bring Windsor back, or proof that he was dead. Marshal Reginald Windsor is alive, locked in a cell in the Dark Irons' prison and in a towering rage, but for now he cannot be set free."},{after=4808,articles={"Felnok Steelspring (quest)","Broodling Essence (quest)"},text="At Flame Crest the goblin Tinkee Steamboil has spent weeks studying the dragonkin of the steppes, and her Draco-Incarcinatrix 900 works: broodlings struck by it release their essence when slain. What she learned from it suggests that dragon whelp eggs could be frozen and kept, and Felnok Steelspring of Everlook has agreed to build the parts for the device she needs. What anyone would want with frozen dragon eggs, she does not say."}}},
[1429]={articles={"Elwynn Forest"},text="Elwynn Forest is a vast woodland of stately hardwoods, the heartland of the human kingdom of Stormwind and where every human adventurer begins. Stormwind City lies to the northwest, the Redridge Mountains to the east, and Duskwood to the south, across the river.\n\nMuch of the forest burned during the First War, but its trees and glades have grown back. Marshal McBride keeps the peace in Northshire Valley, and Marshal Dughan in Goldshire.",chapters={{after=21,articles={"Skirmish at Echo Ridge","Investigate Echo Ridge","Kobold Camp Cleanup"},text="Kobolds have crept into Northshire from the woods to the north, and a band of their workers has dug into the Echo Ridge Mine. One by one they are thieves and cowards, but in numbers they are a real threat, and Marshal McBride wanted them purged before they dug in deeper. The mine is clearer now, though McBride admits there may still be kobolds in its tunnels."},{after=6,articles={"Bounty on Garrick Padfoot","Brotherhood of Thieves"},text="A new gang of thieves has appeared across the river east of the abbey, calling themselves the Defias Brotherhood and wearing bandanas. One of them, the cutthroat Garrick Padfoot, gathered a band of thugs at a shack by the vineyards and preyed on Northshire's farmers and merchants for weeks. Garrick is dead and his bounty paid, but what the Brotherhood itself is after, no one in the valley can say."},{after=87,articles={"Goldtooth (quest)","Lost Necklace","Pie for Billy","Back to Billy"},text="Old Bernice Stonefield, a widow, lost her necklace and blamed young Billy Maclure from the neighbouring farm. Bribed with a pie baked from boar meat, Billy let slip that he had dropped it near the Fargodeep Mine, where a big, gold-toothed kobold snatched it up and carried it off to his den. The necklace is home again, and Bernice gave away her late husband's lucky keepsake, which he had forgotten on his last campaign."},{after=114,articles={"The Escape (Alliance)","Young Lovers","Speak with Gramma","Note to William","Collecting Kelp"},text="Maybell Maclure and Tommy Joe Stonefield are in love, but their families are bitter enemies and keep them apart. Tommy Joe's grandmother saw no future for the pair while the feud lasts, but wanted to steal them a little time together, so William Pestle at the Lion's Pride Inn brewed an invisibility liquor from crystal kelp taken from the murlocs of Crystal Lake. Maybell feels guilty deceiving her family, but she means to drink it at the first chance and slip away to Tommy Joe."},{after=39,articles={"Deliver Thomas' Report","Report to Thomas","Discover Rolf's Fate","Find the Lost Guards","Further Concerns","A Fishy Peril"},text="Murlocs have swum up the streams of eastern Elwynn, but Marshal Dughan, busy with gnolls and bandits, wanted a soldier's report before he would believe they were a danger. Guard Thomas, posted at the eastern bridge, had sent two footmen up the river, and both are dead: Malakai Stone on the riverbank, and Rolf Hartford in a murloc village on the shore of Stone Cairn Lake. Dughan is convinced now, but his defences were stretched thin before he lost them, and he fears there will be fighting in Goldshire within the week."},{after=147,articles={"Manhunt (Classic)","The Collector (Classic)"},text="A pickup schedule taken from the Defias showed that someone known only as the Collector was receiving shipments of gold from Elwynn's mines, from a hideout at the Brackwell Pumpkin Patch. The Collector is dead, and on him was a membership ring of Stormwind's old Stonemason's Guild. Why a common thief would wear an artisan guild's ring, and why the Defias are stealing gold from the kingdom's mines, Marshal Dughan cannot yet say."},{after=176,articles={"Wanted: \"Hogger\"","Westbrook Garrison Needs Help!"},text="Gnolls and thieves are pressing on Elwynn's western border, and Westbrook Garrison has asked Goldshire for soldiers the army cannot spare. The worst of the gnolls was Hogger, a huge brute who prowled the southwestern woods and overpowered every attempt to capture him, until the Stormwind Army put a bounty on his head. Hogger is dead, and Marshal Dughan, who had begun to think no one could do it, paid out the reward with relief."}}},
[1431]={articles={"Duskwood (Classic)","Duskwood"},text="Duskwood was once the southern reaches of Elwynn Forest, until an unnatural darkness fell over it. Now its trees are gnarled and black under a lasting night, its farms stand empty, and the dead walk in Raven Hill Cemetery, while worgen prowl the woods and ogres push in from Deadwind Pass.\n\nStormwind has all but abandoned the forest. Its people hold on in the town of Darkshire, guarded by their own militia, the Night Watch, under Commander Althea Ebonlocke, whose father Lord Ello Ebonlocke is the town's mayor.",chapters={{after=58,articles={"The Night Watch (3)","The Night Watch (Classic)","The Night Watch (2)"},text="When Stormwind withdrew its guards, Darkshire was left to defend itself, and the Night Watch has too few watchers to keep the dead at bay. Skeletons stalk the Tranquil Gardens Cemetery south of town, and worse fill Raven Hill to the west. Volunteers have cleared both graveyards and burned out the plague spreaders crowding the eastern mausoleum at Raven Hill, earning Commander Ebonlocke's thanks."},{after=253,articles={"Bride of the Embalmer","Translation to Ello","Note to the Mayor (Classic)","The Hermit (Classic)","Abercrombie","Stitches"},text="Abercrombie, an old hermit in a shack above Raven Hill, asked a stranger for ghoul ribs, herbs and other odd supplies, then sent a cheerful letter to his old friend the mayor. Sirra Von'Indi translated it, and Lord Ebonlocke went white: Abercrombie is the Embalmer of Darkshire's children's tale, an alchemist driven mad by his wife Eliza's death, who put his own heart in her body to bring her back. With the stranger's help he had built an abomination, Stitches, and sent it lumbering down the road towards the town. His heart is now taken from Eliza's grave, and its magic will shield Darkshire from his evil."},{after=55,articles={"Morbent Fel (quest)","Sven's Revenge","The Shadowy Figure","Return to Sven","Seeking Wisdom","The Doomed Fleet","Armed and Ready (Classic)","Morbent Fel"},text="Sven Yorgen came home to find his family slaughtered by dark riders out of Deadwind Pass, and a shadowy figure burying something by his barn. The figure was Jitters, hiding in a ghost town, whose journal told the whole story, and Sven had seen the riders meeting the necromancer Morbent Fel, who raises Duskwood's dead. Fel's magic shields him from harm, so Bishop Farthing of Stormwind had a weapon forged against him from holy Lightforge iron, salvaged from a sunken Kul Tiran fleet off the Wetlands. Morbent Fel is dead; the dark riders are still at large, but Sven calls the deed the stuff of Duskwood's legends."},{after=98,articles={"The Legend of Stalvan (13)","The Legend of Stalvan (Classic)","Stalvan Mistmantle"},text="The seer Madame Eva dreamed of death stalking her granddaughter Alyssa, and of a name: Stalvan. The town registry led to Stalvan Mistmantle, once headmaster of the Moonbrook schoolhouse and tutor to the Flintridge family. Scattered journal pages told the rest: he became obsessed with his pupil Tilloa, and when she saw him only as a teacher he butchered her and her bridegroom in what the town remembers as a massacre. Clerk Daltry matched the handwriting, and Stalvan, gone mad and undead in his cottage north of Darkshire, has been put down at last."},{after=223,articles={"Worgen in the Woods (Classic, 4)","Worgen in the Woods (Classic)"},text="Not everyone in Darkshire trusts the Night Watch to keep them safe. The Carevin family hunts demons, the undead and anyone who aids them, and its hunter Calor, who left the Watch in disgust at its complacency, tests recruits against the Nightbane worgen of Brightwood Grove and the Rotting Orchard. Those who pass are welcomed by Jonathan Carevin, the head of the family, with a warning to trust no one who will not aid their cause."},{after=1044,articles={"Answered Questions","The Scythe of Elune (Classic)","The Carevin Family","Velinde Starsong"},text="The night elves of Darnassus have searched for Velinde Starsong, a priestess who vanished carrying a relic of their goddess, the Scythe of Elune. The trail led to Duskwood and the mine of Roland's Doom, where the worgen were first seen, and there Velinde's ghost rose from the place where she fell. Jonathan Carevin bears her no ill will for the evil she brought into his woods, and his hunters will hold the worgen back. In Darnassus, the news that the Scythe is lost from Elune's servants is grim indeed."},{after=231,articles={"A Daughter's Love","Mor'Ladim (quest)","Morgan Ladimore (quest)","The Daughter Who Lived","The Weathered Grave (quest)","Mor'Ladim"},text="Morgan Ladimore was a noble knight who went north to fight in Lordaeron and came home to find his house destroyed and his family's graves in Raven Hill. Mad with grief, he cut down the cemetery's attendants, then took his own life; his body rose as Mor'Ladim, a walking horror that killed any who crossed his path. Now he has been laid to rest, and his daughter Sarah, who survived after all, laid her ring on his grave. His spirit gave up his sword, Archeus, hoping her love might earn him forgiveness under the Light."}}},
[1432]={articles={"Loch Modan (Classic)","Loch Modan"},text="Loch Modan is a land of pine-clad hills in eastern Khaz Modan, ringed by grey mountains around a great lake held in place by the Stonewrought Dam, an engineering wonder without equal. Thelsamar is its dwarven town, and Dun Algaz leads north to the Wetlands.\n\nThe orcs overran the loch in the Second War before the dwarves drove them out, and broken siege engines still lie on its islands and bed. Now troggs unearthed by the dwarves' digs haunt the hills, and ogres hold the Mo'grosh Stronghold in the northeast.",chapters={{after=217,articles={"In Defense of the King's Lands (Classic 4)","In Defense of the King's Lands (Classic)","The Trogg Threat (Classic)"},text="With the Ironforge Reserve sent away to the Alliance Front, the few soldiers left at the southern guard tower can barely hold the gate, while troggs tunnel up from every crevice. Captain Rugelfuss and Mountaineer Cobbleflint called on volunteers to thin them out, then to strike at their chieftain, Grawmug, and his two hulking guards in their cave in Stonesplinter Valley. Grawmug is dead, and the captain believes the troggs might yet be driven back into the earth."},{after=309,articles={"Protecting the Shipment (Classic)","After the Ambush"},text="Prospector Ironband's excavation of ancient ruins east of the loch has slowed to a crawl, and he has been pleading for more blastpowder. When it came, the shipment was ambushed by Dark Iron dwarves, betrayed by one of the dwarves guarding it, Saean, a secret sympathizer. The rest of the powder has reached the dig under escort, but Ironband is troubled that the Dark Irons have friends able to get such material for their plans."},{after=2038,articles={"Bingles' Missing Supplies","Find Bingles"},text="The gnome Bingles Blastenheimer was sent flying over Gnomeregan to scout it, and fell silent for a week. He had crashed his gyrocopter into the loch, and troggs from a nearby island carried off his tools and special Blastencapper explosives. With them recovered, Bingles promises that the troggs and leper gnomes in Gnomeregan will soon face a gnomish attack from the air."},{after=283,articles={"A Dark Threat Looms (7)","A Dark Threat Looms","A Dark Threat Looms (4)","A Dark Threat Looms (6)"},text="Most of the Stonewrought Dam's guards were sent to the Alliance Front, and Chief Engineer Hinderweir feared the Dark Irons would strike it next. Their sappers left a strange powder on the dam's ramp, which the explosives expert Ashlan Stonesmirk named Seaforium: harmless dry, but wet, enough to blow Ironforge out of its mountain. Dark Iron swimmers then planted a keg at the foot of the dam, and only a mixture of lurker venom, a crocolisk tear and a Mo'grosh crystal defused it in time. The dam is safe, for now."},{after=455,articles={"The Algaz Gauntlet"},text="The Dragonmaw orcs, who usually keep to the Wetlands hills, have pushed south into the pass of Dun Algaz, threatening to cut Loch Modan off from Menethil Harbor, the dwarves' port in the north. A stranger fought through their scouts and grunts to bring Valstag Ironjaw word in Menethil. Ironjaw counts it as bad news, and no one yet knows what has driven the orcs to surge into Algaz."},{after=704,articles={"Agmond's Fate","Find Agmond"},text="Prospector Agmond, who ran the dwarves' digs in the Badlands, stopped sending reports, and was found at the southernmost site picked to the bones. His last report spoke of ancient urns uncovered at Uldaman, and Ironband fears they drove the troggs into a frenzy, just as the carved idols at his own site did. The urns have been brought to Ironband, who hopes to learn what links the troggs to these ancient relics, and promises Agmond will not be forgotten."}}},
[1433]={articles={"Redridge Mountains (Classic)","Redridge Mountains"},text="The Redridge Mountains rise east of Elwynn Forest, a country of rushing rivers, tall elms and red stone under Stormwind's protection. Its people send timber, fish and crops to the capital, and its heart is the town of Lakeshire on the northern shore of Lake Everstill, where Magistrate Solomon keeps the law.\n\nThe peace has not held. A force of Blackrock orcs from the Burning Steppes has taken Stonewatch Keep in the north, gnoll packs roam the hills, and the dark Tower of Ilgalar looms in the east. Lakeshire's people find themselves besieged.",chapters={{after=126,articles={"Howling in the Hills","A Baying of Gnolls"},text="As if the orcs were not enough, gnoll brutes and mystics have been stalking the ridge above Lakeshire and carrying off Verner Osgood's horses whenever they stray. Their leader, Yowler, howled louder than all the rest, until a stranger ended him and scattered the pack. Verner is grateful, but he knows why it fell to a stranger: the Stormwind army is so busy with the orcs that it has no soldiers left for Redridge's lesser threats."},{after=125,articles={"The Lost Tools"},text="The siege has cut Lakeshire off from its supplies. When the bridge was blown out, Foreman Oslow's tools from Goldshire had to come across Lake Everstill by boat, and an orc catapult sank it, toolbox and all. A stranger brought the toolbox up from the deepest part of the lake, past its murlocs, so the town's rebuilding can go on."},{after=19,articles={"Tharil'zun (quest)","Blackrock Menace"},text="From Stonewatch Keep the Blackrock orcs raid the lands north of Lakeshire and keep its people penned in town, and more of them lurk in the southeast. Their outrunners and renegades set ambushes on the road between the town and the keep, led by a crafty orc named Tharil'zun. Marshal Marris put a price on him, and Tharil'zun is dead."},{after=115,articles={"Shadow Magic"},text="The Blackrocks have brought shadowcasters to Redridge, and with them midnight orbs, demon-tainted devices whose dark power has struck Lakeshire's defenders hard. Orbs taken from the slain casters were handed to Marshal Marris, who means to have them destroyed at once."},{after=146,articles={"Messenger to Darkshire (2)","Messenger to Stormwind","Messenger to Stormwind (2)","Messenger to Westfall","Messenger to Westfall (2)","Messenger to Darkshire"},text="Magistrate Solomon begged Stormwind for reinforcements, and General Marcus Jonathan promised the army's support once the King gave the word; but the reply that followed left Solomon uneasy that something was amiss in the kingdom. He turned to Gryan Stoutmantle in Westfall and Lord Ebonlocke of Darkshire, and both refused: Stormwind had deserted them too, the army withdrawn from Westfall and the guards from Darkshire, and they could spare no one. Lakeshire stands alone, and Solomon suspects treachery and means to get to the bottom of it."},{after=219,articles={"Missing In Action"},text="In a cave in the hills, the Blackrock orcs held a wounded prisoner: Corporal Keeshan of the 12th Sabre Regiment, the last survivor of a company the orcs overpowered and cut down. Too badly hurt to escape alone, he was helped back to Lakeshire, where he reported to Marshal Marris for duty."},{after=169,articles={"Wanted: Gath'Ilzogg (quest)"},text="Gath'Ilzogg led the Blackrock assault that took Stonewatch Keep and the raids on Lakeshire, and he held the keep, plotting the clan's next blow against the humans. A bounty in Magistrate Solomon's name brought his head back to Lakeshire. Solomon calls it vindication for the innocents he killed, and the first step in winning the keep back for Stormwind."},{after=249,articles={"Morganth (quest)","Looking Further","A Watchful Eye","Solomon's Law"},text="The Shadowhide gnolls of the eastern foothills serve Morganth, a warlock in the Tower of Ilgalar, and Lakeshire's law counts aiding him a capital crime. His rival Theocritus, the high mage of Tower Azora in Elwynn, spied on him through hidden glyphs and found him at odds with the orcs of Stonewatch Keep, and hunting for the Scythe of Elune, which he might already have found. Theocritus dared not face him himself, but Morganth is dead, and Theocritus counts the kingdom saved from a grave peril."}}},
[1434]={articles={"Stranglethorn Vale (Classic)","Booty Bay","Zul'Gurub"},text="Stranglethorn Vale is a vast, steaming jungle south of Duskwood, full of beasts and strangle-thorns. For millennia it was the heart of the jungle trolls' Gurubashi Empire, until the cruelty of priests who sacrificed trolls to the blood god Hakkar the Soulflayer tore it apart in civil war. The ruins of its cities crumble in the heat, held now by the Bloodscalp and Skullsplitter tribes, while the temple city of Zul'Gurub in the northeast still belongs to Hakkar's worshippers. The Darkspear trolls, driven out to sea long ago, now fight for the Horde.\n\nStormwind once tried to carve trade routes through the jungle, but its port at Blackwater Cove was overrun by trolls; goblins of the Steamwheedle Cartel took the cove and built Booty Bay, a cutthroat port open to all, ruled by Baron Revilgaz and guarded by the Blackwater Raiders. The Venture Company mines the hills, the renegade Colonel Kurzen holds a compound in the north, Bloodsail pirates prowl the southern shores, and the Horde keeps Grom'gol Base Camp on the western coast.",chapters={{after=338,articles={"The Green Hills of Stranglethorn (Classic)","Welcome to the Jungle (Classic)","Nesingwary's Expedition"},text="In the north of the jungle camps Hemet Nesingwary, the dwarven war hero of the Alliance and master big game hunter, with his companions Ajeck Rouack, Sir S. J. Erlgadin and Barnil. He has come to Stranglethorn after one great and elusive beast, and lets proven hunters share the hunt. Meanwhile Barnil, trusted to proofread his master's novel, The Green Hills of Stranglethorn, let a gust of wind scatter its pages across the jungle. All four chapters are whole again, and he looks forward to seeing the book in libraries throughout the world."},{after=331,articles={"Report to Doren","The Spy Revealed!","Rebel Camp","Kurzen's Expedition"},text="Colonel Kurzen led an expedition of Stormwind's army into Stranglethorn, but something changed him, and a small band under Lieutenant Doren broke away to found the Rebel Camp, outnumbered ten to one by the men who stayed loyal. When their spy inside Kurzen's Compound was found out, the logistics officer Bookie Herod's records and a muddy note signed only \"M\" pointed to a traitor among the rebels themselves. It was Private Merle, a soldier Doren had trusted, and unmasking him may have saved the camp."},{after=202,articles={"Colonel Kurzen (quest)","The Second Rebellion","Kurzen's Expedition"},text="Kurzen was a brilliant tactician, and his medicine men kept his troops in line with an elixir brewed from basilisk blood and blue stone. He commanded from deep inside the Stockpile, the cave in his compound. Kurzen and his leaders are dead now, and the rebels can breathe easier: of all the jungle's menaces, Doren says, none had Kurzen's cruel intellect."},{after=206,articles={"Mai'Zoth (quest)","Mai'Zoth"},text="Brother Nimetz of the Rebel Camp never believed that Kurzen, once a great and noble man, had fallen into evil on his own. He found the cause: the ogre mage Mai'Zoth of the Mosh'Ogg mound in the eastern mountains, who bent the colonel's mind with an artifact called the Mind's Eye. Mai'Zoth is dead, so his evil cannot raise up another Kurzen, and Nimetz is studying the Mind's Eye to learn whether it can be used for good, or must be destroyed."},{after=592,articles={"Saving Yenniku (Classic)","Yenniku","Zanzil the Outcast"},text="Yenniku, Vol'jin's youngest son, was given to the Gurubashi as a child, as jungle troll custom demands, and was lost to his people when the Darkspears left Stranglethorn with Thrall. At Grom'gol the Darkspears forced the truth from the severed heads of the Bloodscalp chief and his witchdoctor: Zanzil the Outcast, a troll exile who drugs his followers into obedience, had taken Yenniku body and soul. With a soul gem made from Mai'Zoth's Mind's Eye, Yenniku's soul has been captured and brought to Nimboya, and the Darkspears will etch the deed in stone."},{after=569,articles={"The Defense of Grom'gol (2)","Grom'gol Base Camp"},text="Grom'gol Base Camp exists to keep a chain of supplies running to Stonard in the Swamp of Sorrows, and Commander Aggro'gosh never meant to make war on the jungle's tribes. But a band of ogres seized the Mizjah Ruins to the southeast and kept ambushing his caravans despite every warning. They have been driven back, a firm message delivered in the Warchief's name."},{after=213,articles={"Hostile Takeover","Venture Company Mining (Classic)"},text="The Venture Company has strung its mining camps through Stranglethorn, to the fury of Booty Bay's goblins. Its Gelriz had already muscled out moguls appointed by the trade princes, and when he tried to cut in on Baron Revilgaz himself, the baron answered with theft: samples of the strange blue crystals the Venture Co. geologists were digging near Lake Nazferiti. The crystals had been tumbled in a way that suggests the company means to use them as fuel or a catalyst, and the samples have gone to a goblin chemist to learn more."},{after=97048,articles={"Returning to Port","The Cook in the Cage"},text="Kurzen's men seized a young man wandering the jungle and caged him in their compound, where talk of tigers and headshrinkers had him half mad with fear. With a stolen key and a Kurzen fighter's uniform, Wendel Mathers slipped out, and reached Booty Bay with tales of how he and his rescuer cut their way free. His captain, Aransas, does not believe a word of it, but she is glad to have her cook back."},{after=613,articles={"Cracking Maury's Foot"},text="\"Sea Wolf\" MacKinley of Booty Bay keeps a long list of people who owe him. Three of them, Maury \"Club Foot\" Wilkins, Jon-Jon the Crow and Chucky \"Ten Thumbs\", skipped town rather than pay, and turned up cursed and bewitched, wandering the jungle ruins. MacKinley cared only about what he was owed, and with Maury's foot unlocked by a key won back from the Mosh'Ogg ogres he used to gamble with, the old sea dog has been paid at last."},{after=611,articles={"The Curse of the Tides","\"Pretty Boy\" Duncan","Stranglethorn Vale (Classic)"},text="Gurubashi legend tells of the Stone of the Tides, which gave its bearer command of water but slowly faded him from the world, and when sailors spoke of a haunted island with water elementals and a raving goblin ghost, Baron Revilgaz guessed that the goblin mage Gazban had found it. Archmage Ansirem Runeweaver of Dalaran knew of a troll dagger that could break the altar amplifying its power, but he had given it to his daughter Catelyn, who ran off to Booty Bay to become a pirate, and her Bloodsail lover \"Pretty Boy\" Duncan had stolen it. With the blade back, the altar in the sunken Vile Reef was shattered and Gazban slain. The Stone now has pride of place in Revilgaz's collection of troll artifacts."},{after=608,articles={"The Bloodsail Buccaneers (5)","The Bloodsail Buccaneers (4)","The Bloodsail Buccaneers (Classic)","Bloodsail Buccaneers"},text="The Bloodsail Buccaneers, the most bloodthirsty pirates on the seas, have been seen all along southern Stranglethorn, and letters found in a cove north of Booty Bay told of a planned attack on the port. Fleet Master Seahorn of the Blackwater Raiders had their command post on the Wild Shore raided for its charts and orders, which showed their fleet anchored just off the cape. Rather than send the whole fleet, he sent a handful of brave adventurers to cut down its leaders, Captain Stillwater, Captain Keelhaul and Fleet Master Firallon, and the Blackwater Raiders raised three cheers."},{after=208,articles={"Big Game Hunter (Classic)","Tiger Mastery","Panther Mastery","Raptor Mastery"},text="Nesingwary's trials led from the jungle's ordinary tigers, panthers and raptors to its legends: the giant tigress Sin'Dall, the elusive panther Bhag'thera and the raptor Tethis, who had killed many hunters before. The true prize, the reason Hemet came to Stranglethorn at all, was King Bangalash, the great white tiger. His head is Nesingwary's now, and the hunter who took it is counted a comrade of the expedition."},{after=630,articles={"Message in a Bottle (2)","Message in a Bottle (Classic)"},text="A message in a bottle washed up from the South Seas, a captive's plea for rescue from an island where a great Skymane held her. On Jaguero Isle, Princess Poobah was kept on a chain by King Mukla, who commands the island's beasts. Mukla is dead and the shackle unlocked, and the princess, saved from great dishonour, gave her rescuer what token she could."},{after=4621,articles={"Avast Ye, Admiral! (quest)","Bloodsail Buccaneers"},text="Not everyone sides with Booty Bay. To those who had made themselves its enemies, Fleet Master Firallon, aboard the Crimson Veil, offered a place among the Bloodsail at a price: Booty Bay's power rests on Fleet Master Seahorn, who commands its ships, and Baron Revilgaz, who makes its law, and both had to be struck down. The deed done, Firallon named the killer an honorary admiral of the Bloodsail navy, with a hat like no other to prove it."}}},
[1435]={articles={"Swamp of Sorrows (Classic)","Stonard"},text="The Swamp of Sorrows is a stagnant, brackish marsh of sucking bogs and weeping trees, once the northern half of the Black Morass. Crocolisks, jaguars and bog beasts haunt its waters, and murlocs its coast. Long ago the Atal'ai, exiled priests of the Gurubashi trolls, raised a temple to their blood god Hakkar here; Ysera sank it beneath the Pool of Tears, and her green dragonspawn still guard it against the evil she warned lies within.\n\nStonard, founded when the orcs first came through the Dark Portal and rebuilt under Thrall, is the Horde's lone town in the swamp, far from its other lands and close to the Alliance's keep at Nethergarde. Most of the draenei stranded on Azeroth when the Portal fell went mad, and live as the Lost Ones of the Fallow Sanctuary; a few Broken cling to their wits at the Harborage, and deal with both Horde and Alliance.",chapters={{after=1392,articles={"Noboru the Cudgel (quest)","Draenethyst Crystals (Classic)","Noboru the Cudgel"},text="The draenei of the Harborage say the Lost Ones look as they do but have become monsters, driven mad by their journey to Azeroth, and they have stolen the draenethyst crystals the Harborage holds sacred. For months the Lost One Noboru and his gang roamed the swamp killing any draenei who crossed their path, and the Harborage was too weak to face him. Noboru is dead and his cudgel brought home, and the draenei rejoice to have found a friend."},{after=1425,articles={"Deliver the Shipment","The Lost Caravan"},text="Watcher Biggs' caravan was bound for Nethergarde Keep when the Lost Ones fell on it, killing its guards and dragging a wagon off to the Fallow Sanctuary; he alone survived to guard what was left. The stolen chest of reagents has been recovered and the most vital supplies carried on to Nethergarde, whose wizards had given caravan and watcher up for lost. Whatever they do with those reagents is tied to the Dark Portal, and vital to the Alliance's security."},{after=1444,articles={"Return to Fel'Zerul","Pool of Tears (Horde)","The Atal'ai Exile"},text="Thrall sent Fel'Zerul to Stonard to look into strange happenings at the Temple of Atal'Hakkar, only for him to find it sunk beneath the Pool of Tears and guarded by green dragons who call themselves Azeroth's protectors. Artifacts from the pool went north to a priest living among the Witherbark trolls of the Hinterlands, exiled by the high priest Jammal'an the Prophet for doubting his prophecy. The relics are harmless, the exile said, but if Jammal'an has found the right fetish enchantment for his summoning ritual, the whole world is in peril."},{after=2623,articles={"The Swamp Talker","Fall From Grace","Fallen Hero of the Horde"},text="Beside the road to the Blasted Lands stands the ghost of an orc, the Fallen Hero of the Horde, who blames himself for leading his men to ruin by marching into the Blasted Lands before his orders came. Stonard's dispatch commander had sent them, but the messengers were ambushed by murlocs, and the one survivor, Bengor, broken and barely able to speak, named the murloc who took them: the Swamp Talker. The orders have been recovered from its cave, and they are worse than the hero feared: nineteen of his men could have been spared their agony."},{after=2702,articles={"Heroes of Old","A Tale of Sorrow","Fallen Hero of the Horde","Corporal Thund Splithoof"},text="The Fallen Hero tells his tale to Horde and Alliance alike: his men were captured by the demon lord Razelikh and his servants, and while he escaped only to die, their souls were bound into stones and enslaved. Adventurers shattered eighteen of those stones and set the soldiers free. One of them, the tauren Corporal Thund Splithoof, appeared across the road to thank them, and gave them a relic his people no longer need for the fight against the Legion."},{after=3627,articles={"Uniting the Shattered Amulet","The Demon Hunter","Razelikh the Defiler"},text="Razelikh's triad, Grol the Destroyer, Lady Sevine and Archmage Allistarj, each wore a third of an amulet that made them unkillable within the Blasted Lands. The demon hunter Loramus Thalipedes, hidden on an island off Azshara, helped forge Enchanted Azsharite Fel Weaponry bearing the demon's true name, and with it all three fell. The amulet is whole again, but Razelikh has felt the break in his chain of command, and knows his enemies are coming."}}},
[1436]={articles={"Westfall (Classic)","Westfall"},text="Westfall lies on the coast west of Elwynn Forest, a land of rolling farms that once fed the kingdom of Stormwind. It has lain half fallow since the Second War, and now its fields are yellowed and dry, picked over by vultures, gnolls and the mechanical harvest watchers that turned on their owners. Murlocs crowd the shore, and the ruined town of Moonbrook sits in the south.\n\nBandits calling themselves the Defias Brotherhood hold many of the farms, and Stormwind, its soldiers and coin stretched thin, has done little to stop them. A few stubborn farmers cling to their land, and the People's Militia under Gryan Stoutmantle keeps watch from its tower on Sentinel Hill.",chapters={{after=184,articles={"Furlbrow's Deed (Classic)"},text="Theodore and Verna Furlbrow are among the farmers driven out: they fled their pumpkin farm in a hurry and got no further than the roadside, where their wagon broke down. Their deed turned up on a Defias thug, with a note to his fellows boasting that they had leaned on Furlbrow for it, and that it would serve as a pattern for forging deeds to farms of their own. The deed is back with Furlbrow, who says the land is no place for honest folk now, but means to reclaim it if times ever improve."},{after=14,articles={"The People's Militia (3)","The People's Militia (quest)","The People's Militia (2)"},text="Gryan Stoutmantle came home from the tainted lands of Lordaeron to find Westfall in a grim state, and raised the People's Militia to win it back. Its recruits earn their place in blood: Defias trappers and smugglers at the Jangolode Mine and the abandoned farms, pillagers at the Gold Coast Quarry and Moonbrook, and highwaymen plotting in the Dagger Hills of the south. Stoutmantle has seen enough to induct new blood into the Militia, and still believes there is hope for Westfall."},{after=92748,articles={"Explosive Consultation","Testing the Wells","Murloc Gills","The State of the Mines","Moonbrook Espionage"},text="An elf named Alba Fairmoon has come to Westfall to find out what is poisoning its soil. Water drawn from the wells of the Jansen Stead and the Molsen Farm was laced with toxic metals, and she feared the taint had reached the sea and its fish. The Defias have put kobolds and gnolls to work stripping the Jangolode Mine and the Gold Coast Quarry, but the poison did not come from there: supplies found in Moonbrook point to a vast industrial works in the Deadmines beneath the town, abandoned since the First War. Alba is certain this is the source, and has sent for explosives from Stormwind's Dwarven District."},{after=104,articles={"The Coastal Menace (Classic)","Keeper of the Flame (Classic)"},text="The Westfall Lighthouse watches over a coast strewn with wrecks, and its keeper is Captain Grayson, a ghost whose own ship broke on those rocks on a night the light was out. The murloc Old Murk-Eye had frightened the keeper's family away then, and when they came back he led the murlocs against the lighthouse again; this time the family died. Grayson keeps the flame burning for the sailors of the Great Sea, and Old Murk-Eye is dead at last."},{after=141,articles={"The Defias Brotherhood (4)","The Defias Brotherhood (quest)","The Defias Brotherhood (2)","The Defias Brotherhood (3)"},text="Stoutmantle sent a stranger to learn who leads the Defias, starting with Wiley, a rogue in the Lakeshire inn who owed him a favour. Wiley would only risk a note: the gang is planning something big, alongside gnolls, kobolds and even goblins, and he let slip a mention of the Stonemasons. Mathias Shaw of SI:7 explained the rest: the Stonemasons' Guild, led by Edwin VanCleef, rebuilt Stormwind after the orcs razed it, and left bitter at how the crown treated them. Stoutmantle knows the name and can hardly believe it, but he wants firmer proof."},{after=155,articles={"The Defias Brotherhood (6)","The Defias Brotherhood (5)"},text="A captured Defias messenger carried the proof Stoutmantle wanted: VanCleef is in charge. Then the Militia caught a thief trying to steal Farmer Saldean's wagon, who bought his life with the Brotherhood's greatest secret. Guarded through the heart of Moonbrook with the gang out for his head, the traitor showed where VanCleef and his men are hiding, and Stoutmantle believes VanCleef is as good as theirs."}}},
[1437]={articles={"Wetlands (Classic)","Wetlands","Menethil Harbor","Grim Batol"},text="The Wetlands are a cold, grey marsh north of Loch Modan, walled by mountains and open to the sea in the west, where mists hide the ground and old dwarven strongholds lie in rubble. Menethil Harbor on the coast, named for King Terenas Menethil II, is the Alliance's foothold, and in the northeast the fortress of Dun Modr guards the Thandol Span, the great bridge to Arathi.\n\nThe land has seen war after war. The Wildhammer dwarves once held it, and the Dark Irons nearly seized their fortress of Grim Batol in the War of the Three Hammers. In the Second War the Dragonmaw orcs took Grim Batol and enslaved red dragons there, until the dragons broke free and burned them out; the survivors fled into the mountains, and the red dragonflight now guards Grim Batol from all comers.",chapters={{after=98191,articles={"A Friend of the Family (2)","A Friend of the Family","Return the Statuette","Reclaiming Goods","Claws from the Deep"},text="Murlocs are building villages along Menethil's coast and raiding its merchants, and when they attacked the merchant ship Blind Princess, they dragged her cargo overboard, among it a silver statuette of King Terenas Menethil as a young paladin. Found at last in a barrel in the muck by the murloc hovels, it was too precious to keep in a harbor ringed by enemies, and Captain Stoutfist feared what the Dragonmaw would do with it. Highlord Bolvar Fordragon sensed that the Light had gathered in it, and had it kept in Stormwind's Cathedral of Light, in memory of a king who hated such memorials."},{after=275,articles={"Blisters on The Land","The Greenwarden","Tramping Paws","Fire Taboo","Rethiel the Greenwarden"},text="In the marsh east of the road dwells Rethiel the Greenwarden, a bog beast who speaks for the land. The Mosshide gnolls lived in balance with the fens for years, but their numbers have swollen, and they trample its plants and burn its trees for warmth. A festering has also risen in the waterways as diseased fen creepers. The gnolls have been thinned, their fire flints smashed and cast into the sea, and the creepers laid to rest, and the land is soothed."},{after=293,articles={"Cleansing the Eye","The Third Fleet (Classic)","The Cursed Crew (Classic)","Lifting the Curse (Classic)"},text="First Mate Fitzsimmons, nursing his hangover in Menethil, is the sole survivor of the Third Fleet of Kul Tiras, burned and sunk off this coast by red dragons ridden by orcs. His shipmates still walk the wrecks, held there by a cursed treasure in Captain Halyndor's strongbox aboard the Intrepid: a blackened Eye of Paleth. Only the priests of Stormwind could help, and Archbishop Benedictus has cleansed it, saying that the Eye had witnessed a terrible tragedy."},{after=296,articles={"Ormer's Revenge (3)","Ormer's Revenge","In Search of The Excavation Team (2)","In Search of The Excavation Team"},text="Merrin Rockweaver's letters to her husband stopped coming, and the scout sent after her found the Whelgar Excavation in ruins. The diggers had been uncovering huge bones when mottled raptors overran the site, and many dwarves died; Ormer Ironbraid saved Merrin and old Prospector Whelgar, and the three have been trapped on a bluff ever since. Ormer has had his vengeance on the raptors, and on Sarltooth, the great beast he believes led them."},{after=474,articles={"Defeat Nek'rosh","Nek'rosh's Gambit","War Banners"},text="The Dragonmaw clan was nearly destroyed after the Second War, but its remnants have regrown in the mountains and set up a war camp northeast of Menethil. Their chieftain, Nek'rosh, built catapults to besiege the harbor while the fighting in Dun Algaz kept reinforcements away. The catapults have been burned and Nek'rosh slain, and Captain Stoutfist thinks the clan may never recover."},{after=304,articles={"A Grim Task","Fall of Dun Modr"},text="Dark Iron dwarves blew up the Thandol Span's western bridge and then fell on the regiment guarding Dun Modr before it could regroup. Dun Modr has fallen, and the survivors, under Longbraid the Grim, cling to the hills outside, a shadow of the regiment they were. Their enemies were led by a cowardly warlock, Balgaras the Foul, who sent others to fight for him. Balgaras is dead, and Longbraid means to wipe out the rest before a new leader is sent."},{after=634,articles={"Plea To The Alliance","The Thandol Span","The Thandol Span (3)"},text="Longbraid sent Ol' Rustlocke to scout the remaining bridge, and he was found dead in a Dark Iron ambush, clutching a parchment: Kam Deepfury, the mastermind of the first attack, had a cache of explosives hidden across the span in Arathi. The cache is destroyed and the last bridge saved. But Captain Nials at Refuge Pointe can send no help: Stromgarde has fallen, and his soldiers will not leave the Highlands until it is retaken. Longbraid is on his own."},{after=1250,articles={"The Missing Diplomat (12)","The Missing Diplomat (11)"},text="In the Menethil tavern, Elling Trias's old friend Mikhail spotted a man listening too closely to his conversation: Tapoke \"Slim\" Jahn, who bolted and was run down. Slim confessed that the \"diplomat\" was no diplomat at all. The Defias took the king of Stormwind himself on his secret voyage to meet Jaina Proudmoore in Theramore, and they were working with someone bigger than themselves. Slim's part was to get their man, Hendel, aboard the king's ship; Mikhail, stunned, sent word to Trias at once."}}},
[1438]={articles={"Teldrassil"},text="Teldrassil, \"Crown of the Earth\", is the World Tree the night elves grew on an island off Kalimdor's northwestern coast after the Third War. Its crown holds their capital, Darnassus, and its boughs hold Rut'theran Village, Dolanaar and Shadowglen, amid twilit forests with a world of their own.\n\nIt is the Alliance's greatest stronghold in Kalimdor, and young night elves set out into the world from its shelter.",chapters={{after=97977,articles={"Nature's Call (Forever)","The Woodland Protector (Classic)"},text="The dryad Tarindrella, a protector of the forests who had not been felt in Kalimdor for years, has returned to Shadowglen, and that alone means something is wrong. The Gnarlpine furbolgs, the night elves' old allies, have abandoned their camps in the glen, and the totems they left behind tell why: they began to dream of being wiped out by a great shadow, until the fear became rage and the shadow took the shape of their elven friends. Tarindrella hopes their plight will help explain what is happening on Teldrassil."},{after=2561,articles={"Druid of the Claw (quest)","The Sleeping Druid"},text="In the Ban'ethil Barrow Den, Gnarlpine shaman found a way to tear a sleeping druid's spirit from his body. Oben Rageclaw was trapped in the Emerald Dream while the furbolgs sent his empty body against anyone exploring the den. The only way to free him was to kill that body and use the shamans' charm on it; now his spirit is free of them, to rest in the Dream for good."},{after=486,articles={"Ursal the Mauler (quest)","The Relics of Wakening","Gnarlpine Corruption (Classic)","A Troubling Breeze"},text="In the Ban'ethil Barrow Den the Druids of the Talon sleep to keep their pact with Ysera, guarded by the Great Warden Gaerolas Talvethren. The Gnarlpine, driven mad by their chieftain Ursal the Mauler and the fel moss he wields, ambushed the warden, pillaged Starbreeze Village and overran the den. Athridas Bearmantle used the Relics of Wakening to rouse the sleepers, a ritual that could only take hold once Ursal was dead. The druids are safe, but the source of Ursal's corruption is still somewhere in the forest."},{after=489,articles={"Seek Redemption!","Zenn's Bidding"},text="Zenn Foulhoof, who paid well for nightsaber fangs, owl feathers and spider silk, is a satyr, an enemy of the forest, and those who ran his errands killed Nature's creatures for him. To win back the trust of the Council of the Forest, they fed him fel cones, corrupted seeds that fall from the trees, billowing green smoke. Thinking them a harmless snack, he ate them, and was turned into a frog."},{after=2499,articles={"Oakenscowl (Classic)","Return to Denalan","Tumors","Rellian Greenspyre (Classic)"},text="A sickness is spreading among Teldrassil's timberlings: mossy tumours full of poison grow on them, and even their seeds carry the taint. Rellian Greenspyre in Darnassus could do nothing for them, and the worst afflicted, around Wellspring Lake, had to be cut down. Denalan, who studies the tree's plant life at Lake Al'Ameth, traced the corruption there to Oakenscowl, once a great leader of the timberlings, poisoned by a gargantuan tumour. Oakenscowl is dead and Denalan has the tumour to study, but the disease itself is still a mystery."},{after=99073,articles={"Easing Suffering","The Great Tree Provides","Not Dead Yet","The Lost Runner"},text="The Oracle Tree sends messages to the elders, and Sentinels posted at its glade carry them to Darnassus. The latest runner, Eralya Leafshadow, still in training, never arrived: she lay dying on the road, attacked by harpies sick with the same foulness spreading through the forest, and that rot had taken hold of her too. Byancie of Dolanaar brewed a salve from lasher fronds and spring water; it brought Eralya some peace, though Byancie could not promise it would save her."},{after=935,articles={"Crown of the Earth (6)","Crown of the Earth (5)","Crown of the Earth (4)","Crown of the Earth (3)","Crown of the Earth"},text="At Mount Hyjal, Nordrassil's power was spent to defeat Archimonde, and with it went the night elves' immortality; then Illidan the Betrayer went free, and Malfurion Stormrage vanished. Arch Druid Fandral Staghelm took the lead and, with the Circle of Ancients' approval, grew Teldrassil to win their immortality back. But Nozdormu refused to bless the new World Tree, chiding Staghelm for his arrogance, and Alexstrasza refused with him. Without their blessings Teldrassil's growth is flawed and unpredictable, which is why strange beasts rise from its soil and its furbolgs have gone mad."},{after=940,articles={"Teldrassil (quest)","The Enchanted Glade (Classic)"},text="Sentinel Arynia Cloudsbreak and a few Sentinels guard the Oracle Tree from the harpies nesting all around its glade, and the last runner the tree sent to Darnassus was killed by them. With the harpies driven from their nests, the tree trusted its message for Fandral Staghelm to the one who drove them off. Some of Teldrassil's troubles have eased, he found, while others have grown, and he fears his work on the tree, and the night elves' immortality, may never be restored."}}},
[1439]={articles={"Darkshore (Classic)"},text="Darkshore is a long, rocky strip of coast in northwestern Kalimdor, shrouded in mist, rain and shadowed woods. It was once among the richest lands of the ancient night elf empire, and the crumbling ruins of its great cities, Ameth'Aran, Bashal'Aran and Mathystra, still stand on its cliffs, haunted by the ghosts of the Highborne.\n\nThe night elves abandoned the coast during the Third War, and only a few have returned to rebuild the port of Auberdine, whose ships sail to Teldrassil and to Menethil Harbor across the sea. Rivers flowing down from Felwood carry its taint into Darkshore, sickening the wildlife and the Blackwood furbolgs, once friends of the night elves. Naga and murlocs prowl the shore, and cultists of the Dark Strand and the Twilight's Hammer lurk beyond the road.",chapters={{after=957,articles={"Bashal'Aran (4)","Bashal'Aran (quest)","Bashal'Aran (2)","Bashal'Aran (3)"},text="The satyrs and sprites infesting the ruins of Bashal'Aran feed on its old magic, yet there is one shrine, wrapped in a strange blue aura, that they will not approach. Bound within it was the spirit of Asterion, a Highborne imprisoned since the days when Queen Azshara held court beside the Well of Eternity. The moonstone seal that held him, crafted by his former master, was taken from the satyr leading the demons, and it could only be destroyed in the ancient flame that still burns in Ameth'Aran. The seal is destroyed and Asterion is free to see the changed world for himself; he senses that his jailer, Athrikus, still lives, and his despair is turning to thoughts of vengeance."},{after=2139,articles={"Tharnariun's Hope (quest)","Plagued Lands","Cleansing of the Infected"},text="A sickness is spreading through Darkshore's forests, and the thistle bears have suffered worst, turned rabid and frenzied. Tharnariun Treetender of Auberdine captured a living bear to study a cure, but in the meantime the sick animals had to be culled, grim work for those who serve nature. The infected Den Mother, whose cave near Bashal'Aran sent rabid bears into the land, is dead; the forest's pain is eased, though perhaps only for a moment."},{after=994,articles={"Escape Through Force","How Big a Threat?","How Big a Threat? (2)","A Lost Master","A Lost Master (2)"},text="Terenthis of Auberdine set out to learn how far the furbolgs' corruption had spread, and found a maddened Blackwood camp just south of the village. Worse, the druid Volcor had gone missing among them; only his bear companion Grimclaw came back. Cloaked in an enchanted moonstalker hide, a stranger slipped past the furbolgs to Volcor's hiding place in a cave, and fought a way out with him to the road. Volcor is safe, but no cure for the corruption has yet been found, and the Blackwood camps grow ever closer to Auberdine."},{after=951,articles={"Mathystra Relics","The Master's Glaive (quest)","The Twilight Camp","Return to Onu"},text="Onu, the Ancient of Lore at the Grove of the Ancients, was troubled to find both naga and the Twilight's Hammer in Darkshore. The cult worships the old lords of the earth, and has camped at the Master's Glaive, where one such lord fell long ago, impaled. A page torn from their maddening tome turned out to be ancient elven writing, cursed by the cult: the Twilight's Hammer is using Highborne magic to bring its old masters back. Relics gathered from the ruins of Mathystra may teach Onu how, if he can unlock their secrets before the cult does."},{after=981,articles={"The Tower of Althalaxx (9)","The Tower of Althalaxx","The Tower of Althalaxx (4)","The Tower of Althalaxx (6)","The Tower of Althalaxx (8)"},text="The Cult of the Dark Strand had gathered at the Tower of Althalaxx, northeast of Auberdine, and its master was Athrikus Narassin, an ancient warlock who once held Asterion prisoner. Delgren the Purifier at Maestra's Post found that Athrikus drew his power from soul gems holding trapped Highborne spirits, kept in the satyrs' temples of Ashenvale. With the souls set free, Athrikus was slain at the top of his tower. The Dark Strand is beaten and scattered, but its demonic allies may yet regroup."},{after=741,articles={"The Absent Minded Prospector (3)","The Absent Minded Prospector","The Absent Minded Prospector (2)"},text="Prospector Remtravel of the Explorers' League was digging south of Auberdine when horrible golems sprang from the ground and overran his site. He barely noticed, too busy looking for his stone brush, and his assistant Hollee fled to Auberdine for help. Escorted through his own dig, the oblivious dwarf at last found the mysterious fossil he had promised the League. The fossil is on its way to Chief Archaeologist Greywhisker in Darnassus, and Remtravel, as ever, stays behind."},{after=4763,articles={"The Blackwood Corrupted"},text="The Blackwood furbolgs did not fall to madness on their own: the satyrs hold sway over them through talismans that channel their corrupting magic. Thundris Windweaver brewed a cleansing bowl from moonwell water and the furbolgs' own fruit, nuts and grain, and set it by the bonfire of their northern camp, where it lured out the satyr corruptor. The satyr is dead and its talisman destroyed; it is one battle won in the night elves' war to reclaim their forests."},{after=5321,articles={"The Sleeper Has Awakened (Classic)"},text="Kerlonian Evershade, a druid newly awoken from the Emerald Dream, set out for Maestra's Post to meet Liladris Moonriver, but kept dozing off along the road. With a horn to rouse him whenever he lay down, a stranger guided him south past Blackwood furbolgs and Dark Strand cultists to Ashenvale. Kerlonian arrived safely, and Liladris fully expects to find him asleep somewhere around her camp."},{after=98028,articles={"Baron Marinous (quest)"},text="At the bottom of the sunken Ruins of Mathystra, a powerful water elemental, Baron Marinous, guarded the Fathom Stone. Once summoned and destroyed, it left behind a globe of water clouded by something that seemed to resist being looked at, as if whatever had befallen the ruins were still at work. Onu was startled to hear of stairs descending beneath Mathystra, and must confer with the other Ancients; he was glad, at least, that the discovery was made before the naga's."}}},
[1440]={articles={"Ashenvale (Classic)"},text="Ashenvale is an ancient forest south of Darkshore and north of the Barrens, the ancestral homeland of the night elves, who have tended it for thousands of years. They hold Astranaar, Maestra's Post, Silverwind Refuge, Forest Song and the Shrine of Aessina, and the Sentinels patrol its roads.\n\nThe Horde has come into the eastern woods to fell trees for lumber, from Splintertree Post and the Warsong Lumber Camp, with a smaller outpost at Zoram'gar on the western coast, and the Warsong Outriders and Silverwing Sentinels fight over every grove. Corrupted furbolgs roam the forest, satyrs haunt ancient elven ruins at Xavian, Night Run and Satyrnaar, demons hold Fire Scar Shrine and Demon Fall Canyon, and naga lurk in the drowned city of Zoram.",chapters={{after=1140,articles={"The Tower of Althalaxx (6)","The Tower of Althalaxx (4)"},text="Delgren the Purifier, hunting the Cult of the Dark Strand from Maestra's Post, learned that a cultist's soul gem held the secret of its power. Inside was a trapped Highborne spirit, and the cult's orders, scrawled in orcish, revealed that its master Athrikus held two more such gems, in the satyr temples of Night Run and Satyrnaar. Those souls are free now, and Athrikus has lost the last of the power he stole from them."},{after=1046,articles={"Raene's Cleansing (11)","Raene's Cleansing","Raene's Cleansing (6)","Raene's Cleansing (9)","Raene's Cleansing (10)"},text="Raene Wolfrunner's friend Teronis went looking for a way to slow the furbolg attacks on Astranaar: a rod made by a dead, evil wizard named Dartol. Teronis was found slain by murlocs at Lake Falathim, but the gem he sought was recovered, the rod rebuilt and recharged at a hidden shrine, and its magic turned its bearer into a furbolg. In that guise a stranger met Krolg, an uncorrupted furbolg who blames the night elves for much, yet would not let his own kind prey on their children. Ran Bloodtooth, the fiercest of the maddened furbolgs, is dead, and his skull is in Raene's hands."},{after=1009,articles={"Ruuzel (Classic)","The Ancient Statuette"},text="Talen, a night elf exploring the drowned ruins of Zoram, was attacked by naga and lost his prized find, an ancient statuette that may unlock many of the old city's secrets. Its key is the Ring of Zoram, said to open any lock in the city; it belonged to Zoram's rulers before the Sundering's waves swallowed it, and the naga had lately found it. Their leader Ruuzel is dead, and the ring is in Talen's hands."},{after=1035,articles={"Fallen Sky Lake (quest)","Bathran's Hair (Classic)","Orendil's Cure (Classic)","Elune's Tear (Classic)"},text="Relara, the young daughter of Pelturas Whitemoon of Astranaar, fell gravely ill with a sickness of the spirit. The herbalist Orendil Broadleaf brewed a cure from Bathran's Hair, and the stranger who gathered it found Forsaken lurking in the ruins of Bathran's Haunt. The cure only slowed the disease, but Elune's Tear, a stone from Iris Lake, broke her fever, and a moonstone taken from the Shadethicket Oracle at Fallen Sky Lake gave back her strength. Relara is saved."},{after=1017,articles={"Mage Summoner","Elemental Bracers (quest)"},text="Water elementals were defiling the lakes around Silverwind Refuge, and Sentinel Velene Starstrike used a divining scroll on their bracers to learn who had made them. Their creator was Sarilus Foulborne, a Forsaken mage who mocked the Sentinels from the top of Dreadmist Peak in the Barrens. He is dead, killed in the heart of Horde territory, and Velene has seen his smile wiped away for good."},{after=1012,articles={"Insane Druids (Classic)","Forsaken Diseases"},text="Kayneth Stillwind of Forest Song believed the Forsaken had come to Ashenvale to spread disease, and a bottle stolen from their camp by the Dor'danil Barrow Den proved him right. Testing it nearly killed him, but in his fever he saw a vision: the Dor'danil druids had been poisoned by the undead, and their spirits, torn from their bodies, had gone mad. Their three leaders have been destroyed, and the barrow's anguish has eased."},{after=1037,articles={"Velinde Starsong (quest)","The Howling Vale (quest)"},text="Wolf-men have overrun the ruined shrine of Mel'Thandris near Felwood, and its tome, whose leaves record the events of the shrine, holds clues to why they came. Sentinel Melyria Frostshadow inherited her post from the priestess Velinde Starsong, who seemed to have the demons of Felwood in hand until her efforts faltered and she disappeared. Melyria knows nothing more of her, and the trail now leads to the Sentinels' commanders in Darnassus."},{after=1032,articles={"Satyr Slaying! (Classic)","Vile Satyr! Dryads in Danger! (Classic)","The Branch of Cenarius (Classic)"},text="A band of dryads led by Anilia went into the satyr ruins of Xavian to take back the Branch of Cenarius, and did not return. Anilia was found dying, and with her last breath named its thief, Geltharis. The satyr is dead and the branch returned to the dryads, and Illiyana of Forest Song, grieving for her sister, has seen the satyrs who took Anilia pay for it."},{after=6482,articles={"Freedom to Ruul"},text="The Thistlefur furbolgs captured Ruul Snowhoof, a tauren druid, believing he was a bear, and were starving him in their village. Freed and led out of the Thistlefur camps, Ruul made it home to his sister Yama at Splintertree Post."},{after=6544,articles={"Torek's Assault"},text="The Warsong's war with the night elves is fought raid by raid. Torek and his raiders struck the Silverwing Outpost, heavily defended by Sentinels and warriors, to kill its leader, Duriel Moonfire. The attack succeeded, and Ertog Ragetusk at Splintertree Post, who rates the night elves as skinny but fierce foes, was pleased to hear it."},{after=247,articles={"The Hunt Completed","The Ashenvale Hunt (Orgrimmar)"},text="To bring glory to the Horde's new frontier, Senani Thunderheart of Splintertree Post called young hunters to a great hunt of Ashenvale. Its three quarry were the beasts Sharptalon, who had long preyed on the peons travelling from the lumber camps, Ursangous and Shadumbra. All three are dead, and songs of the hunt will be heard at the camps and mills."},{after=6621,articles={"King of the Foulweald (Classic)","Between a Rock and a Thistlefur"},text="From Zoram'gar, Karang Amakkar wants a clear path to Splintertree Post that bypasses Astranaar, and the furbolg villages stand in the way. The Thistlefur were thinned, and a banner planted on the Foulweald totem mound drew out their chief, Murgut, to defend his tribe's pride. Murgut is dead and his totem taken, a strong message to the Foulweald and a step toward the Horde claiming the whole forest."},{after=6641,articles={"Vorsha the Lasher (quest)"},text="The hydra Vorsha the Lasher had attacked Zoram'gar Outpost again and again, honoured by the naga with a brazier kept burning on an island offshore. Muglash guessed that putting out the brazier would draw her out in anger, and it did. Vorsha is dead, and the outpost's defenders rest easier."},{after=6571,articles={"Warsong Supplies (quest)","The Warsong Reports"},text="Warchief Thrall's orcs have pushed into Ashenvale to harvest its timber, with scouts and outriders watching the roads from Zoram'gar to the borders of Azshara. The Warsong Lumber Camp ran short of saw blades, axes, rope and oil; the furbolgs stole the rope and the satyrs seized the oil for their rituals. Locke Okarr has his supplies back, and the camp's shredders and saws can keep working."},{after=824,articles={"Je'neu of the Earthen Ring (quest)","The Befouled Element","Stonetalon Standstill"},text="Befouled water elementals rising from Mystral Lake had halted traffic between Ashenvale and Stonetalon. One of them left a glowing globe of tainted water, which Je'neu Sancrea of the Earthen Ring at Zoram'gar found deeply disturbing: powerful elementals draw their power from such globes, and to find one so corrupted is worse. The Earthen Ring will study it, hoping to end the corruption of the elements."}}},
[1441]={articles={"Thousand Needles (Classic)"},text="Thousand Needles is a vast, dry canyon in southern Kalimdor, filled with towering spires of sandstone carved by a river that ran there before the Sundering. Bridges link the tops of many of the needles, and the tauren of Freewind Post keep watch from one of them. The rival Grimtotem tauren hold Darkcloud Pinnacle, and the Galak centaur roam the canyon floor.\n\nTo the east the canyon opens onto the Shimmering Flats, the salt bed of a long-dried lake, where goblin and gnome engineers race their rocket cars at the Mirage Raceway. The Great Lift climbs from the canyon to the Barrens, and roads lead on to Feralas and Tanaris.",chapters={{after=4966,articles={"Protect Kanati Greycloud","Assassination Plot"},text="The Galak centaur have stepped up their raids across the canyon, and Kanati Greycloud's bold new hunting camp at Whitereach Post drew their hatred. A note taken from the centaur revealed a plot to kill him and drive out any tauren who might follow. Warned in time, Kanati stood his ground, and the Galak assassins who came for him were cut down."},{after=5064,articles={"Grimtotem Spying","Message to Freewind Post","Pacify the Centaur"},text="After the Galak slaughtered one of his supply caravans, Cliffwatcher Longhorn of Freewind Post sent his reprisal against their camp at Splithoof Crag. Meanwhile the Grimtotem tauren had grown strangely cold toward their kin, and three agents of unknown origin were seen visiting Darkcloud Pinnacle. Their secret notes, hidden in chests across the Pinnacle, held news so dire that Longhorn began at once to plan for whatever may come."},{after=5088,articles={"Arikara (quest)","Serpent Wild","Sacred Fire"},text="Arikara, a red wind serpent, is a tauren legend, a spirit of vengeance, and she has appeared in Thousand Needles. Magatha Grimtotem of Thunder Bluff enchanted Incendia agave into a powder that would summon her at the Grimtotem's sacred fire of life, and warned that Arikara might be stalking Cairne Bloodhoof himself. Arikara is dead, but Motega Firemane cannot understand why she would hunt Cairne, and only trusts that Magatha, the most powerful of shamans, could not be wrong."},{after=4904,articles={"Free at Last (Thousand Needles)"},text="Lakota Windsong turned down Arnak Grimtotem's offer of marriage, and that night he came back and carried her off to Darkcloud Pinnacle, killing her brother when he tried to stop it. A stranger led her out of the Grimtotem's stronghold and home to Thalia Amberhide at Freewind Post."},{after=1394,articles={"Final Passage","Test of Lore (6)"},text="Dorn Plainstalker, who keeps the Weathered Nook in Thousand Needles, sets tests of faith, endurance, strength and lore for those who would prove the balance of their body, mind and spirit. The tests of lore sent a seeker as far as the Undercity, to answer questions on the history of the undead. All have been passed, and Dorn has sensed the change within; he may test again in future, if that balance holds."},{after=1147,articles={"The Swarm Grows (3)","The Swarm Grows","The Swarm Grows (2)"},text="Korran at the Crossroads came to believe that the insects hatching in the Barrens were a far greater threat than anyone thought, and Belgrom Rockmaul in Orgrimmar agreed: they must be pushed back into the depths they crawled from. Moktar Krin's war party, already half dead from fighting the Explorers' League's dwarves at a dig site, needed help to face the silithid swarming near the Shimmering Flats. Their numbers have been beaten back, for now."},{after=1189,articles={"Safety First (2)","Goblin Sponsorship","Goblin Sponsorship (5)","The Eighteenth Pilot"},text="The goblin racing team at the Mirage Raceway is the pride of goblins everywhere, but the gnomes are gaining on them. A prototype fuel regulator from Gazlowe went missing, and the blueprints were tracked down so that the engineer Pozzik could build a better one himself. The team's pilot, Razzeric, the eighteenth to hold the job, also wanted his own seaforium booster, modified in Gadgetzan; one slip setting it off, he was warned, and the team will be looking for a nineteenth."},{after=1194,articles={"Rizzle's Schematics","Keeping Pace"},text="The gnome racers of the Brassbolts brothers have grown faster without any change to their designs, and the goblins suspected a new material. While Zamek made a diversion, Rizzle Brassbolts' plans were stolen from under his nose. They describe refining indurium ore, a heat-resistant metal mined at the dwarven excavation of Uldaman in the Badlands, and the goblins mean to get their own supply."},{after=1122,articles={"Report Back to Fizzlebub","Zanzil's Mixture and a Fool's Stout","Delivery to the Gnomes","The Rumormonger"},text="Kravel Koalbeard, a dwarf at the raceway, runs errands for both teams while quietly working his own angles: he pocketed a small piece of the gnomes' parts order and dug for dirt on the goblin pilot in Booty Bay. His real scheme was a Fool's Stout, brewed with Zanzil's Mixture by Crank Fizzlebub of Booty Bay. It worked: one pit crew drank it, Kravel bet on the next race, and Fizzlebub has his report of a successful test."}}},
[1442]={articles={"Stonetalon Mountains"},text="The Stonetalon Mountains rise west of the Barrens, between Ashenvale and Desolace. They were the harpies' ancestral home, though turmoil has thinned their numbers, and Stonetalon Peak, the tallest of them, has long been sacred to both the night elves and the tauren.\n\nThe tauren hold Sun Rock Retreat at the heart of the range, and the Darkspear trolls a village at Malaka'jin in the south. Windshear Crag has been ravaged by years of logging, its air thick with soot and its water fouled with oil, and beyond it the Charred Vale runs down to the sea, home to black dragons and elementals.",chapters={{after=6629,articles={"Avenge My Village","Kill Grundig Darkcloud"},text="The Grimtotem clan raided Makaba Flathoof's village on the southeastern edge of Stonetalon and killed nearly everyone there; Makaba fought until he could fight no more, and barely escaped with his life. Grundig Darkcloud, who led the attack, has since been slain at Grimtotem Post along with his brutes, but Makaba will never forget what the Grimtotem did."},{after=6401,articles={"Protect Kaya","Kaya's Alive"},text="Makaba Flathoof's daughter Kaya, believed lost in the attack on their village, was alive after all: a captive, until a stranger set her free and saw her safely to Camp Aparaje. Her aunt, Tammra Windfield of Sun Rock Retreat, has heard the good news."},{after=1062,articles={"Goblin Invaders"},text="The goblin-run Venture Company has moved into Stonetalon, felling its trees and burning great stretches of forest, and the spirits of the mountains are nearly blind with pain and rage. The Horde has struck back at the loggers in Windshear Crag, but it may take the wisdom of the tauren elders to calm the spirits."},{after=6481,articles={"Boulderslide Ravine (quest)","Earthen Arise"},text="The kobolds of Boulderslide Ravine were frantically mining resonite, a rare crystal that Mor'rogal of Sun Rock Retreat found to carry traces of earthen magic. Deep in their cave they had uncovered Goggeroc, an earthen, one of the beings the Titans made to shape the land, asleep in a resonite cask. Mor'rogal enchanted the crystals to break the cask open while the earthen was still weak from his long slumber, and Goggeroc was slain before he could threaten Stonetalon."},{after=1096,articles={"Super Reaper 6000 (quest)","Further Instructions","Gerenzo Wrenchwhistle (quest)"},text="The Venture Company's works in Stonetalon are run by a gnome, Gerenzo Wrenchwhistle, his rotting flesh grafted with metal and gears, who watches over them from high above Cragpool Lake. Ziz Fizziks, a goblin he threw out, now works for Sputtervalve of Ratchet, who wanted the plans for the company's enormous woodcutting machine, the Super Reaper 6000. The plans are in Sputtervalve's hands now, and Gerenzo is dead."},{after=6301,articles={"Cycle of Rebirth"},text="At Sun Rock Retreat, Tammra Windfield is working to heal what industrial greed and endless war have done to Stonetalon. She gathers Gaea seeds from the shores of Mirkfallon Lake and imbues them with the Earthmother's blessing, so that new life can take root even in the Charred Vale."},{after=6283,articles={"Harpies Threaten","Bloodfury Bloodline"},text="The Bloodfury harpies of the Charred Vale are the source of Kalimdor's growing numbers of harpies, preying on every living thing within their reach. Maggran Earthbinder of Sun Rock Retreat, for whom the tauren are the protectors of these lands, called for them to be culled, and their leader, Bloodfury Ripper, all but a queen to them, has been slain in the Vale's western hills. Without her, Maggran hopes, their numbers will dwindle."},{after=1088,articles={"Cenarius' Legacy","Ordanus (quest)"},text="Not everyone at Sun Rock Retreat loves the land. Braelyn Firehand, who longs to return to Jaedenar but whose master will not allow it until her work is done, hates the druids of Stonetalon Peak for stifling the flow of magic, and has turned Horde adventurers against the Sons and Daughters of Cenarius there. The druids spirited Keeper Ordanus, one of the forest god's eldest sons, away to Raynewood Retreat in Ashenvale, but Braelyn's magic found him, and his head is hers now. She knew him once, long ago, she says: he held himself above even the Highborne."}}},
[1443]={articles={"Desolace (Classic)","Valley of Bones"},text="Desolace is a grey, ash-covered wasteland in western Kalimdor, where the skulls of ancient kodo stare from the dust and dead trees jut from the ground. It is the homeland of the centaur, born from the depths of Maraudon in its western canyons. Legend says the first khans were the children of Zaetar, a son of Cenarius, and the earth elemental princess Theradras, and their clans, the Kolkar, Gelkis, Magram and Maraudine, war on one another as fiercely as on outsiders.\n\nThe Alliance holds Nijel's Point in the northern mountains and the Horde holds Shadowprey Village on the coast, with the tauren Ghost Walkers at Ghost Walker Post in the middle of the land. The orcs of the Burning Blade summon demons at Thunder Axe Fortress and Mannoroc Coven, and naga gather in the northwest around the Highborne ruins of Ethel Rethor. In WoW Forever, the haunted Valley of Bones on its southern edge belongs to Shen'dralas, the land beyond.",chapters={{after=1488,articles={"The Corrupter","The Corrupter (2)"},text="A flayed demon skin taken from the Burning Blade bore runes in a tongue akin to the demons' own, and a signature. Maurin Bonesplitter, the orc warlock at Ghost Walker Post, read it as the mark of a demon lord, Lord Azrethoc, who was reaching for control of the Burning Blade. With the scrying wards broken by a Hatefury satyr's scalp, Maurin found him, and Azrethoc and his warlock servant Jugkar Grim'rod are dead."},{after=1436,articles={"Alliance Relations (4)","Alliance Relations","Alliance Relations (3)","The Burning of Spirits"},text="Keldran, a student of magic in Orgrimmar, explained a quieter side of Thrall's rule: the Warchief wants peace with the Alliance, and the renegade orcs of the Burning Blade in Desolace give the humans every reason to fear the Horde. Maurin Bonesplitter led the Horde's campaign against them, using a burning gem to draw the corrupted spirits out of dying cultists. The fight goes well, and Keldran is proud that by keeping the Horde's name clean, no more needless war will be provoked."},{after=1440,articles={"Return to Vahlarriel","Vahlarriel's Search","Vahlarriel's Search (3)","Search for Tyranis"},text="Vahlarriel Demonslayer came to Desolace to find Tyranis and Dalinda Malem, the heirs of a Darnassian merchant house, whose wagon was found burnt and empty. The Burning Blade had taken them to Thunder Axe Fortress, meaning to sacrifice Dalinda to their demon masters, while Tyranis, always hungry for arcane power, tried to win the cultists over and join them. Dalinda walked out of the fortress under her own strength and is safe at Nijel's Point; her brother did not come back, and the shame of his choice is hers to carry home."},{after=6027,articles={"Book of the Ancients (quest)","Sceptre of Light (quest)"},text="Azore Aldamort, studying from his tower at Ethel Rethor, learned that the Burning Blade had taken the Sceptre of Light from the naga, an artifact said to be able to sink whole cities beneath the sea. The sceptre was seized back from a seer atop a Thunder Axe watchtower. Then, with the Gem of the Serpent placed on a statue's hand on Ranazjar Isle, the guardian of the naga's Book of the Ancients was summoned and defeated; the book's centuries-old secrets go to the high council."},{after=1366,articles={"Centaur Bounty (Horde)","Khan Dez'hepah (quest)","The Kolkar of Desolace"},text="The Kolkar centaur have spread from Desolace into the Barrens, and Regthar Deathgate sent agents to Ghost Walker Post to deal with them at the source. Felgur Twocuts reasons that the centaur clans are dangerous mostly because they might one day unite. Khan Dez'hepah of the Kolkar is dead, and centaur of every clan have been hunted since, until the centaur know the name of the banner their hunter serves: the Horde."},{after=1380,articles={"Khan Hratha (Gelkis)","Raid on the Kolkar","Stealing Supplies","Ongeku (quest)","Khan Jehn (quest)"},text="The Gelkis, led by the shaman Uthek the Wise, revere their centaur mother Theradras, and were willing to accept outsiders who fought their brother clan, the Magram. For them a stranger raided the Kolkar, stole the Magram's meat stores, brought a shard of draenethyst from the Swamp of Sorrows to strengthen Uthek's magic, and killed Khan Jehn of the Magram. Then, at the Maraudine war horn in the Valley of Spears, Khan Hratha of the Maraudine was drawn out and slain, and the piece of a key he carried is now Uthek's. The Gelkis believe they will rule the land."},{after=1381,articles={"Khan Hratha (Magram)","Assault on the Kolkar","Broken Tears (quest)","Gizmo for Warug","Khan Shaka (quest)"},text="The Magram mock their brothers the Gelkis for trusting in Theradras, and through their spokesman Warug they took on outsiders who proved themselves against the other clans. For them a stranger slaughtered Kolkar, smashed the crystal tears of Theradras hoarded in the Gelkis' cave, found Warug a new target dummy to break, and brought him the head of Khan Shaka of the Gelkis. Then, at the Maraudine war horn in the Valley of Spears, Khan Hratha of the Maraudine was drawn out and slain, and the piece of a key he carried is now Warug's. The Magram boast they are the strongest of the centaur."},{after=5581,articles={"Portals of the Legion (Classic)","Hand of Iruxos (quest)"},text="At Mannoroc Coven the Burning Blade opens portals for demons using the Hand of Iruxos, kept in a demon box at Thunder Axe Fortress. Taiga Wisemane of Shadowprey Village had the box broken open with a demon pick, and with the gruesome Hand in Horde keeping, six of the Coven's portals were banished despite their guardians. The tide of demons into Azeroth has been stemmed, for now."},{after=1052,articles={"Down the Scarlet Path (2)","Brother Anton (quest)","Down the Scarlet Path"},text="Brother Anton of the Scarlet Crusade crossed the sea to Desolace on hearing rumours of an undead infestation, and made his base at Nijel's Point. A stranger destroyed the undead ravagers of the Valley of Bones and earned his commendation, and the Crusade's welcome. The letter goes to Raleigh the Devout in Southshore, who will lead its bearer on to the Scarlet Monastery, though Raleigh seemed surprised Anton was still preaching the Crusade's cause."}}},
[1444]={articles={"Feralas (Classic)","Feralas","Feathermoon Stronghold","Camp Mojache","Gordunni"},text="Feralas is a lush, trackless rainforest in southwestern Kalimdor, sheltered by mountains between the wastes of Desolace, Thousand Needles and Un'Goro Crater. Its forests hide the overgrown ruins of the ancient night elf empire, the greatest of them the city now called Dire Maul, and most of those ruins belong to the bloodthirsty Gordunni ogres, whose spreading camps defile the land around them.\n\nBoth factions hold ground here. The Sentinels of General Shandris Feathermoon keep Feathermoon Stronghold on Sardor Isle off the western coast, with the little waystation of Thalanaar on the eastern border, while the tauren of Camp Mojache, above Wildwind Lake in the east, have long been all that stops the ogres taking the whole forest. Between them roam Woodpaw gnolls, Grimtotem tauren and Northspring harpies, Hatecrest naga crawl over the western isles, and in the north the Dream Bough is one of the four Great Trees that lead into the Emerald Dream.",chapters={{after=2980,articles={"The Ogres of Feralas (2)","The Ogres of Feralas (1)"},text="The Gordunni are the strongest military force in Feralas, and their numbers are still growing. Belgrom Rockmaul of Orgrimmar sent fighters to Rok Orhan at Camp Mojache to push them back before they threaten the Horde's hold on the forest: first at their outpost up the river north of the camp, then in the High Wilderness, where stronger ogres have taken over the broken structures of the old night elves and left the trees withered and the ground desecrated. The ogres have been pushed back, and Rok Orhan counts the one who did it a dependable ally."},{after=3002,articles={"The Gordunni Orb (Classic)","Dark Ceremony","The Gordunni Scroll"},text="A soiled scroll taken from the Gordunni showed a meeting of some kind: a dark ceremony conducted by the ogres' mage-lords. Rok Orhan, a warrior who knows her enemy but not its magic, had an orb taken from one of the mage-lords and sent it to the mage Uthel'nay in Orgrimmar's Darkbriar Lodge. He found it fascinating, very powerful and very dangerous, and the Horde hopes it will show why the Gordunni are here and how to bring them down."},{after=2976,articles={"A Grim Discovery (2)","A Grim Discovery (Classic)"},text="Krueg Skullsplitter came to Feralas on business for his masters in Orgrimmar, and wanted faerie dragon wings for his mate's new cloak. The hunter he sent for them came back with stranger news: tauren in the woods west of Camp Mojache, of the Grimtotem clan. Belgrom Rockmaul keeps a bounty on the Grimtotem, so a crate of their horns went to him in the Valley of Honor, and he was eager to hear how they had been found so far from home."},{after=2972,articles={"Doling Justice (2)","Doling Justice","Freedom for All Creatures"},text="The Grimtotem have been catching faerie dragons and penning them in their camp, for what purpose no one knows. Two night elves watching from the cliffs, Kindal Moonweaver and the priestess Jer'kai, were too few to fight their way in, until a stranger helped Kindal free the sprite darters from their cage, and then the three took the fight to the Grimtotem camps. Jer'kai's signet ring went to High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind in Darnassus, who was glad to hear that dragonkind and one of her own priestesses had been helped."},{after=7732,articles={"Zukk'ash Report","Zukk'ash Infestation (Classic)"},text="The Woodpaw gnolls' raids on Camp Mojache had grown fevered, and too well coordinated to be the gnolls' own doing. Hadoken Swiftstrider had their alphas struck down and their battle plans seized, and the plans pointed south, to an insect-like race in a place called the Writhing Deep that was driving the gnolls into a frenzy; its largest beast, Stinglasher, is dead. The report went to Zilzibin Drumlore in Orgrimmar, who named the creatures for what they are: silithid, an ancient threat the Horde keeps choosing to ignore, and far worse than anything the Alliance could muster."},{after=4267,articles={"Rise of the Silithid (Feralas)","A Hero's Welcome (Classic)","The Writhing Deep (quest)","The Woodpaw Gnolls"},text="Raschal, a courier who had served Feathermoon since General Feathermoon first led the expedition here, set out by rowboat for Thalanaar, meaning to cross southern Feralas and avoid the Horde camp, and vanished. His wrecked boat, a lost knife, the druid Quintis Jonespyre's visions and the Woodpaw camps led to his note: he had found insect creatures in the Writhing Deep and gone to see them for himself. He was found alive, sealed in a pod in their hive, and brought out, and his report went to the priestess Gracina Spiritmight in Darnassus. The Zukk'ash, she says, are silithid, mere tools of one of the greatest threats the Alliance and the Horde will ever face."},{after=2871,articles={"Delivering the Relic","Against Lord Shalzaru","The Ruins of Solarsal"},text="Hatecrest naga have lately filled the Ruins of Solarsal, on Sardor Isle south of Feathermoon, in alarming numbers. A strange gazebo at the heart of the ruins hinted they were after more than the stronghold, and when Latronicus Moonspear's scouts learned that the naga lord Shalzaru had dug up an odd relic in a cave on the Isle of Dread, he was slain for it. Latronicus's wife Vestia, a priestess and scholar, judged the relic neither native to the ruins nor of naga make, but something more alien, and it will take her time to learn what it is."},{after=2942,articles={"The Morrow Stone","The Stave of Equinex"},text="Troyas and Angelas Moonbreeze study the ancient ruins that cover Feralas, and in the rubble Troyas found a stave he was sure was more than it seemed. A colleague in Darnassus named it the Stave of Equinex, the key to the Equinex Monolith in the Ruins of Ravenwind. Charged with the essence of four flames still burning in those ruins, the stave opened the monolith, and the sparkling stone hidden inside is the Morrow Stone: what it does Troyas cannot yet say, only that something hidden away so carefully must matter greatly."}}},
[1445]={articles={"Dustwallow Marsh (Classic)"},text="Dustwallow Marsh is a vast, fetid swamp in central Kalimdor, sunk into a sundered valley during the Great Sundering, where crocolisks, raptors and spiders hunt beneath the trees. Black dragons nest in such numbers in the south that it is called the Wyrmbog.\n\nAfter the battle of Mount Hyjal, Jaina Proudmoore led her people here and founded Theramore on a rocky isle off the coast. When her father, Admiral Daelin Proudmoore, arrived with his fleet and attacked the Horde, he was defeated and killed in the marsh, yet Jaina and Thrall hold to an uneasy peace. The Stonemaul ogres, brought to Kalimdor by Rexxar, have opened Brackenwall Village to the Horde, and the Shady Rest Inn stood on the road to the Barrens.",chapters={{after=1267,articles={"The Missing Diplomat (17)","The Missing Diplomat","The Missing Diplomat (8)","The Missing Diplomat (11)","The Missing Diplomat (14)","The Missing Diplomat (16)"},text="Bishop DeLavey in Stormwind Keep asked, discreetly, for help with a matter of great importance. The trail ran through the thugs of Stormwind's Old Town and the taverns of Menethil Harbor to Theramore, and to Private Hendel, a soldier secretly allied with the Defias. With Jaina Proudmoore's soldiers at hand, Hendel was subdued and taken to Theramore for questioning, and the hunt for the missing King of Stormwind goes on. Jaina, who cannot thank her helpers openly, has promised to keep them told of what she learns."},{after=1220,articles={"Captain Vimes","The Orc Report"},text="Thrall and Jaina may keep the peace, but spies watch on both sides. Buried in the swamp lay an orc's severed hand, clutching a bone tube with a report written in orcish. Captain Garran Vimes read it: an orc spy had been watching Theramore closely, and well. The spy is dead, but Vimes has ordered tighter patrols around the city."},{after=1202,articles={"The Theramore Docks","Theramore Spies"},text="Despite Thrall's orders that Theramore be left alone, a secret war is fought in the marsh. Nazeer Bloodpike, Brackenwall's spymaster, had Theramore's infiltrators hunted down outside the village, and sent spies to steal documents from a ship at Theramore's docks. They were discovered and forced to throw the papers into the sea, but the footlocker has been recovered from beneath the docks, and its documents are in Nazeer's hands."},{after=7541,articles={"Service to the Horde","The Lost Report","The Troll Witchdoctor","Marg Speaks","Report to Zor"},text="Marg Nighteye, Nazeer's agent outside Theramore, never came back; only his last report did. The Darkspear witchdoctor Kin'weelay called up his angry spirit, and Marg told how he had stolen a jewelled pendant from a soldier camped on the beach, far too rich for any soldier, before a muckshell crab-man killed him. The pendant belonged to a noble of Stormwind, and Zor Lonetree in Orgrimmar, who knows Alliance politics, has set scholars to unravel whose it is."},{after=1320,articles={"The Black Shield (Alliance 3)","They Call Him Smiling Jim","The Black Shield (Alliance)"},text="\"Smiling Jim\", James Hyal, once ran the Shady Rest Inn at the edge of the marsh, too stubborn to give up his dream for the safety of Theramore's walls after the ogres took the roads. The inn was burned to the ground. On its crumbling chimney hung an iron shield, blackened by the fire, made by no smith on this side of the sea, and with a strap sized for someone very large, perhaps a tauren. It is little to go on, and Captain Vimes hopes other pieces of the mystery will fall into place."},{after=1273,articles={"Questioning Reethe","Lieutenant Paval Reethe (Horde)","The Black Shield (Horde)"},text="In the ashes of the Shady Rest Inn lay a badge of the Theramore guard bearing the name of Lieutenant Paval Reethe. Krog of Brackenwall knew him as a deserter hiding in the woods and stealing from the ogres' supplies, and Ogron went to find him. But when they questioned him, Theramore soldiers arrived to kill Reethe rather than bring him to justice, and he died of his wounds. Whoever burned the inn, the trail leads nowhere for now."},{after=1173,articles={"Challenge Overlord Mok'Morokk","Identifying the Brood","The Brood of Onyxia","The Brood of Onyxia (3)","Army of the Black Dragon"},text="The Stonemaul ogres fled their old village after black dragons burned it and killed Tharg's wife, and Overlord Mok'Morokk, made leader by Rexxar, settled them at Brackenwall rather than fight. The learned ogre Draz'Zilb traced the attackers to the brood of Onyxia, daughter of Deathwing, and wondered what greater purpose brought her to the marsh. Onyxia's eggs in the Wyrmbog were destroyed, and the cowardly Mok'Morokk was beaten and fled, so the Stonemaul can choose a new leader to reclaim their home."}}},
[1446]={articles={"Tanaris (Classic)","Tanaris","Gadgetzan","Wastewander"},text="Tanaris is an endless sea of sand in southeastern Kalimdor, south of Thousand Needles and east of Un'Goro Crater, blazing under a cloudless sky and swept by sandstorms. The goblins of the Steamwheedle Cartel have made it their home, with their capital Gadgetzan in the north and Steamwheedle Port on the coast. Gadgetzan is neutral ground: anyone with coin is welcome, and goblin bruisers deal with anyone who starts trouble.\n\nWater is the desert's true wealth. The Wastewander bandits have seized the wells of the Waterspring Field and the Noonshade Ruins, Dunemaul ogres and Sandfury trolls hold the other natural wells, and Southsea pirates raid from Lost Rigger Cove. The Sandfury rule from Zul'Farrak in the northwest, silithid burrow in the Noxious Lair and the Gaping Chasm, and in the eastern mountains lie the Caverns of Time, home of Nozdormu's bronze dragonflight, about which little is known.",chapters={{after=113,articles={"Insect Part Analysis (2)","The Scrimshank Redemption","Noxious Lair Investigation"},text="With the nomads holding the wells, the Gadgetzan Water Company went looking for water elsewhere, and found strange insects draining the pools of what is now the Noxious Lair. Its junior surveyor Scrimshank went missing among the same bugs in the Gaping Chasm, and only his equipment came back; Senior Surveyor Fizzledowser fears they are cunning enough to be under some third party's control. Alchemist Pestlezugg's analysis of their parts was so grave that Fizzledowser, who usually sells his information, handed out copies to anyone who could read: the threat is to Gadgetzan, to all of southern Kalimdor, perhaps to the whole world."},{after=1691,articles={"More Wastewander Justice","Wastewander Justice"},text="The Wastewander nomads have seized nearly every water well in the desert, and without them Gadgetzan's supply would soon run dry. Chief Engineer Bilgewhizzle of the Gadgetzan Water Company called for some goblin-style justice: first against the bandits and thieves east of the city, then against their rogues, assassins and shadow mages. The nomads have been thinned enough that the goblins can take the fight to them at last and start planning to win back their wells."},{after=2875,articles={"WANTED: Andre Firebeard"},text="The Southsea pirates hold a walled town on the coast south of Steamwheedle Port, and the vilest of them, Andre Firebeard, was wanted for crimes against Steamwheedle and Gadgetzan. The posted decree authorised lethal force, and allowed that his head might stand in for him at his hearing. Security Chief Bilgewhizzle has the head, so Andre will make his trial after all; the small print on the bounty contract, he assures everyone, is harmless."},{after=3520,articles={"Screecher Spirits"},text="Yeh'kinya, an old troll at Steamwheedle Port, sought the spirits of the vale screechers of Feralas, cousins of an old, old god. With their essence gathered, he told of a prophecy among the trolls: that the dead god Hakkar the Soulflayer will one day return. Some believe his coming will bring glory to trollkind, others the end of all things. Yeh'kinya does not believe the myth, but he knows Hakkar's essence holds great power, and that it must be kept from those who would abuse it."},{after=3444,articles={"The Stone Circle","The Sunken Temple (Alliance)","The Sunken Temple (Horde)"},text="The goblin scholar Marvon Rivetseeker left Ratchet long ago to study the troll ruins of Tanaris. He once entered the Sunken Temple, a place banished deep below the earth by great powers, and barely found his way out through the ooze on its floors, remembering only a great round chamber full of serpent statues. A stone bearing the same serpent symbol, shipped home to his workshop in Ratchet, has brought it all back to him."},{after=4507,articles={"Pawn Captures Queen","Bungle in the Jungle"},text="Pestlezugg found that the silithid behave like bees: kill the queen, and the hive falls into disarray. A lure brewed from Un'Goro soil and a Gorishi scent gland, used on the attunement crystal deep in the Gorishi hive of Un'Goro Crater, drew out the hive queen, and she is dead, her brain carried back to Gadgetzan for study. Pestlezugg calls it a real victory but doubts it is the end: the silithid may be controlled by some malign intelligence, and the win was much too clean."},{after=4508,articles={"Calm Before the Storm (Alliance)"},text="Pestlezugg's findings went to Gracina Spiritmight in Darnassus, who has friends among the Alliance's leaders, but she doubts they will listen. The Alliance sees no threat but the Horde, and even Darnassus cannot agree what the silithid are: Fandral Staghelm insists they are nothing like the ancient danger Gracina warns of. She holds his ignorance second only to his arrogance, and means to keep fighting the silithid anyway."},{after=4509,articles={"Calm Before the Storm (Horde)"},text="Pestlezugg's findings went to Zilzibin Drumlore in Orgrimmar, who doubts the Horde will listen. It sees no threat but the Alliance, and though Thrall himself might act if the facts were put before him, the Warchief is beset by conflicting advice from all sides. Zilzibin means to keep fighting the silithid anyway, with the few who have seen the threat for themselves."}}},
[1447]={articles={"Azshara (Classic)","Azshara"},text="Azshara is a shattered stretch of coast in northeastern Kalimdor, east of Ashenvale, south of Winterspring and north of Durotar, cloaked in an eternal autumn. Before the Great Sundering it was part of Zin-Azshari, the greatest city of the night elves; when the demons were driven out the land was torn apart and the sea rushed in, and the survivors named the ruin after the queen whose madness doomed it. Night elves agree that the region is cursed.\n\nThe broken towers of the Highborne still cling to its cliffs and lie among the coral below, haunted by restless spirits. Spitelash naga stalk the shores, satyrs of the Haldarr and Legashi hold the forests, and giants roam the south. The Alliance keeps a foothold at Talrendis Point and the Horde at Valormok, and Archmage Xylem studies alone in his tower.",chapters={{after=3141,articles={"Loramus (quest)","Loramus Thalipedes"},text="On an island off Azshara's southern shore lives Loramus Thalipedes, a night elf demon hunter guarded by felhounds, who tells his tale to those who would fight demons. In the age of war, Razelikh the Defiler was the most feared of Lord Kazzak's lieutenants, left to secure the Blasted Lands. There he bound the Shadow Council's sects and the Dreadmaul ogres to him with gifts of immortal soldiers, and their leaders Sevine, Allistarj and Grol learned too late that the price was their own eternal servitude. Each now holds a fragment of the amulet that summons the demon lord, and no ordinary weapon can harm them."},{after=3621,articles={"The Formation of Felbane","Azsharite (quest)","The Name of the Beast (3)","The Name of the Beast (2)","Breaking the Ward"},text="Only a weapon bearing a demon's true name can harm him and those he commands. Lord Arkkoroc, a lesser god in a temple beneath Azshara's waters, gave up the name, Rakh'likh, once the self-styled sea queen Hetaera, who preyed on his children, was slain, and he squeezed her three heads into a temper. With that and azsharite, a hard crystal left behind by the southern cliff giants that eat the stone, Loramus readied the weapons' materials and sent them to Galvan the Ancient of the Mithril Order in Stranglethorn Vale, who had not heard Loramus's name in many years."},{after=3507,articles={"Betrayed (4)","Betrayed (3)","Betrayed"},text="Belgrom Rockmaul of Orgrimmar trusted a blood elf, Magus Rimtori, who promised him great riches in Azshara if he lent her warriors for protection; she killed them all. Ag'tor Bloodfist at Valormok led the vengeance against her workers on the northern bluffs, and her own camp held a circle of arcane focusing crystals, one tied to her and the rest to something far more sinister. Destroying hers brought her out to fight, and her head went to Belgrom in a sack, so the spirits of dozens of slain warriors can rest."},{after=3517,articles={"Stealing Knowledge"},text="Jediga was sent to Azshara as a punishment by Belgrom Rockmaul, but she has made the most of it. Before she left she learned how many valuable things lie about in the ruins, and the Ruins of Eldarath held four tablets named after Highborne wizards of old. All four are hers now, in good condition, and she means to sell them to every party who might pay."},{after=5534,articles={"Kim'jael's \"Missing\" Equipment","Kim'jael Indeed!"},text="The goblin Kim'jael jumped at the job when Magus Rimtori's blood elves hired him to help find lost treasures in Azshara, until they turned on him, tried to kill him and kept his equipment. The name they gave him means something like \"little rat\". His compass, scope, stuffed chicken and Wizzlegoober are back from their camp, along with a rune Rimtori wanted, and Kim'jael means to find out what it is for."},{after=5535,articles={"Spiritual Unrest"},text="Loh'atu, a tauren on the Ashenvale border, swore to the Earthmother to help the night elves, and promised the Sentinels to release the Highborne spirits wherever she found them. The Highborne were the night elves' mightiest students of the arcane, and their hunger for magic did great harm to all of Azeroth; their ghosts at the Shadowsong Shrine crave it still, a hunger she calls a harbinger of the Burning Legion. Those spirits are at peace now, and the dangers their longing drew are lessened."}}},
[1448]={articles={"Felwood (Classic)","Felwood","Emerald Circle"},text="Felwood was once as close to paradise as the world has known: meadows and quiet streams tended by the night elves under Cenarius's protection. In the Third War the Burning Legion befouled it with the Skull of Gul'dan, and it has never recovered. Its meadows stay charred, its rivers boil with poison, and its ancients and beasts wander blindly, twisted by fel. It lies on the road from Ashenvale to Mount Hyjal, and most who cross it do so only out of need.\n\nThe night elves, shamed that they failed to protect it, have never stopped trying to heal it. The druids of the Emerald Circle, a branch of the Cenarion Circle, work from the Emerald Sanctuary in the south; the Alliance holds Talonbranch Glade and the Horde Bloodvenom Post. Against them stand the Shadow Council in the barrow dens of Jaedenar, the Jadefire satyrs and the maddened Deadwood furbolgs, while in the far north the Timbermaw, one of the last uncorrupted furbolg tribes, guard the tunnels to Winterspring and Moonglade.",chapters={{after=5156,articles={"Verifying the Corruption"},text="In Shatter Scar Vale, north along the road, infernals were called down during the last great war, and their craters still scar the land. Taronn Redfeather of the Emerald Sanctuary hoped the fetid water gathered in them had cleared, giving the forest at least one pure source to heal from. It has not, and entropic elementals still roam the vale, so Taronn will save what creatures he can and destroy those so corrupt they endanger themselves and every traveller."},{after=4101,articles={"Cleansing Felwood (Alliance)"},text="Arathandris Silversky of the Cenarion Circle knows ways to cleanse Felwood's plants, but would not trust that knowledge to a stranger who had not proven loyal to the Circle. The Warpwood elementals of the Irontree Caverns are beyond saving, and their blood amber may help her find a way to defeat the corruption. With it delivered, she trusts the bringer, and the work can turn to salves that preserve life: the time has come to cleanse Felwood."},{after=4102,articles={"Cleansing Felwood (Horde)"},text="Maybess Riverbreeze of the Cenarion Circle knows ways to cleanse Felwood's plants, but would not trust that knowledge to a stranger who had not proven loyal to the Circle. The Warpwood elementals of the Irontree Caverns are beyond saving, and their blood amber may help find a way to defeat the corruption. With it delivered, the work can turn to salves that preserve life: the time has come to cleanse Felwood."},{after=5242,articles={"A Final Blow","Dousing the Flames of Protection (Classic)","Collection of the Corrupt Water","Forces of Jaedenar (Classic)"},text="In Jaedenar the Shadow Council plots with the Burning Legion, behind braziers whose spells kept Shadow Hold hidden from the druids' scrying. Greta Mosshoof of the Emerald Circle had water from Jaedenar's corrupted moon well purified by the shaman Islen Waterseer in the Barrens, and with it the braziers were doused just long enough for her to see who led the cult. Shadow Lord Fel'dan, the mortal making pacts with the Legion, is dead with his two succubi, and Greta hopes the Council will now turn on itself, leaving it open to the druids."},{after=5385,articles={"The Remains of Trey Lightforge","Retribution of the Light","Rescue From Jaedenar","A Strange Red Key"},text="A strange red key taken from the Shadow Council opened a cell in Shadow Hold, where the night elf Arko'narin waited to be sacrificed. Her friend Trey Lightforge, a Knight of the Silver Hand, had been tortured to death there, and she would not leave without his sword, Lightforge; she carried it out and hurried on to Darnassus with her news. Her friend Jessir Moonbow asked one last thing, and it is done: the succubus Rakaiah, Trey's torturer, is dead, and his remains have been brought out for proper rites."},{after=4261,articles={"Ancient Spirit (quest)","Felbound Ancients","Flute of Xavaric (quest)","Further Corruption"},text="Eridan Bluewind of the Emerald Circle sent adventurers against the Jadefire satyrs, who spread corruption to every creature they touch: Xavathras was cultivating a corrupted moonwell at the Ruins of Constellas, its spoils meant for Xavaric to the north. Xavaric's flute was carved from one of the ancients that once protected the night elves, its spirit bound and tortured by fel. Purified with blessed water from Darnassus, it freed the spirit Arei, who was led out of the cursed forest to rest at last."},{after=4505,articles={"Well of Corruption"},text="Winna Hazzard at Bloodvenom Post is called cruel by some; she calls it a strong desire to see the Horde gain every advantage over the Alliance. Her newest interest is the moonwell at the Ruins of Constellas: once a night elf symbol of renewal, it has been corrupted by the Jadefire satyrs and now brings forth more of their kind. She has her sample of its water, meant for the kitten at her side, and means to find a way to turn the corruption against the Horde's enemies."},{after=4883,articles={"Guarding Secrets (2)","Guarding Secrets","Wild Guardians"},text="Trull Failbane at Bloodvenom Post heard that the wildkin of Winterspring guard the night elves' secrets, and sent hunters through their territory to find out what. A glowing blue-feathered necklace from one of the owlbeasts went to Nara Wildmane in Thunder Bluff, who knew its source. The wildkin were made by the goddess Elune to guard her most sacred places; they are confused and misguided now, but she urged a deeper look at what that means."},{after=8481,articles={"The Root of All Evil","Timbermaw Ally","The Brokering of Peace (Alliance)","The Brokering of Peace (Horde)"},text="The Timbermaw trust few outsiders, since so many kill furbolgs without asking friend or foe, but they welcome those who fight the corrupted Deadwood and Winterfall. The Winterfall's corruption had a root: a demon, Xandivious, dominating the tribe. Lured out at the mouth of High Chief Winterfall's cave and destroyed, its essence begins the long healing of the Winterfall and keeps Timbermaw Hold safe for years to come. The Timbermaw sent offerings of peace to King Magni Bronzebeard and to Thrall, who once tried to reach them."}}},
[1449]={articles={"Un'Goro Crater (Classic)","Un'Goro Crater"},text="Un'Goro Crater is a vast, lush basin in southern Kalimdor between Tanaris and Silithus, almost cut off from the rest of the world and ringed by deserts. Its jungle teems with exotic life, from peaceful earth elementals to dinosaurs, great apes and raging devilsaurs, and at its centre the volcano of Fire Plume Ridge smoulders, prowled by fire elementals. Silithid of the Gorishi hive burrow in the south.\n\nStrange, beautiful crystals dot the crater, and on its ridges stand ancient crystal pylons that defy explanation. The crater is ancient beyond living memory; scientists and explorers camp at Marshal's Refuge in the north to study its life, its history and its crystals.",chapters={{after=3881,articles={"Expedition Salvation","Williden's Journal"},text="Williden Marshal leads a non-aligned expedition troupe, and its greatest venture yet, into Un'Goro, has met with disaster after disaster. They underestimated the crater's savagery: their base camps in the southwest and northeast were overrun, and they fled to higher ground at Marshal's Refuge, leaving their supplies behind. Their food and research equipment are back, and with them, Williden thinks, the expedition can survive here for a while yet."},{after=4321,articles={"Making Sense of It","Crystal pylon"},text="Most visitors ignore the crystals scattered all over the crater, but J.D. Collie, a gnome at Marshal's Refuge, was sure they held power. She was just as sure the rumours of crystal pylons were true, and so they were: three of them, to the north, east and west on the crater's rim. From what was found at each she worked out how the crystals and pylons fit together and wrote it all into a manual, though who raised the pylons, and why, she cannot say."},{after=4245,articles={"Chasing A-Me 01 (3)","A-Me 01"},text="To get closer to the apes of Un'Goro, the inventor Karna Remtravel built a mechanical ape with scrying gems for eyes, A-Me 01. She made it as far as Fungal Rock, the apes' lair, before an alpha male rushed up to say hello and Karna lost contact. With a new mithril casing and a flip of her switch, A-Me 01 walked home, badly dented but nothing a little work won't fix."},{after=4292,articles={"The Bait for Lar'korwi","The Fare of Lar'korwi"},text="Torwa Pathfinder, a tauren hunter at the crater's entrance, hears the Earth Mother in Un'Goro, whose beasts seem nobler versions of those elsewhere. Long ago his brother took meat from a kill of the great beast Lar'korwi, and Lar'korwi stalked him for days and killed him as he fled the crater. Meat from Lar'korwi's latest kill, rubbed with Torwa's lure, drew the beast out at last, and it is dead; Torwa says only the greatest of his ancestors could have done the same."},{after=3962,articles={"It's Dangerous to Go Alone","Linken's Adventure","A Grave Situation","Meet at the Grave","The Videre Elixir","It's a Secret to Everybody (3)"},text="Linken washed up at Marshal's Refuge with a wrecked raft and no memory of who he was or why he had come. His trail ran far and wide: his sword was tempered in the hot springs of Winterspring and laid in a hidden grave outside Gadgetzan, found with the help of a spirit seen only through the Videre Elixir, and a totem was forged for him with the druid Eridan Bluewind's help and the water elemental Aquementas's power. With the totem in his hands, his memory came back. He had come to defeat Blazerunner, the fire elemental of Fire Plume Ridge who guarded the Golden Flame. Blazerunner is dead, and the Flame goes back to Linken's homeland, never again to be used for evil."}}},
[1450]={articles={"Moonglade"},text="Moonglade is a sacred vale in northern Kalimdor, sheltered by mountains between Felwood and Winterspring, and the home of the Cenarion Circle. Cenarius dwelt here in ancient times, and here Malfurion Stormrage founded the Circle; now Keeper Remulos is its guardian. Its old, strong forests are untouched by corruption, and the holy Lake Elune'ara lies at its heart.\n\nThe vale is a haven for druids of both factions. Night elves and tauren live side by side in the village of Nighthaven, and the Moonglade Wardens tolerate no violence between the races. The only road in runs through the tunnels of Timbermaw Hold, though druids can reach the glade by their own magic.",chapters={{after=1124,articles={"Wasteland","Rabine Saturna (Alliance)","Rabine Saturna (Horde)","The New Frontier (2)"},text="The Cenarion Circle called for able heroes to explore the wastelands far to the west of Tanaris and Un'Goro, and Keeper Remulos's majordomo, Rabine Saturna, welcomes all who answer, Alliance or Horde alike. Those who came were warned of growing tension between the night elves of Darnassus and the Circle in Moonglade. Rabine sent them on to Silithus, where few know that an ancient war was fought between the night elves and an alien power. That evil was sealed away, but the Circle suspects that something is stirring it again."},{after=1185,articles={"Under the Chitin Was...","Uncovering Past Secrets","Umber, Archivist"},text="The object found in the ruins of Southwind Village, sealed in silithid chitin, reached Umber, the Circle's brilliant and rather eccentric archivist in Nighthaven. Under the shell it was a book: a journal written in a very ancient dialect of the Darnassian tongue. Umber will need a long while to study it, but it is extremely valuable to the Circle, and Rabine Saturna is delighted."}}},
[1451]={articles={"Silithus (Classic)","Silithus","Cenarion Hold"},text="Silithus is a harsh desert in the far southwest of Kalimdor, reached only by a steep path out of the northwest of Un'Goro Crater. For thousands of years it has held the prison of the Qiraji, sealed behind the Scarab Wall in the ancient city of Ahn'Qiraj after a war with the night elves; their servants, the silithid, have since multiplied, and their great hives break up the desert's rock and sand.\n\nThe Cenarion Circle watches over the land from Cenarion Hold, one of the largest druid settlements in Kalimdor, and keeps a resting place for the fallen at Valor's Rest. The wreckage of night elf villages such as Southwind lies scattered across the wastes, dwarven explorers dig at Bronzebeard's Encampment, and the Twilight's Hammer has pitched its camps among the desert's strange crystals. The bronze dragonflight has returned to the sands for the first time in ten thousand years.",chapters={{after=8281,articles={"Stepping Up Security","Securing the Supply Lines"},text="The silithid have gone unchecked too long, and tremors near their hives across Azeroth warn that they are massing to spread. Cenarion Hold has called on the Alliance and the Horde alike to take the fight to them. Windcaller Proudhorn believes victory is certain so long as the hold stands and its supplies get through, and the desert worms were a worse threat to the caravans than the silithid themselves; with the dredge strikers and the crushers that came after them culled, the supply lines are safer."},{after=6844,articles={"Umber, Archivist","The Spirits of Southwind","Wasteland"},text="Southwind Village was once the night elves' base in Silithus, long since overrun by the hives. Layo Starstrike of Valor's Rest sent adventurers to free the tortured spirits of its druids and Sentinels, and their freeing proved the great swirling hive nearby had played its part in the village's end. Stirring up the hive in the village tower turned up something book-shaped, crusted in chitin, and Layo sent it to the Circle's archivist in Moonglade, hoping it would give them a window into the past."},{after=8287,articles={"A Terrible Purpose","The Twilight Lexicon","The Deserter","The Twilight Mystery"},text="A Twilight's Hammer camp northwest of Cenarion Hold is now overrun by elementals, and Geologist Larksbane pieced together a clay tablet from its wreckage. Its code was read by Hermit Ortell, a deserter who hides in the mountains south of Southwind while the cult hunts him, using the three chapters of the Twilight Lexicon taken from the cult's keepers. The decoded tablet went to Commander Mar'alith, and it shed enough light on why the Hammer is in Silithus that he began changing his battle plans."},{after=8321,articles={"Vyral the Vile (quest)","Twilight Geolords"},text="The Twilight's Hammer, an insane cult bent on destruction, performs rituals day and night in its camps, and Huum Wildmane suspects they are linked to the pulsing red crystals spread across the desert. Its geolords, the heads of the serpent, were struck down first, and then Vyral the Vile, the cult's local leader, in his cave at Twilight's Run in the northeast. More cultists may still be scurrying about, but it is a blow from which the Hammer's work in Silithus will not easily recover."},{after=8348,articles={"Signet of the Dukes","Dukes of the Council","Aurel Goldleaf (quest)"},text="The Hammer's work in Silithus has not slowed after all: an even darker power, the Abyssal Council, commands the cult through the arcane wind stones in its camps. The craftswoman Aurel Goldleaf made a replica of the medallion worn by the cult's middle ranks, so that a stranger could commune through a wind stone, pass for one of its leaders and call down a duke of the Council. One duke has been summoned and smashed, its signet carried back to Bor Wildmane, and the Council's lords still wait above them."},{after=8306,articles={"Into The Maw of Madness (Classic)","Dearest Natalia (Classic)"},text="Commander Mar'alith's wife Natalia had been acting strangely before she vanished. The dwarves at Bronzebeard's Encampment told why: she joined their expeditions into the hives, fell into a trance against a glyphed crystal in Hive'Regal, and weeks later returned, demanding they go back, chanting in a tongue none of them knew and attacking them with eyes aglow. What was found in Hive'Regal brought the commander a name he had never heard, and he cannot understand what C'Thun is, or how it could make his Natalia into such a monster."},{after=8308,articles={"Brann Bronzebeard's Lost Letter (quest)"},text="A letter turned up in the desert from the famous explorer Brann Bronzebeard, written while most of his crew lay dead. He had found word of a great battle between a titan and an Old God, and an ancient tunnel leading into Ahn'Qiraj, and he was taking it. His camp is asked to look after his monkey, and the postscript warns of a mad night elf who worships the Old God; the dwarves can only hope to see him again, if anyone ever finds a way into Ahn'Qiraj."},{after=8315,articles={"The Calling (quest)","Unraveling the Mystery","Glyph Chasing (Classic)"},text="Since the dwarves' first forays and Natalia's madness, the three hives have been spewing out silithid, and the glyphed crystals in each seemed to be the cause. Rubbings of the glyphs and the brains of silithid let the dwarves recreate them on a prism: a means for the Qiraji to send messages to the silithid, though one that hides its meaning. The prism showed an emissary of the Qiraji meeting three silithid at the bones of a dragon; the glyphs drawn in the sand at the Bones of Grakkarond summoned the hive lords and the emissary, and with the key it carried, Geologist Larksbane holds twenty thousand years of history in her hands."}}},
[1452]={articles={"Winterspring (Classic)","Winterspring","Everlook","Winterfall tribe"},text="Winterspring is a valley of near-perpetual winter high on the slopes of Mount Hyjal, in northeastern Kalimdor above Moonglade and Azshara. After the Great Sundering the night elves came here to start anew, and old ruins such as Kel'Theril still stand beside its frozen lake. The valley has long been fought over by malevolent blue dragons, whose great cave of Mazthoril lies in the south, and the fierce white frostsabers, old friends of the night elves.\n\nThe goblins of the Steamwheedle Cartel trade with every race and faction at Everlook, their only major outpost in northern Kalimdor, where miners dig for thorium and arcanite, and the night elves keep Starfall Village. The Winterfall furbolgs, once allies of the Timbermaw, have grown hostile to everyone, wildkin roam the hills, and in the south demons have taken Darkwhisper Gorge. The way in from Felwood runs through Timbermaw Hold.",chapters={{after=5087,articles={"Winterfall Runners (Classic)","Toxic Horrors","Falling to Corruption (Classic)","Winterfall Firewater (Classic)","Threat of the Winterfall (Classic)"},text="Donova Snowden came to Winterspring to study the Frostfire Hot Springs, whose waters have strange qualities, but the Winterfall furbolgs guard them fiercely. A flask they carried held a glowing green liquid, traced to the corrupted Deadwood furbolgs of Felwood, who gather toxins from the toxic horrors of the Irontree Woods. Mixed with the springs' water the toxin loses its poison but keeps its effect, which is why the Winterfall guard the springs. Their runners' latest shipment has been stolen, and Donova wonders whether drinking the stuff is making the furbolgs larger and more aggressive."},{after=5128,articles={"Words of the High Chief (Classic)","The Final Piece (Classic)","High Chief Winterfall (Classic)"},text="The Winterfall are not by nature an aggressive tribe, and Donova reasoned that their change came on their chief's orders. High Chief Winterfall is dead in his village in the east, and though the damage done cannot be undone, his tribe should get no worse. On him was a log in markings so crude no one could read them, and it has gone to Kelek Skykeeper of the Emerald Circle in Felwood, who knows the furbolgs better than most and has promised to send word once he can translate it."},{after=4842,articles={"Strange Sources","The New Springs"},text="The goblin Krakle in Un'Goro suspected that its hot springs are heated by the volcano nearby, and wondered at Winterspring's, which have no volcano near them at all. Donova Snowden had a darker answer: at the far south of the valley, where the air turns thick and muggy and the sky goes dark, lies Darkwhisper Gorge, and she believes it is the demons there whose presence is changing the land. She will keep watching the springs."},{after=4901,articles={"Guardians of the Altar","Find Ranshalla","Moontouched Wildkin"},text="Erelas Ambersky of Rut'theran Village found that wildkin feathers carry a magic that is part of the creatures' very essence. His colleague Ranshalla, studying the wildkin of Winterspring, found an altar of Elune atop a ridge, and with incantations copied from the ruins of Kel'Theril and five torches lit, it woke. The spirits of its priestesses told of a gem through which Elune once spoke to them, and of the wildkin the goddess gave them to guard all her sacred places. Without her guidance the wildkin have grown feral and confused, but wherever they gather, they still guard something of importance."},{after=5253,articles={"The Crystal of Zin-Malor","Remorseful Highborne (quest)","Troubled Spirits of Kel'Theril","The Ruins of Kel'Theril (Classic)"},text="The Highborne of Kel'Theril once stole a sacred artifact, the Crystal of Zin-Malor, hoping to siphon its power, but it shattered in a great explosion, driving its pieces under the ice of the lake and cursing them to haunt the place forever. With its fragments dug from the ice and the crystal recovered, one remorseful spirit found peace and begged that it be returned. Its temple lies in ruin, so it went to Arch Druid Fandral Staghelm in Darnassus, who will keep it safe from those who would abuse its power, and may the spirits that guarded it rest at last."}}},
[1453]={articles={"Stormwind City"},text="Stormwind City, on the northwest coast above Elwynn Forest, is the capital of the human kingdom of Stormwind and the heart of the Alliance in the Eastern Kingdoms. The orcs razed it in the First War, and the Stonemasons' Guild rebuilt it after the Second, from the Cathedral of Light to the Valley of Heroes, its gate lined with statues of the Alliance's champions.\n\nKing Varian Wrynn is away, and his young son Anduin sits on the throne in Stormwind Keep, with Highlord Bolvar Fordragon ruling as regent and the noblewoman Lady Katrana Prestor at his side. Mathias Shaw's spies, SI:7, keep watch over the city from Old Town.",chapters={{after=92752,articles={"Explosive Consultation (2)","A Dynamite Plan","Detonation at a Distance","Detonation at a Distance (2)"},text="Alba Fairmoon's errand found a willing helper in Sprite Jumpsprocket of the Dwarven District, who was delighted by the idea of industrial sabotage, but had no remote detonator for a blast safely set off from outside. SI:7 did, and its agent Jasper Fel already knew about the stranger's work in Moonbrook, since SI:7 keeps track of everything in the kingdom's lands. He handed over a prototype on one condition: that Alba never hear of SI:7's part. The bomb is ready."},{after=434,articles={"The Attack!","The Curious Visitor","Look to an Old Friend","Infiltrating the Castle","Lord Gregor Lescovar"},text="For years no one visited Bazil Thredd, a Defias leader held in the Stockade, until a quiet man calling himself Maelik began to come every week. Mathias Shaw traced him to Marzon the Silent Blade, and Marzon to Lord Gregor Lescovar, a noble of the court too well connected for the law to touch. So Elling Trias, a cheesemaker with a past, and his gnome Tyrion arranged a meeting in the castle garden and listened in as Marzon brought Lescovar word from VanCleef and the Defias. The traitor and his messenger are both dead."},{after=396,articles={"An Audience with the King","Brotherhood's End","The Head of the Beast"},text="Mathias Shaw was delighted with the work, and Baros Alexston, once of the Stonemasons, was grateful to be spared VanCleef's revenge; Shaw's advice was to tell everything except whose hand killed Lescovar, and never to mention Trias at all. Alexston's report on the Brotherhood went to the throne room, where Lady Katrana Prestor received it with the kingdom's thanks. The king, she said, could not present the reward himself: he was away on a diplomatic mission, and would not return for some time."},{after=1248,articles={"The Missing Diplomat (10)","The Missing Diplomat","The Missing Diplomat (2)","The Missing Diplomat (5)","The Missing Diplomat (9)"},text="Bishop DeLavey of Stormwind Keep confided a secret: a diplomat sent to meet Jaina Proudmoore in Theramore never arrived, and he suspected the Defias. His contacts led to Elling Trias, and from there to a Defias docket hidden at Addle's Stead in Duskwood, laying out a plan to seize the diplomat. A beating loosened the tongue of the thug Dashel Stonefist in Old Town: their first attempt failed, and their second relied on an insider from Menethil Harbor known only as Slim. Trias has sent the trail north to an old friend in Menethil."},{after=6187,articles={"Order Must Be Restored","The First and the Last","Honor the Dead (quest)","Flint Shadowmore (quest)"},text="Nathanos Marris was the first and last of the human ranger lords, a disciple of Sylvanas Windrunner believed killed defending Lordaeron five years ago. Mathias Shaw sent five agents to learn his fate, and the only one to return died in his sleep, gasping two words: \"Nathanos\" and \"Blightcaller\". The truth, uncovered in the Plaguelands, was that Nathanos lives on as the Blightcaller, champion of the Forsaken, and an Alliance army has struck him down in the Eastern Plaguelands. To the agent who sent them, it is one more great hero lost to his people."}}},
[1454]={articles={"Orgrimmar"},text="Orgrimmar, named for the legendary Orgrim Doomhammer, is the capital of the orcs and of the Horde, set in a system of valleys on Durotar's northern edge and carved partly into the mountains behind it. Warchief Thrall founded it after the battle of Mount Hyjal, with help from goblins, tauren, trolls and Rexxar, as a home for a Horde turned from conquest to survival.\n\nOrcs and Darkspear trolls live within its walls beside tauren and the newly welcomed Forsaken. Thrall rules from Grommash Hold, elder shamans teach the new generation, and warriors spar in the Valley of Honor, while warlocks keep to the Cleft of Shadow.",chapters={{after=97249,articles={"Favorite Food","Meal Appeal"},text="Thatog's son Migi spends his days playing with friends behind Grommash Hold and almost never picks up the lunch his parents arrange for him at Borstan's Firepit. Borstan had an idea: a sweetglazed hog belly sandwich with crispy crumbs on top, the boy's favourite with a twist. Migi loved it, and thinks his friends had better stop their game and come for lunch together."},{after=97326,articles={"Rocks to Rests"},text="The children's spot behind Grommash Hold is clear enough to set up the figures for their game, but they had nothing to sit on but the dirt. Thra asked a passing stranger to carry back smooth boulders, and now the children can shift their seats and tables wherever they want them."},{after=97275,articles={"Whuut's the Rush","Yelmak's Medley"},text="Whuut, the young apprentice at Yelmak's Alchemy and Potions, is a prodigy, but too proud to ask for help, and he was using up the rarest reagents in the shop without first mastering the basics. Yelmak brewed him a potion from nothing but the cattails and speargrass growing by the water in the Valley of Honor. Surprised that such common plants could be so potent, Whuut admits there may be a lot he has been missing."},{after=5727,articles={"Hidden Enemies (2)","Hidden Enemies"},text="Thrall will not tolerate traitors, but he knows that striking too soon would only let the corruption spread. The warlock Neeru Fireblade believes he has the Warchief's trust; in truth Thrall knows he leads the Burning Blade, and means to use him. Carrying a lieutenant's insignia taken in Skull Rock, a young adventurer passed as one of Neeru's own, and the warlock spoke freely. It is the first step toward destroying the Shadow Council."},{after=4511,articles={"Calm Before the Storm (Horde 2)","Calm Before the Storm (Horde)","March of the Silithid (Horde)"},text="Zilzibin Drumlore, on the upper Drag, believes the silithid live only to devour everything not of their own kind, and despairs that the Horde's leaders think only of the Alliance. He sought the insects' heart in Un'Goro Crater, and with the alchemist Pestlezugg of Gadgetzan the hives there were studied. Now he fears he was wrong about Un'Goro, and that a far stronger silithid presence waits somewhere else."}}},
[1455]={articles={"Ironforge"},text="Ironforge is the ancient capital of the dwarves, carved deep into Ironforge Mountain in Dun Morogh around the Great Forge, whose rivers of magma made its smiths masters of their craft. It is the seat of King Magni Bronzebeard and the Bronzebeard clan, and the Hall of Explorers houses the Explorers' League, who dig across the world for the dwarves' past.\n\nSince Gnomeregan was lost, its gnomes have lived as exiles in Ironforge's Tinker Town, beside the Deeprun Tram to Stormwind.",chapters={{after=700,articles={"A King's Tribute (3)","Sara Balloo's Plea (quest)","A King's Tribute","A King's Tribute (2)","Sully Balloo"},text="Sully Balloo, a soldier from Dun Modr, died when the Dark Irons struck the Thandol Span, and his widow Sara sent a plea to the king. Magni could not bring her husband back, and called such losses the price of war, but he commissioned a memorial to Sully from Grand Mason Marblesten, carved in fine granite from the hills of Alterac. It is finished, and Marblesten hopes it will remind those who come after of the soldiers who died for them."},{after=3461,articles={"Return to Tymor","Passing the Burden","Arcane Runes","Tymor"},text="Tymor, a dwarf of the Mystic Ward and a keen student of the arcane, offered to help the Explorers' League in its new work in Azshara, once the home of the night elves' sorcerers. Their mages left runes in public places setting out what they had learned of magic, and a stranger took rubbings of four of them among the Ruins of Eldarath, to be picked up by the goblin pilot Xiggs Fuselighter. Tymor is thrilled with them, and a little envious of anyone who has walked in Azshara."}}},
[1456]={articles={"Thunder Bluff"},text="Thunder Bluff is the tauren capital, built atop a series of windswept mesas in northern Mulgore, several hundred feet above the grasslands, linked by bridges of wood and rope and reached by the tauren's own elevators and by wind riders. For countless generations the tauren lived as nomads, harried by centaur, until the Horde helped Cairne Bloodhoof and his tribe drive the centaur from Mulgore.\n\nCairne built the city as a refuge where tauren of every tribe are welcome, and most have united under him, though a few tribes, such as the Grimtotem, disagree with the path he has chosen. Its rises hold tents, longhouses, totems and spirit lodges, and hunters, traders and artisans gather there.",chapters={{after=264,articles={"Until Death Do Us Part"},text="Clarice Foster, one of the Forsaken, still burns with anger at her husband Yuriv, who left her and their children for his crusade against the undead. He died for it, and she became the very thing he fought. She wanted nothing more to do with his pendant, and it was laid on his neglected, overgrown tomb at the Sepulcher in Silverpine Forest, where its gem grew dull."},{after=1086,articles={"The Flying Machine Airport","Forsaken Aid"},text="The goblins of the Venture Company are stripping the Stonetalon Mountains, and the shaman Magatha Grimtotem believes the land cannot heal until that disease is removed. She turned to the Forsaken, whose knowledge of disease could help and whose aid would build trust between the two peoples. Apothecary Zamah brewed a toxin, and a fogger was planted at the Venture Company's small sky port, in the hope it would reach their headquarters."},{after=1197,articles={"The Sacred Flame (3)","The Sacred Flame (quest)"},text="The Galak centaur of Thousand Needles guard an artifact from the time of their race's creation, held within a sacred flame in Splithoof Hold. Water stolen from a night elf moonwell in Ashenvale doused the flame just long enough to snatch the relic, the Cloven Hoof, from among the centaur. Rau Cliffrunner at Freewind Post senses a strange energy in it, and hopes it will reveal much about the centaur."},{after=1136,articles={"Frostmaw (quest)","Steelsnap (quest)","Melor Sends Word"},text="Melor Stonehoof of Hunter's Rise sets young hunters on the path of the hunter, which takes them across the Earth Mother's lands. First came the hyena Steelsnap, a tyrant of Thousand Needles who spread fear among gentler beasts, and then Frostmaw, an elusive yeti in the snows of Alterac, who could only be lured out by Melor's instructions. Both are dead, proof of the strength and cunning a hunter needs."},{after=1205,articles={"Deadmire (quest)"},text="Deadmire, an ancient crocolisk of Dustwallow Marsh, still moved with the strength of youth, but his aging body tormented the great spirit within it with constant pain. Melor Stonehoof asked for a hunt of mercy rather than of food or honour. Deadmire is dead, and his suffering over."}}},
[1457]={articles={"Darnassus"},text="Darnassus is the capital of the night elves, built high in the boughs of the World Tree Teldrassil, along the shores of a great lake spanned by graceful bridges. It is quiet and open to the sky, its lodges and groves set wide apart; the Temple of the Moon, seat of High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind and the Sisters of Elune, rises above them, beside the Cenarion Enclave of the druids and the Warrior's Terrace of the Sentinels.\n\nArch Druid Fandral Staghelm, who led the planting of Teldrassil after the Third War in the hope of winning back the night elves' immortality, guides the druids while Malfurion Stormrage is missing. Visitors reach the city through a portal from Rut'theran Village at the tree's roots.",chapters={{after=2520,articles={"Sathrah's Sacrifice (quest)","Tears of the Moon (Classic)","The Temple of the Moon (Classic)"},text="Lady Sathrah was a spider beloved of Elune, whose silvery dew, spun through the moonlight, had healing powers kept in the Temple of the Moon. Of late she had fallen into madness, and Priestess A'moora asked that her suffering be ended near Wellspring Lake. Her spinnerets were offered at the temple's fountain, whose waters cleanse corruption; her spirit appeared there, so that she may be reborn at peace."},{after=942,articles={"The Absent Minded Prospector (4)"},text="Chief Archaeologist Greywhisker of the Explorers' League recognised Remtravel's mysterious fossil: it closely resembles one an archaeologist named Flagongut once brought to the League's conference in Ironforge, believing it held a power that could be unlocked. Flagongut was last heard of in Menethil Harbor, and the fossil is on its way to him."},{after=1039,articles={"The Barrens Port","Velinde's Effects","Velinde Starsong (quest)"},text="Thyn'tel Bladeweaver of the Sentinels was reluctant to speak of the priestess Velinde Starsong, believing she left for reasons of her own and might return at any time. But Velinde's journal, kept in a chest in the Sentinels' bunkhouse, made her disappearance far more troubling. The Sentinels suspect she took ship from Ratchet, the only port in the Barrens, and the search for her now leads to the goblins' docks."},{after=3785,articles={"Morrowgrain Research (Alliance, 2)","Morrowgrain Research (Alliance, 1)","Assisting Arch Druid Staghelm"},text="Arch Druid Fandral Staghelm is leading a research project of his own for the Cenarion Circle. His druids plant Tharlendris seeds in soil from Un'Goro Crater, and among the potent herbs that sprout is morrowgrain, a mysterious plant about which little is known. Fandral means to unravel its secrets, and wants a steady supply brought to the Cenarion Enclave, though his druids ask that he not be disturbed."},{after=4510,articles={"Calm Before the Storm (Alliance 2)","Calm Before the Storm (Alliance)","March of the Silithid (Alliance)"},text="Gracina Spiritmight of the Temple of the Moon believes the silithid exist only to devour everything not of their own kind. She sought their heart in Un'Goro Crater, and with the help of the alchemist Pestlezugg in Gadgetzan the hives there were studied. But the more she learns, the more she fears that Un'Goro is not their source, and that a far stronger silithid presence lies elsewhere in Kalimdor."}}},
[1458]={articles={"Undercity","Royal Apothecary Society","Apothecarium"},text="The Undercity lies beneath the ruins of Lordaeron's capital, in the old crypts, dungeons and sewers below the royal palace. Arthas left undead behind to carve it out as the seat of his Scourge, but when the Lich King's grip faltered, Lady Sylvanas Windrunner led the freed undead to claim it for her Forsaken, and from here they wage war on the Scourge and the living who hunt them.\n\nSylvanas rules from the Royal Quarter with the dreadlord Varimathras at her side, while slime-green canals run between the city's quarters. Beneath the Royal Quarter lies the Apothecarium, where the Royal Apothecary Society brews poisons and plagues and works towards the new plague Sylvanas has ordered, to wipe out the Scourge. The rest of the Horde believes the apothecaries are searching for a cure.",chapters={{after=97891,articles={"Prompt Potion Runner"},text="Alessandro Luca's potions have a very short shelf life, and he cannot carry them across the slime canal himself, since his knees pop out of their sockets if he runs, one of undeath's many indignities. Six of his deliveries to Doctor Martin Felben in the Apothecarium had already gone inert along the way, but this one arrived in time."},{after=97985,articles={"Thundering Hearts"},text="The apothecary Calder Gray has been allowed, for the first time, to study the secrets of making abominations, and his notes called for a heart, a massive one. He chose that of Stormheart, a great thunder lizard of Thousand Needles. The abomination lived, briefly: the heart's electrical pulses seared away most of the construct's flesh, and Master Apothecary Faranell, not for the first time, ordered Calder to start over."},{after=2995,articles={"Lines of Communication (Horde)"},text="Oran Snakewrithe of the Undercity keeps an eye on the high elves of Highvale, whose ambassadors at the Quel'Danil Lodge were sharing what they knew of the Witherbark with other races. The Forsaken want no such friendships, so the lodge's records, reports and notes have been burned, and while the elves struggle to recover them, Oran means to cut their lines of communication further still."},{after=3570,articles={"Seeping Corruption (3)","Seeping Corruption (2)","Seeping Corruption (1)"},text="The tauren Thersa Windsong came to the Undercity because she had heard its alchemists were seeking a cure for their own condition: she had drunk from a tide pool in Azshara's Ruins of Eldarath and something was tearing her apart from inside, and no priest or shaman could help. Chemist Cuely was very accommodating, disturbingly so. His elixir turned her green and killed her almost at once, and Cuely, delighted at how quickly it took hold, set to work on the rest of the tide pool samples for the Dark Lady."},{after=4642,articles={"Melding of Influences","A Sample of Slime...","... and a Batch of Ooze"},text="The apothecaries study oozes, and Chemist Fuely wanted to know what the demonic corruption of Felwood would do to the untouched life of Un'Goro Crater. Corrupted slime from Felwood, sealed in a case, was released beside a pure primal ooze in the crater to see what the two would make of each other. Fuely was thrilled with the result, and means to study how the land changes such creatures."}}},
[1459]={articles={"Alterac Valley"},text="Alterac Valley lies hidden in the Alterac Mountains, home of the Frostwolf clan. Long ago the warlock Gul'dan exiled the Frostwolves here, under Thrall's father Durotan, for refusing to drink the blood of Mannoroth, and when Thrall united the orcs, the clan, led now by the old shaman Drek'Thar, chose to stay in the valley it had called home for so long.\n\nIts peace ended when the dwarves of the Stormpike Expedition arrived to mine the valley and search its caves for relics of their ancestral past, and King Magni has since laid claim to the land for Ironforge. Who struck first is still argued. Now General Vanndar Stormpike holds Dun Baldar in the north and Drek'Thar holds Frostwolf Keep in the south, and both call on heroes to drive the other out."},
[1460]={articles={"Warsong Gulch"},text="Warsong Gulch is a forested valley between southern Ashenvale and the northern Barrens, near where Grom Hellscream's orcs cut great swaths of forest during the Third War. Despite the misgivings of Thrall and the shaman, some orcs stayed on to keep felling trees to build the Horde: the Warsong Outriders.\n\nThe night elves, pushing to retake Ashenvale, see only the destruction of their ancestral home, and the Silverwing Sentinels have sworn not to rest until every orc is driven from the gulch. The two sides fight for each other's banners, for whoever holds the enemy's flag shows that their claim to the forest is the strongest."},
[1461]={articles={"Arathi Basin"},text="Arathi Basin is a mountain-rimmed valley in the Arathi Highlands, with some of the richest land in the Eastern Kingdoms: mines, farmland, timber and grazing. Its stables, blacksmith, mine, lumber mill and farm are prizes worth fighting for.\n\nThe League of Arathor, the remnant of the fallen kingdom of Stromgarde, would claim the basin for its allies in Stormwind. Against them stand the Defilers, Forsaken who want its resources to secure the Undercity and to cut the humans off from their dwarven allies to the south."},
[2521]={articles={"Zephras Isle","Skyborne"},text="Zephras Isle is a flying island in Skywall, the Elemental Plane of Air, and the home of the Skyborne. The air elementals who once lived there beside them vanished years ago, and since then the magic holding the island up has been fading.\n\nThe Skyborne's other islands, Anvilas and Siroccas, have fallen silent and are collapsing, and their refugees are fleeing to Zephras. But Zephras is starting to fall apart too.\n\nThe Skyborne, who call themselves the shen'dorei, descend from Highborne who left Eldre'Thalas some ten thousand years ago, and lived beside the wind spirits ever since. An elected Council of Elders governs them, led on Zephras by High Elder Talaanis Shadowsong. Two orders guide their people: the Windshapers, shaman who honour the elements, and the High Order, heirs of the Highborne magisters, who seek the arcane lore of their forebears.",chapters={{after=92465,articles={"Agitators","Return to Rorian"},text="Not every shen'dorei is willing to wait for the spirits to return. The Al'Aketh cult has been stirring the simple wind sprites by Thendal's standing stones into violence, and shen'dorei blood has now been shed in the grove. Rorian the Dayseeker had long watched the cult from afar; this, he says, is a clear escalation."},{after=92472,articles={"The Next Step (Skyborne)","Skyborne"},text="The Al'Aketh are zealots devoted to Al'Akir the Windlord, the elemental ruler of Skywall, a name the wind spirits rarely spoke. Their leaders are what remains of an older order of his priests, grown strong on the fear the spirits' disappearance left behind, and some of the refugees from the failing islands have joined them just to survive. Brutal and single-minded, they now threaten Shen'dar Village to the south."},{after=94411,articles={"Skyborne"},text="In the Shen'dar Highlands, the old quarrel between the two orders has turned ugly. Apprentices of the High Order keep meddling at the Windshapers' sacred standing stones, whatever they are told, and Elder Illaya Amberwind, loath as she is to see shen'dorei blood spilled, has had them driven off by force. To the Windshapers, the magic that binds the island is fragile, and one careless spell could bring it down."},{after=94413,articles={"Skyborne"},text="In the Shen'dar Highlands, the old quarrel between the two orders has turned ugly. As Zephras cracks, ley lines have begun breaking through the ground: raw arcane power to the High Order, scars to be healed to the Windshapers. When Windshaper novices set out to close them, Elder Rathiril Sunlance of the High Order had them killed."},{after=93927,articles={"Skyborne"},text="Shen'dar Village tolerated an Al'Aketh missionary, Sania Silverstream, to keep the peace, but that peace was always an illusion. Posing as a new recruit in the cult's stronghold of Falaath Village, the hero learned that High Priestess Lorthuna, the leader of the whole cult, had come in person to plan attacks on Shen'dar and its western watchtower. By the time help reached the tower, its peacekeepers lay dead, burned by storm-wielding priests who had brought a great cache of windstones to do something to the anchor pylon there. Why the cult wants the pylon, no one yet knows."},{after=93746,articles={"A Firm Response","Skyborne"},text="The crisis has strained the bond between the two orders. One of the few working constructs the wind spirits left in Valanaar was found stripped of its parts by Belathaan Brightwish, a High Order magister whose own mother was a Windshaper, and Grand Skyseer Ayessa Dawnsinger sent someone to frighten him off. On the road to the Shrine of Akir, Belathaan turned out to be meeting High Priestess Lorthuna, sent by the High Order's Supreme Magister, Elaadrin Evengale, to talk peace. Lorthuna had him killed instead, and his death is one more grievance between the orders."},{after=92703,articles={"Skyborne"},text="In the lowlands west of Valanaar, a gang of bandits led by Ferauu has been preying on Windfield Orchard. Alvarion Windfield, badly wounded, went to the city for fighters, while his wife Aamelia stayed to make her stand. With a stranger's help she faced Ferauu's thugs and won, aided by the old cyclone construct that has wandered her fields for decades: a relic of the wind spirits whose purpose no one remembers."},{after=94568,articles={"Skyborne","High Priestess Lorthuna (Forever)"},text="The cult struck the eastern watchtower too, led by Belguilos the Breaker, once a peacekeeper herself, and only just failed. A turncoat who offered to reveal the cult's plans was murdered before he could be brought in, but he left behind a damaged recall crystal: Lorthuna had ordered her forces to march. The anchor pylons do more than hold Zephras up; they also hide it from the eyes of Skywall. High Elder Shadowsong concluded that Lorthuna means to bring down the wards and let the Windlord in."},{after=94491,articles={"Skyborne"},text="Deep in Shadowgale Forest, not far from the ruins of fallen Ban'aethal, the secretive Nightclaw druids keep a conclave in a cave. The Al'Aketh fell on it without warning, led by Haalien, a druid of the circle and a cousin of Elegael Thornpaw, and many of the druids died. A missive found on Haalien's body showed that the cult had come for a pylon nearby, and that its orders came from Valanaar: someone in the city is feeding the cult. Lotheluum Starbreeze has taken the evidence to the High Elder."},{after=92646,articles={"High Priestess Lorthuna (Forever)","Skyborne"},text="The Peacekeepers, Windshapers and High Order stormed the Shrine of Akir together, under a truce both orders swore would be only temporary, and many died on both sides. Above the shrine, Ayessa and Elaadrin found Lorthuna, Ayessa's own sister, lighting a beacon: the wards had been down for days. Rohash of Al'Akir's Conclave of Wind answered it, promised that the Windlord's judgement would come, and knocked Lorthuna away into the endless sky for her presumption. When his elementals attacked, the Earthen Ring and the Kirin Tor came through a portal to help, and Muln Earthfury persuaded Rohash to withdraw; but Rohash left vowing to tell Al'Akir everything."},{after=93835,articles={"High Priestess Lorthuna (Forever)","Skyborne"},text="The Peacekeepers, High Order and Windshapers stormed the Shrine of Akir together, under a truce both orders swore would be only temporary, and many died on both sides. Above the shrine, Elaadrin and Ayessa found Lorthuna, Ayessa's own sister, lighting a beacon: the wards had been down for days. Rohash of Al'Akir's Conclave of Wind answered it, promised that the Windlord's judgement would come, and knocked Lorthuna away into the endless sky for her presumption. When his elementals attacked, the Kirin Tor and the Earthen Ring came through a portal Archmage Ansirem Runeweaver had opened, and Muln Earthfury persuaded Rohash to withdraw; but Rohash left vowing to tell Al'Akir everything."},{after=93090,articles={"What Comes Next (Horde)"},text="With the Al'Aketh defeated, the Windshapers have found new friends in the Earthen Ring and, through them, joined the Horde. With High Elder Shadowsong's blessing, their shaman are leaving Zephras to seek a lasting answer to the island's failing magic. Muln Earthfury has helped open a skycutter route to Mulgore, where the Earthen Ring make their home, and some of the Windshapers are already waiting on the other side."},{after=93089,articles={"What Comes Next (Alliance)","Skyborne"},text="With the Al'Aketh defeated, the High Order has found new friends in the Kirin Tor of Dalaran and, through them, joined the Alliance. With High Elder Shadowsong's blessing, its magisters are leaving Zephras to recover the lost arcane knowledge of their Highborne forebears on Azeroth, and with it an answer to the island's failing magic. Archmage Ansirem Runeweaver has helped open a skycutter route between Zephras and Dalaran, and after much testing, the High Order's vanguard is ready to depart."}}},
[2524]={articles={"Darkspear Islands (battleground)","Darkspear Islands"},text="The Darkspear Islands are a volcanic island chain in the South Seas, once the home of the Darkspear trolls after they fled the cruelty of the other Gurubashi tribes in Stranglethorn. During the Third War, Thrall's storm-scattered fleet landed here and found the trolls warring with Kul Tirans stranded from Admiral Proudmoore's fleet, and the Darkspear left with the Horde for Kalimdor.\n\nNow the islands are a battlefield again. The Darkspear Raiders and the Theramore Expeditionary Force fight fifteen against fifteen for the Abandoned Tower, the Camp, the Ruins and Shipwreck Cove."},
[2548]={articles={"Riverglades","Brotherhood of the Horse","A Wartorn Land and The Brotherhood","Powderfuse Port"},text="The Riverglades are green, contested river country in southeastern Khaz Modan, east of the Badlands and the Burning Steppes. They were once Stormwind's, until the First War: the Horde sacked the prosperous town of Sunnyglade, and its survivors founded Windstead by a hidden lake and, with the knights who rescued them, Meadowsbrook and the island fortress of Farholde. Stormwind judged the land lost and turned to defending its heartland.\n\nLate in the Second War, Twilight's Hammer ogres and Stormreaver orcs fleeing the Tomb of Sargeras came east and joined an ogre mound already here. The Brotherhood of the Horse came after them and has held Farholde ever since, and under Knight Commander Demar Brant it still fights to win back its homeland, with little patience for the \"cowards\" behind Stormwind's walls. Sunnyglade lies in ruins, held by the Twilight's Hammer around its last mage tower, and pirates squat in Windstead.\n\nThe Horde has its own camp at Rog'mar, and the Bolder'ok ogres have broken with the Hammer to side with it. On the eastern coast, the goblin Melbin Powderfuse claims to have built Powderfuse Port from a vacant lot, and his ships sail to Tanaris.",chapters={{after=94617,articles={"Coyote Huntin'","Wheeler's Grange"},text="At Wheeler's Grange, near Farholde Keep, Benjy Wheeler is trying to get a horse farm running again. A pack of coyotes, pushed off their fishing grounds on the shore by the murlocs, had taken to raiding his stables and eating his horses. Their dark-furred leader, Sootfur, is dead, and with him gone the pack should scatter; Benjy means to make a hat of the pelt."},{after=94861,articles={"Helping Hands"},text="The lumberjacks of Turner's Logging Camp work in dangerous country. When young Curly failed to come back for his lunch break, his long-suffering partner Hurley went after him with a stranger at his side, and they reached him just in time. Curly is hurt but alive, and Hurley hopes the knucklehead heals up and learns some sense."},{after=95951,articles={"Fetch 'n' Fletch"},text="At Rog'mar the orcs fight the humans with axes, but the troll headhunter Tan'jani teaches a quieter way: spears that strike before the enemy sees them coming. The wood for them came straight from the human logging camp to the west, carried off from its woodpiles and sharpened into twenty makeshift spears."},{after=94556,articles={"Jom-Jom's Escape","Riverglades"},text="The Bolder'ok ogres have left the Twilight's Hammer and thrown in with the Horde. One of them, Jom-Jom, went out looking for a friend and got into trouble too deep to escape on his own. A Horde escort brought him safely home to Clugfist at Bolder'ok, and the ogres now count that \"little one\" as a friend."},{after=95267,articles={"The Monster Surfaces"},text="Fresh wreckage keeps turning up in the waters around the murloc islands off Powderfuse Port, and Argo Brinewhistle, more often drunk than not, swore a sea monster was to blame. Lured to the surface with his big ball of bait, the beast lost its head to a stranger's blade. Argo means to mount it on his boat's stern, and the drinks are on him."}}},
[2652]={articles={"Shen'dralas","Valley of Bones","Mordent Evenshade"},text="Shen'dralas is contested country in the heart of Kalimdor, southeast of Desolace, between Feralas and Mulgore. It is reached on foot from Desolace through the Valley of Bones, a cursed crag where two colossal skeletons lie against the cliffs and most centaur clans will not go; outcast centaur necromancers raise the dead among the bones.\n\nThe Magram centaur hold the valley and the Magram Front beyond it, and the Bristleback quilboar have dug in at Bristleback Retreat. At Evenshade Overlook, north of Dire Maul, the Highborne archmage Mordent Evenshade keeps watch over the land his people named."},
}
